ADDART({id:"originoflife",t:"The Origin of Life",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌋",w:"Abiogenesis",r:["geotime","earthplanet","cell","dna","bacteria","astrobio"],
p:[["A great mystery","No one knows exactly how life began on Earth."],["Very early","Life appeared within about a billion years of Earth forming."],["Clues from chemistry","Experiments show life’s building blocks can form naturally."]],
l:"The origin of life is the question of how living things first arose from non-living chemicals on the early Earth, a process scientists call abiogenesis. Earth formed about 4.54 billion years ago. The oldest widely accepted fossils, layered mounds built by microbes called stromatolites, are about 3.5 billion years old, and chemical traces may be older still, so life appeared surprisingly early. Scientists do not yet know exactly how it happened, but experiments show that amino acids and other building blocks of life can form naturally. Leading ideas suggest life began in warm pools on land or around hot springs on the deep seafloor, perhaps starting with self-copying molecules of RNA.",
s:[["The young Earth","The early Earth was very different from today. There was no oxygen in the air, volcanoes were everywhere, and asteroids and comets struck often. Oceans formed early, perhaps by 4.4 billion years ago. Comets and meteorites may have delivered water and organic molecules, the carbon-based chemicals life is made of."],["The Miller–Urey experiment","In 1952 Stanley Miller, a student of Harold Urey, filled glass flasks with water and gases thought to resemble the early atmosphere and zapped them with sparks like lightning. Within days, amino acids, the building blocks of proteins, had formed. The experiment showed that life’s ingredients could arise naturally."],["The RNA world","Living cells need DNA to store instructions and proteins to do work, but each needs the other to be made. RNA can do both jobs: it stores information and can speed up reactions. Many scientists think early life used RNA alone, an idea called the RNA world, before DNA and proteins took over."],["Where did it start?","Some scientists favor hydrothermal vents on the deep seafloor, where hot, mineral-rich water provides energy and chemicals. Others favor shallow pools on land near volcanoes, where repeated drying and wetting could join small molecules into long chains. A few suggest life’s ingredients, or even life itself, came from space."]],
k:[["Age of Earth","About 4.54 billion years"],["Oldest accepted fossils","Stromatolites, about 3.5 billion years old"],["Scientific name","Abiogenesis"],["Famous experiment","Miller–Urey, 1952"],["Leading idea","An RNA world"]],
d:[["c. 4.54 billion years ago","Earth forms"],["c. 4.4 billion years ago","The first oceans may form"],["c. 3.5 billion years ago","Microbes build stromatolites"],["1952","The Miller–Urey experiment makes amino acids"],["2025","Scientists report amino acids and DNA bases in samples from asteroid Bennu"]],
s2:[["Our last universal ancestor","All living things share the same genetic code, which suggests they descend from one ancient population of cells, called LUCA, the last universal common ancestor. A 2024 study estimated that LUCA lived about 4.2 billion years ago and was already a fairly complex microbe."],["Life elsewhere?","If life began easily on Earth, it might have begun on other worlds too. Space missions search for signs of past or present life on Mars, Jupiter’s moon Europa and Saturn’s moon Enceladus. Samples from asteroids such as Bennu and Ryugu contain amino acids and other ingredients of life."]],
sp:["Living stromatolites","Stromatolites still form today in a few salty, sheltered places, such as Shark Bay in Western Australia. They are built slowly by mats of cyanobacteria that trap sand and minerals, layer by layer. Seeing them is like looking back more than 3 billion years."],
g:[["Abiogenesis","Life arising from non-living chemicals"],["Stromatolite","A layered rock mound built by microbes"],["RNA","A molecule related to DNA that can store information and speed up reactions"]],
f:["Life appeared within about a billion years of Earth forming.","The Miller–Urey experiment made amino acids from simple gases.","All living things share the same genetic code."],
fun:"Every living thing on Earth, from bacteria to blue whales, may be descended from a single population of microbes that lived more than 4 billion years ago."});

ADDART({id:"snowballearth",t:"Snowball Earth",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"❄️",w:"Snowball Earth",r:["iceage","glaciers","geotime","ediacaran","climate","volc"],
p:[["A frozen planet","At times, Earth may have frozen almost from pole to pole."],["Long ago","The biggest freezes happened between about 720 and 635 million years ago."],["Volcanoes to the rescue","Gases from volcanoes slowly warmed the planet again."]],
l:"Snowball Earth is the idea that Earth’s surface froze over completely, or almost completely, during a few extreme ice ages long before animals evolved. The best-known episodes came during the Cryogenian Period, which means cold birth: the Sturtian glaciation, from about 717 to 660 million years ago, and the Marinoan glaciation, which ended about 635 million years ago. Rocks left by glaciers have been found in places that were then near the equator, suggesting ice reached the tropics. Life survived, probably in pockets of open water, under thin ice or near hot springs. Soon after the ice melted, the first large, complex living things appeared.",
s:[["Clues in the rocks","Glaciers leave behind jumbled rocks called tillites and dropstones, boulders that fell from floating ice into soft seafloor mud. Rocks of this age contain such deposits from every continent, including places that magnetic studies show were near the equator. On top of them often lie thick layers of carbonate rock that formed in warm water."],["How it froze","Ice and snow reflect sunlight back to space. Once ice spreads far enough from the poles, the planet reflects so much sunlight that it cools further, and more ice forms. Climate models show that past a certain point, this runaway freeze could cover the whole globe. Changes in continents and falling carbon dioxide may have started it."],["How it thawed","With the oceans frozen, carbon dioxide from volcanoes could not dissolve in seawater or be removed by weathering rocks. Over millions of years it built up in the air until the greenhouse effect melted the ice, perhaps quickly. The hot, stormy aftermath may explain the carbonate rocks found above the glacial deposits."],["Snowball or slushball?","Some scientists think the oceans never froze completely and that a band of open water stayed near the equator, an idea nicknamed Slushball Earth. Fossils of algae that needed light and the survival of many kinds of life support some open water. The debate continues."]],
k:[["Main period","Cryogenian, about 720–635 million years ago"],["Major freezes","Sturtian and Marinoan glaciations"],["Earlier freeze","Huronian glaciation, about 2.4–2.1 billion years ago"],["Term coined","1992, by Joseph Kirschvink"],["What ended it","Carbon dioxide from volcanoes"]],
d:[["c. 2.4 billion years ago","The Huronian glaciation begins as oxygen rises"],["c. 717 Ma","The Sturtian glaciation begins"],["c. 635 Ma","The Marinoan glaciation ends"],["1992","Joseph Kirschvink coins the term Snowball Earth"],["1998","Paul Hoffman and colleagues publish evidence from Namibia"]],
s2:[["A trigger for animals?","Shortly after the last big freeze, the Ediacaran Period began, and the first large, complex organisms appeared. Some scientists think the melting of the ice released nutrients into the oceans and raised oxygen levels, helping life grow larger and more complex. Others think the timing may be a coincidence."],["Could it happen again?","Today’s Sun is brighter than it was 700 million years ago, and continents and the carbon cycle are arranged differently, so a full Snowball Earth is very unlikely in the near future. The idea, however, shows how powerful the feedback between ice, sunlight and carbon dioxide can be."]],
sp:["Ice on a tropical island","Some Snowball Earth glacial rocks are now found in hot places such as Namibia, Australia and the Middle East. At the time, continents were arranged differently, and some of these rocks formed near the equator, which is why scientists think ice reached the tropics."],
g:[["Glaciation","A time when ice sheets cover large areas"],["Dropstone","A rock dropped from floating ice into seafloor mud"],["Albedo","How much sunlight a surface reflects"]],
f:["Glaciers may once have reached the equator.","The Cryogenian Period’s name means cold birth.","Volcanic carbon dioxide ended the deep freeze."],
fun:"During Snowball Earth, the ice over the oceans may have been hundreds of meters thick, yet some algae survived, including relatives of the green algae from which land plants later evolved."});

ADDART({id:"pangaea",t:"Pangaea",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🗺️",w:"Pangaea",r:["continents","earthlayers","geotime","volc","dinosaurs","massext"],
p:[["One giant continent","Pangaea was a supercontinent that joined almost all of Earth’s land."],["Age of the first dinosaurs","It existed from about 335 to 175 million years ago."],["Still moving","The continents keep drifting today, a few centimeters a year."]],
l:"Pangaea was a supercontinent that brought together almost all of Earth’s land into a single huge landmass. It formed about 335 million years ago, as earlier continents collided, and began breaking apart about 200 million years ago, splitting into the continents we know today. Its name means all lands, and the vast ocean around it is called Panthalassa, all sea. Pangaea existed when early reptiles spread and the first dinosaurs appeared. The German scientist Alfred Wegener proposed in 1912 that the continents had once been joined and had drifted apart. His idea was doubted for decades, until the discovery of plate tectonics in the 1960s proved him right.",
s:[["Wegener’s clues","Wegener noticed that the coasts of South America and Africa fit together like puzzle pieces. Fossils of the same land animals and plants, such as the reptile Mesosaurus and the fern-like plant Glossopteris, are found on continents now separated by oceans. Matching rock layers and signs of ancient glaciers in today’s tropics added to his evidence."],["Why it was doubted","Wegener could not explain what force could move whole continents, so most geologists rejected his idea of continental drift. He died in 1930 on an expedition across the Greenland ice sheet, long before he was proved right."],["Plate tectonics","In the 1950s and 1960s, maps of the seafloor revealed long underwater mountain ranges where new crust forms and spreads apart. Earth’s outer shell is broken into plates that move slowly over hotter rock below. This theory, plate tectonics, explained how Pangaea formed and broke up."],["Breaking apart","Pangaea first split into a northern part, Laurasia, and a southern part, Gondwana, as the Atlantic Ocean began to open. Volcanic eruptions along the rifts poured out enormous lava flows and may have contributed to a mass extinction about 201 million years ago. Over time India raced north and crashed into Asia, raising the Himalayas."]],
k:[["Formed","About 335 million years ago"],["Began breaking up","About 200 million years ago"],["Name meaning","All lands"],["Surrounding ocean","Panthalassa"],["Proposed by","Alfred Wegener, 1912"]],
d:[["c. 335 Ma","Pangaea comes together"],["c. 252 Ma","The Great Dying strikes life on Pangaea"],["c. 200 Ma","Pangaea begins to break apart"],["1912","Alfred Wegener proposes continental drift"],["1960s","Plate tectonics is accepted"]],
s2:[["Life on a supercontinent","Pangaea’s interior was far from the sea, so much of it was hot and dry desert, with seasonal monsoon rains near the coasts. Because land was connected, animals could walk from what is now Antarctica to North America. This is why fossils of the same dinosaurs are found on many continents."],["The next supercontinent","Continents keep moving at about the speed fingernails grow. Supercontinents seem to form and break apart in cycles every few hundred million years. Scientists predict that in about 200 to 250 million years, the continents may join again in a new supercontinent, sometimes called Pangaea Ultima or Amasia."]],
sp:["The Atlantic is still growing","The Atlantic Ocean, which opened as Pangaea split, is still widening by about 2 to 5 centimeters a year along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, an underwater mountain chain where new seafloor forms. In Iceland, the ridge rises above the sea, and visitors can walk in a rift valley between the North American and Eurasian plates."],
g:[["Supercontinent","A landmass made of most or all of Earth’s continents"],["Continental drift","The slow movement of continents across Earth’s surface"],["Plate tectonics","The theory that Earth’s outer shell is broken into moving plates"]],
f:["Pangaea means all lands.","Mesosaurus fossils are found in both South America and Africa.","Continents move about as fast as fingernails grow."],
fun:"Because the land was all connected, a dinosaur could, in theory, have walked from present-day Brazil to present-day China without crossing an ocean."});

ADDART({id:"srivijaya",t:"Srivijaya",c:"History",i:"⛵",w:"Srivijaya",r:["indonesiacountry","malaysiacountry","buddh","trade","seaempires","majapahit"],
p:[["A sea empire","Srivijaya was a trading kingdom based on the island of Sumatra."],["Controlled the straits","It guarded the sea routes between India and China."],["Buddhist center","It was a famous place of Buddhist learning."]],
l:"Srivijaya was a powerful Buddhist kingdom based at Palembang on the island of Sumatra, in today’s Indonesia, that flourished from the 7th to about the 11th century. By controlling the Strait of Malacca and the Sunda Strait, the busy sea lanes between India and China, it grew rich from trade, taxing ships and selling forest products, spices and gold. Its influence spread across Sumatra, the Malay Peninsula and parts of Java and Borneo. Srivijaya was also a renowned center of Buddhist learning, visited by monks on their way to India. Its power declined after a raid by the Chola kingdom of southern India in 1025. Its very existence was largely forgotten until historians pieced it together in the 20th century.",
s:[["Masters of the straits","Ships sailing between India and China had to pass through narrow straits and wait for the monsoon winds to change. Srivijaya offered safe ports, supplies and protection from pirates, and it made ships stop and pay. Its navy and alliances with local sea peoples helped it control the routes."],["Monks and learning","The Chinese monk Yijing stopped at Srivijaya in 671 and later stayed for years, copying and translating Buddhist texts. He advised other Chinese monks to study Sanskrit there before going on to India. Srivijaya supported famous teachers and sent gifts to Buddhist centers in India."],["Inscriptions in Old Malay","Stone inscriptions found around Palembang, such as the Kedukan Bukit inscription of 682, are among the oldest known texts in the Malay language. They record a ruler’s journey, a victory and the founding of the kingdom, and some contain curses against anyone who betrayed the king."],["Decline","In 1025 the Chola king Rajendra I of southern India sent a fleet that raided Srivijaya’s ports and captured its king. Srivijaya survived but weakened, and its center may have moved to Jambi. Over the next centuries other powers, including Majapahit on Java, took over the trade routes."]],
k:[["Center","Palembang, Sumatra"],["Height","7th to 11th centuries"],["Religion","Buddhism"],["Wealth from","Controlling sea trade"],["Oldest inscription","Kedukan Bukit, 682"]],
d:[["671","The Chinese monk Yijing visits Srivijaya"],["682","The Kedukan Bukit inscription records the kingdom’s founding"],["1025","The Chola kingdom of southern India raids Srivijaya"],["1918","Historian George Cœdès identifies Srivijaya as a great kingdom"]],
s2:[["A forgotten empire","Srivijaya left few buildings, because much was built of wood, and its name was rarely remembered. Chinese records described a kingdom called Shilifoshi, and Arab writers spoke of the rich land of Zabaj. In 1918 the French historian George Cœdès connected these records with inscriptions in Sumatra and revealed a great forgotten empire."],["Legacy","Srivijaya spread the Malay language as a language of trade across the islands, which helped lay the foundation for modern Malay and Indonesian. Indonesians today remember Srivijaya, together with Majapahit, as an early example of a great kingdom uniting the archipelago."]],
sp:["A monk’s voyage","In 671 the Chinese monk Yijing sailed from Guangzhou and reached Srivijaya in less than 20 days. He found more than a thousand Buddhist monks there studying as in India. His writings are one of the main sources historians use to learn about the kingdom."],
g:[["Strait","A narrow sea passage between two larger seas"],["Monsoon","Seasonal winds that change direction"],["Inscription","Writing carved into stone or metal"]],
f:["Srivijaya controlled the Strait of Malacca.","Its inscriptions are among the oldest texts in Malay.","Its name was nearly forgotten until 1918."],
fun:"Some Srivijaya inscriptions include long curses, warning that anyone who disobeyed or betrayed the king would be destroyed by magic."});

ADDART({id:"majapahit",t:"Majapahit",c:"History",i:"🏯",w:"Majapahit",r:["indonesiacountry","srivijaya","hindu","seaempires","mongol","trade"],
p:[["Java’s great empire","Majapahit was a Hindu-Buddhist empire based in eastern Java."],["Islands united","At its height it claimed influence over much of today’s Indonesia."],["Golden age","It is remembered for its art, poetry and powerful leaders."]],
l:"Majapahit was a Hindu-Buddhist empire based in eastern Java, in today’s Indonesia, from 1293 to around 1527. It was one of the last and greatest of the Hindu-Buddhist kingdoms of maritime Southeast Asia. Under its famous prime minister Gajah Mada and King Hayam Wuruk in the 14th century, Majapahit grew rich from rice farming and trade and claimed influence over much of the Indonesian archipelago and beyond. Its capital, near today’s Trowulan, was a large city of brick temples, canals and markets. Majapahit declined in the 15th and 16th centuries as Muslim trading states rose along Java’s coast. Indonesians today remember it as a symbol of a united archipelago.",
s:[["Born from a Mongol invasion","In 1293 the Mongol ruler of China, Kublai Khan, sent a fleet to punish a Javanese king. A Javanese prince, Raden Wijaya, first allied with the Mongols to defeat his rival, then turned on them and drove them out. He founded Majapahit, named after a bitter maja fruit."],["Gajah Mada’s oath","Gajah Mada, who became prime minister in the 1330s, is said to have sworn the Palapa Oath: that he would not enjoy pleasures until he had united the islands under Majapahit. Through warfare and diplomacy, he expanded the empire’s power across the archipelago."],["The golden age","Under King Hayam Wuruk, who ruled from 1350 to 1389, Majapahit reached its height. A long poem written in 1365, the Nagarakretagama, describes his court, travels and the many places that paid tribute or traded with Majapahit, from Sumatra to New Guinea."],["Decline","After Hayam Wuruk’s death, disputes over the throne weakened Majapahit. Muslim port cities along Java’s north coast, such as Demak, grew powerful from trade. By the early 16th century Majapahit had faded away, and many Hindu nobles and artists moved to Bali, where Hindu culture survives today."]],
k:[["Center","Eastern Java, near Trowulan"],["Lasted","1293 to about 1527"],["Religions","Hinduism and Buddhism"],["Famous leaders","Gajah Mada, Hayam Wuruk"],["Key text","Nagarakretagama, 1365"]],
d:[["1293","Raden Wijaya founds Majapahit after defeating a Mongol force"],["c. 1336","Gajah Mada swears the Palapa Oath"],["1350","Hayam Wuruk becomes king"],["1365","The Nagarakretagama is written"],["c. 1527","Majapahit comes to an end"]],
s2:[["Unity in diversity","Indonesia’s national motto, Bhinneka Tunggal Ika, meaning unity in diversity, comes from a Majapahit-era poem, the Kakawin Sutasoma, by the poet Mpu Tantular. It originally described the unity of Hindu and Buddhist beliefs. The red and white of Indonesia’s flag are also linked to Majapahit’s colors."],["Art and brick","Majapahit artists made red brick temples, gateways and fine terracotta figures. Archaeologists at Trowulan have found the remains of canals, water tanks, houses and Chinese coins and pottery, showing the city’s wealth and trade connections."]],
sp:["A poem that survived","The only known copy of the Nagarakretagama, written on palm leaves, was found in 1894 on the island of Lombok, when Dutch colonial forces attacked a royal palace there and took it to the Netherlands. It was returned to Indonesia in 1973 and is now listed in UNESCO’s Memory of the World register."],
g:[["Tribute","Gifts paid by a smaller state to a stronger one"],["Archipelago","A large group of islands"],["Terracotta","Baked clay used for pottery and figures"]],
f:["Majapahit was founded after driving out a Mongol force.","Indonesia’s motto comes from a Majapahit-era poem.","Many Majapahit nobles moved to Bali."],
fun:"Majapahit is named after the maja fruit, which is bitter, from Javanese words meaning bitter maja."});

ADDART({id:"mauryaempire",t:"The Maurya Empire",c:"History",i:"🦁",w:"Maurya Empire",r:["ashoka","indiacountry","alexander","buddh","hindu","persia"],
p:[["India’s first great empire","The Maurya Empire united most of South Asia for the first time."],["Founded by Chandragupta","It began around 321 BCE."],["Ashoka’s empire","Its most famous ruler, Ashoka, spread Buddhism."]],
l:"The Maurya Empire was the first empire to unite most of the Indian subcontinent, lasting from about 321 to 185 BCE. It was founded by Chandragupta Maurya, who, with the help of his advisor Chanakya, overthrew the Nanda kingdom and expanded his rule soon after Alexander the Great’s armies left the region. At its height, under Chandragupta’s grandson Ashoka, the empire stretched from today’s Afghanistan to Bangladesh and far into southern India. Its capital, Pataliputra, near modern Patna, was one of the largest cities in the world. The Mauryas built roads, collected taxes and kept a large army, and Ashoka’s inscriptions spread messages of tolerance and nonviolence.",
s:[["Chandragupta and Chanakya","Chandragupta Maurya came to power around 321 BCE. Tradition says he was guided by Chanakya, a shrewd teacher also called Kautilya, who is credited with the Arthashastra, an ancient book about government, economics and war. Chandragupta defeated the Greek general Seleucus, who gave him lands in the northwest in exchange for 500 war elephants."],["A great capital","The Greek ambassador Megasthenes lived at Pataliputra and described it as a vast city surrounded by a wooden wall with hundreds of towers and gates. He wrote about officials who managed markets, roads, foreigners and births and deaths, showing how organized the empire was."],["Ashoka’s change of heart","Ashoka conquered the kingdom of Kalinga around 261 BCE, but the bloodshed horrified him. He turned to Buddhism and ruled by dhamma, a code of moral behavior. He carved edicts on rocks and pillars across the empire urging kindness, respect for all religions and care for people and animals."],["The end","After Ashoka’s death around 232 BCE, the empire weakened and broke apart. In 185 BCE the last Maurya king, Brihadratha, was killed by his general Pushyamitra Shunga, who founded a new dynasty."]],
k:[["Lasted","About 321 to 185 BCE"],["Founder","Chandragupta Maurya"],["Capital","Pataliputra (near Patna)"],["Most famous ruler","Ashoka"],["Famous book","Arthashastra"]],
d:[["c. 326 BCE","Alexander the Great reaches the Indus region"],["c. 321 BCE","Chandragupta Maurya founds the empire"],["c. 305 BCE","Chandragupta makes peace with Seleucus"],["c. 268 BCE","Ashoka becomes emperor"],["c. 261 BCE","The Kalinga War"],["185 BCE","The last Maurya king is killed"]],
s2:[["Symbols of modern India","The Lion Capital of Ashoka, a sculpture of four lions that once topped a pillar at Sarnath, is India’s national emblem. The 24-spoked wheel from Ashoka’s pillars, the Ashoka Chakra, appears at the center of India’s flag."],["Spreading Buddhism","Ashoka sent Buddhist missionaries far beyond his empire. Tradition says his son Mahinda and daughter Sanghamitta took Buddhism to Sri Lanka. From South Asia, Buddhism later spread across Southeast and East Asia."]],
sp:["Reading the edicts","For centuries no one could read Ashoka’s inscriptions, written in an ancient script called Brahmi. In 1837 the British scholar James Prinsep deciphered it, revealing that a king called Devanampiya Piyadasi, Beloved of the Gods, was Ashoka. Brahmi is the ancestor of many scripts used in South and Southeast Asia, including Devanagari, Bengali, Tamil, Thai and Tibetan."],
g:[["Edict","An official public order or message"],["Dhamma","Ashoka’s code of moral behavior"],["Dynasty","A series of rulers from the same family"]],
f:["The Maurya Empire was the first to unite most of South Asia.","India’s national emblem comes from Ashoka’s lion pillar.","Chandragupta traded land for 500 war elephants."],
fun:"Ashoka’s edicts include orders to plant shade trees and dig wells along roads, and to provide medical care for both people and animals."});

ADDART({id:"recalgorithms",t:"Recommendation Algorithms",c:"Science and Technology",i:"🎯",w:"Recommender system",r:["socialmedia","youtube","screentime","misinformation","influencers","digitalfootprint"],
p:[["Why you see what you see","Apps use algorithms to choose videos and posts for you."],["Learning from you","They learn from what you watch, like, skip and share."],["Helpful and tricky","They can show you great things but can also keep you scrolling."]],
l:"A recommendation algorithm is a computer program that decides which videos, posts, songs, products or games to show each person. Apps such as YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Spotify and online shops use them to fill your feed. The algorithm studies what you watch, like, share, comment on, search for and even how long you pause on something, then predicts what you are likely to enjoy next. Recommendations help people discover things they love, but they are also designed to keep people using an app for as long as possible. Understanding how they work helps you stay in control of your time and of what you see.",
s:[["How a feed is made","When you open an app, the algorithm picks from millions of possible posts. It scores each one by how likely you are to watch, like or share it, using signals from your past behavior and from people with similar tastes. The highest-scoring posts appear first. Small actions, such as rewatching a video, quickly change what you see."],["Rabbit holes","Because algorithms show more of what holds your attention, you can fall into a rabbit hole, watching video after video on one topic. Sometimes recommendations drift toward more extreme or upsetting content, because it grabs attention. Researchers and governments study how to make feeds safer, especially for young people."],["Filter bubbles","If an algorithm only shows you things you already agree with or like, you may end up in a filter bubble, seeing a narrow slice of the world. This can make it harder to understand other points of view and easier for false information to spread. Following a variety of sources helps."],["Taking control","Most apps let you tell the algorithm what you do not want, using buttons such as Not interested, or by muting words and accounts. You can clear your watch history, turn off autoplay and set time limits. Searching for topics you care about also teaches the feed what you want to see."]],
k:[["What it does","Chooses what to show each user"],["Learns from","Watching, liking, sharing, pausing"],["Used by","Video apps, social media, music and shopping sites"],["Main goal for companies","Keep users engaged"],["Risk","Rabbit holes and filter bubbles"]],
d:[["1992","An early recommender system, Tapestry, filters email at Xerox PARC"],["2006","Netflix offers a $1 million prize for better movie recommendations"],["2011","Eli Pariser warns of filter bubbles"],["2016","TikTok, built around its For You feed, launches as Douyin in China"],["2023","EU rules start requiring very large platforms to offer a feed not based on profiling"]],
s2:[["Rules and laws","Governments are starting to regulate recommendation feeds. The European Union’s Digital Services Act requires the largest platforms to explain how their feeds work and to offer a version not based on personal profiling. Australia banned social media accounts for children under 16 from December 2025, and platforms removed about 4.7 million accounts in the first month."],["Not just social media","Recommendation algorithms also suggest products on shopping sites, routes in map apps, news stories, games and even people to follow or date. Streaming services say most of what people watch comes from recommendations rather than searches."]],
sp:["Why short videos learn fast","Short-video apps can learn a new user’s tastes within minutes. Each video lasts only seconds, so the app quickly collects many signals: which videos are watched to the end, replayed or swiped away. This is why such feeds can feel personal so quickly, and why they can be hard to put down."],
g:[["Algorithm","A set of step-by-step instructions a computer follows"],["Feed","A stream of posts or videos chosen for a user"],["Filter bubble","A situation where you mostly see views like your own"]],
f:["Algorithms notice even how long you pause on a post.","Netflix once offered $1 million to improve its recommendations.","Many apps have a Not interested button to train your feed."],
fun:"In 2009, a team that improved Netflix’s movie predictions by just over 10 percent won the company’s $1 million prize."});

ADDART({id:"copyrightonline",t:"Copyright and Sharing Online",c:"Science and Technology",i:"©️",w:"Copyright",r:["netiquette","digitalfootprint","youtube","memes","wikipedia","deepfakes"],
p:[["Who owns it?","Copyright protects the creators of songs, pictures, videos and writing."],["Ask or check first","You usually need permission to use someone else’s work."],["Free to share","Some works are in the public domain or use open licenses."]],
l:"Copyright is a kind of legal protection that gives creators control over how their original work is copied and shared. It covers books, songs, photos, drawings, films, video games, computer code and online posts. In most countries copyright begins automatically the moment a work is created, without needing to register it. Online, it is very easy to copy and repost things, but that does not make it legal or fair. Using someone’s work without permission can get videos taken down or accounts blocked. At the same time, the law allows some limited uses, such as quoting for school or review, and many creators share their work freely using open licenses.",
s:[["What copyright protects","Copyright protects the way an idea is expressed, not the idea itself. A story about a wizard school is an idea anyone can use, but copying another author’s actual sentences is not allowed. Names, short phrases and facts usually are not protected by copyright, though logos and brand names may be protected by trademark law."],["How long it lasts","Copyright lasts a long time: in many countries, for the creator’s life plus 50 or 70 years. After that, a work enters the public domain, and anyone can use it freely. Most music and writing from before 1900 is in the public domain."],["Fair use","Copyright law allows some uses without permission, called fair use or fair dealing. Quoting a short passage in a school report, using a clip to review a film, or making a parody may be allowed. Whether a use is fair depends on things like how much is used, why, and whether it harms the creator’s ability to earn money."],["Open licenses","Many creators want others to use their work. Creative Commons licenses, created in 2002, let creators say, for example, that anyone may share their work if they give credit. Wikipedia’s articles and many photos on Wikimedia Commons use such licenses, which is why they can be reused with proper credit."]],
k:[["Protects","Original creative works"],["Starts","Automatically when a work is created"],["Typical term","Creator’s life plus 50–70 years"],["Key treaty","Berne Convention, 1886"],["Open licenses","Creative Commons, from 2002"]],
d:[["1710","Britain’s Statute of Anne creates the first modern copyright law"],["1886","The Berne Convention sets international copyright rules"],["1996","The WIPO Copyright Treaty updates copyright for the digital age"],["2001","Creative Commons is founded"],["2002","The first Creative Commons licenses are released"]],
s2:[["Giving credit","Giving credit, saying who made something, is good manners and is required by many open licenses. But credit alone does not make it legal to use copyrighted work without permission. When in doubt, use your own photos and music, ask the creator, or choose works marked as free to use."],["Copyright and AI","Artificial intelligence tools that create pictures, text and music are trained on huge amounts of existing work. Courts and governments are deciding whether this training is fair and who owns what AI creates. Many countries say copyright protects only works made by humans."]],
sp:["The monkey selfie","In 2011 a crested macaque in Indonesia picked up a photographer’s camera and took photos of itself. The photos spread online, and a long argument followed over who owned them. The US Copyright Office stated that works created by animals cannot be copyrighted."],
g:[["Copyright","The legal right to control copying of an original work"],["Public domain","Works no longer protected by copyright, free for all to use"],["License","Permission to use something under certain conditions"]],
f:["Copyright starts automatically when you create something.","Ideas are not protected by copyright, only how they are expressed.","Creative Commons licenses began in 2002."],
fun:"Your own drawings, photos, stories and videos are protected by copyright the moment you make them, even if you are a child."});

ADDART({id:"onlinecommunities",t:"Online Communities",c:"Science and Technology",i:"💬",w:"Online community",r:["netiquette","onlinesafety","cyberbullying","onlinegaming","socialmedia","wikipedia"],
p:[["Groups on the internet","Online communities are groups of people who meet and talk online."],["Shared interests","They form around games, hobbies, school, fandoms and causes."],["Rules matter","Good communities have clear rules and moderators."]],
l:"An online community is a group of people who regularly gather and communicate on the internet around a shared interest, place or purpose. They include discussion forums, group chats, gaming servers, fan groups, open-source software projects and wikis. Online communities let people find friends who share their interests, ask for help, learn new skills and work together, even across the world. Many young people belong to communities on platforms such as Discord, Reddit, Roblox or Minecraft servers. Like any community, they work best with clear rules, kind members and good moderators, and they can also bring risks such as bullying, scams and contact with strangers.",
s:[["A long history","Online communities are older than the World Wide Web. In 1978 two hobbyists in Chicago created one of the first bulletin board systems, where people dialed in by phone to post messages. Usenet newsgroups started in 1980. Later came web forums, chat rooms, social networks, and in the 2010s platforms such as Discord."],["Building things together","Some online communities create amazing things. Thousands of volunteers write and edit Wikipedia. Programmers around the world build free software such as Linux. Players design levels, write guides and create mods for games, and fans translate shows and books into other languages."],["Moderation","Moderators are people who enforce a community’s rules, removing harmful posts and banning troublemakers. Many are volunteers. Clear rules about respect, no harassment and no sharing of personal information help keep communities welcoming. Many platforms also use automated tools to catch spam and harmful content."],["Staying safe","In online communities, people may not be who they say they are. Keep personal details private, never share passwords, be careful with links, and do not meet online friends in person without a trusted adult. If someone makes you uncomfortable, block and report them and tell an adult you trust."]],
k:[["Examples","Forums, servers, group chats, wikis"],["Early example","Bulletin board systems, 1978"],["Kept safe by","Rules and moderators"],["Benefits","Friendship, help, learning, teamwork"],["Risks","Bullying, scams, strangers"]],
d:[["1978","One of the first bulletin board systems opens in Chicago"],["1980","Usenet newsgroups begin"],["2001","Wikipedia is founded"],["2005","Reddit launches"],["2015","Discord launches"]],
s2:[["Finding your people","For young people who feel different or isolated, online communities can offer support and belonging, connecting them with others who share their interests, identities or experiences. Studies show these connections can be valuable, especially when they add to, rather than replace, friendships in daily life."],["Toxic communities","Some communities encourage hate, dangerous challenges or unhealthy behavior. Warning signs include pressure to keep secrets, cruelty toward outsiders and leaders who demand money or loyalty. Leaving a community that makes you feel bad is always okay."]],
sp:["Wikipedia’s volunteers","Wikipedia is written and checked by volunteers, not paid writers. Tens of thousands of people edit it regularly, discussing changes on talk pages and following shared rules about neutrality and citing sources. It shows how a large online community can build something useful for everyone."],
g:[["Moderator","A person who enforces a community’s rules"],["Forum","A website where people post and reply to messages"],["Server","In apps like Discord, a community space with channels"]],
f:["Online communities are older than the World Wide Web.","Wikipedia is written by volunteers in an online community.","Moderators help keep communities safe and friendly."],
fun:"The first bulletin board system was built in early 1978, partly because a Chicago blizzard kept its two creators indoors."});

;
/* data/thin6.js */
// Thin sections, part 6 (28 November 2026): signals, the Moon, space issues, money and pop culture.
ADDART({id:"morsecode",t:"Morse Code",c:"Science and Technology",i:"📡",w:"Morse code",r:["telegraph","radio","ships","titanic","mobilenetworks"],
p:[["Dots and dashes","Morse code spells letters with short and long signals."],["Sent by wire and radio","It carried messages by telegraph and radio for over a century."],["SOS","Its distress call, SOS, became famous worldwide."]],
l:"Morse code is a way of sending text using short and long signals, called dots and dashes, or dits and dahs. Each letter and number has its own pattern: E is a single dot and T a single dash, while SOS is three dots, three dashes and three dots. It was developed in the 1830s and 1840s by the American inventor Samuel Morse and his partner Alfred Vail for the electric telegraph. Morse code can be sent by clicks on a wire, radio beeps, flashes of light or even taps and blinks. For more than a century it carried news, business and emergency messages around the world, and amateur radio operators still use it today.",
s:[["How it works","Common letters get short codes, so messages are quicker to send: E is one dot, T one dash, A is dot dash. A dash lasts three times as long as a dot, and gaps separate letters and words. Skilled operators can send and understand more than 20 words a minute by ear."],["The telegraph","On 24 May 1844 Samuel Morse sent the first long-distance telegraph message in the United States, from Washington, D.C., to Baltimore: What hath God wrought. Telegraph lines soon crossed continents, and undersea cables later linked them, letting news travel in minutes instead of weeks."],["International Morse","The original American Morse code was revised in Europe in the mid-1800s into International Morse code, which became the worldwide standard. It added codes for letters with accents and is the version used today."],["Radio and the sea","With the invention of radio, ships could send Morse messages across the ocean. In 1906 an international conference chose SOS as the distress signal, because its pattern is easy to send and recognize. The Titanic used both the older CQD call and SOS when it sank in 1912."]],
k:[["Invented by","Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail"],["First long-distance US message","24 May 1844"],["Distress signal","SOS (· · · – – – · · ·)"],["Standard form","International Morse code"],["Used today by","Amateur radio operators, pilots’ navigation beacons"]],
d:[["1837","Morse and Vail demonstrate their telegraph"],["1844","What hath God wrought is sent from Washington to Baltimore"],["1906","SOS is adopted as an international distress signal"],["1912","The Titanic sends SOS messages"],["1999","Ships switch from Morse code to satellite distress systems"]],
s2:[["Blinking a message","In 1966 Jeremiah Denton, an American prisoner of war in North Vietnam, was forced to give a television interview. While speaking, he blinked the word TORTURE in Morse code, confirming to the world how prisoners were being treated."],["Still useful","Morse code can get through when voice signals are weak or noisy, so amateur radio fans still use it. Radio beacons that guide aircraft identify themselves in Morse, and people with severe disabilities can use Morse with a single switch or eye blinks to communicate with computers."]],
sp:["Light and sound","Morse code does not need electricity. Navies flash it between ships with signal lamps, and people have sent it by tapping on pipes or walls. In emergencies, three short, three long and three short flashes of a flashlight or blasts of a whistle are widely recognized as SOS."],
g:[["Telegraph","A system for sending messages along wires using electric signals"],["Dit and dah","The short and long signals of Morse code"],["Distress signal","A call for urgent help"]],
f:["E is a single dot in Morse code.","SOS became the international distress signal in 1906.","Ships stopped using Morse for distress calls in 1999."],
fun:"SOS does not stand for anything. It was chosen simply because three dots, three dashes and three dots are easy to send and hard to mistake."});

ADDART({id:"barcodes",t:"Barcodes",c:"Science and Technology",i:"🏷️",w:"Barcode",r:["qrcodes","rfid","ecommerce","computer","laser"],
p:[["Stripes with a message","A barcode stores a number in black bars and white spaces."],["Beep at the checkout","Scanners read barcodes in a fraction of a second."],["Everywhere","Barcodes track products, parcels, books and boarding passes."]],
l:"A barcode is a pattern of black bars and white spaces, or squares and dots, that a machine can read. The widths of the bars stand for numbers or letters, which link to information in a computer, such as a product’s name and price. Barcodes speed up shopping, help stores and warehouses keep track of stock, and are used on parcels, library books, hospital wristbands, tickets and boarding passes. The barcode was invented by Norman Woodland and Bernard Silver, who patented it in 1952, and the first product scanned at a supermarket checkout was a pack of chewing gum in 1974. Two-dimensional codes such as QR codes can hold much more information.",
s:[["How a barcode works","A scanner shines light, often a laser, across the bars. Black bars absorb light and white spaces reflect it, so the scanner sees a pattern of light and dark that it turns into numbers. A check digit at the end lets the scanner confirm it read the code correctly."],["Drawn in the sand","Norman Woodland, a student who knew Morse code, came up with the idea in 1948 while sitting on a beach in Miami. He drew dots and dashes in the sand with his fingers, then pulled them downward into thin and thick lines. He and Bernard Silver filed a patent, granted in 1952."],["The Universal Product Code","Barcodes became practical only after lasers and computers became cheap. In 1973 American grocers chose a design developed at IBM by George Laurer, the Universal Product Code, or UPC. On 26 June 1974, a pack of chewing gum was scanned at a supermarket in Troy, Ohio, the first product sold using a UPC barcode."],["Many kinds","The 12-digit UPC is used in North America, and the 13-digit EAN code in much of the rest of the world; the first digits identify the country where the code was issued. Books have ISBN barcodes. Two-dimensional codes such as QR codes and Data Matrix store information in squares and can be read by phone cameras."]],
k:[["Invented by","Norman Woodland and Bernard Silver"],["Patent granted","1952"],["First supermarket scan","26 June 1974"],["Common types","UPC, EAN, QR code"],["Read by","Laser or camera scanners"]],
d:[["1948","Norman Woodland sketches the idea in the sand"],["1952","Woodland and Silver receive a patent"],["1973","US grocers adopt the Universal Product Code"],["1974","The first product is scanned at a checkout"],["1994","The QR code is invented in Japan"]],
s2:[["Beyond the store","Barcodes help hospitals match patients to the right medicine, let airlines scan boarding passes, track parcels through delivery networks and help scientists tag samples. Some researchers have even glued tiny barcodes to bees to study their behavior."],["What comes next","Newer systems such as RFID tags use radio waves instead of light, so they can be read without a direct line of sight, even many at once. Some stores already use them for clothing and self-checkout, but printed barcodes remain cheap and common."]],
sp:["The gum in the museum","The pack of Wrigley’s Juicy Fruit gum scanned in Troy, Ohio, in 1974 was chosen for the first scan partly to show that even small products could carry a barcode. The pack and its receipt are now kept at the Smithsonian’s National Museum of American History."],
g:[["Barcode","A machine-readable pattern of bars or squares"],["Scanner","A device that reads barcodes"],["Check digit","An extra number used to catch reading errors"]],
f:["The barcode idea was first drawn in sand on a beach.","The first barcode scanned at a checkout was on a pack of gum.","QR codes are a kind of two-dimensional barcode."],
fun:"Norman Woodland based the first barcode on Morse code, stretching dots and dashes into thin and thick lines."});

ADDART({id:"antennas",t:"Antennas",c:"Science and Technology",i:"📶",w:"Antenna (radio)",r:["radio","mobilenetworks","wifi","radioastronomy","smartphone"],
p:[["Catching invisible waves","Antennas send and receive radio waves."],["Hidden everywhere","Phones, cars, routers and satellites all have antennas."],["Size matters","An antenna’s size is matched to the wavelength it uses."]],
l:"An antenna is a device that sends out or picks up radio waves, turning electric signals into invisible waves and back again. Antennas are used in radios, televisions, mobile phones, Wi-Fi routers, GPS receivers, radar, satellites and radio telescopes. They come in many shapes: rods, wires, flat panels, loops and large curved dishes. The size and shape of an antenna depend on the wavelength of the radio waves it is designed for, so a radio tower can be hundreds of meters tall while a phone antenna fits inside the case. Heinrich Hertz built the first antennas in the 1880s to prove that radio waves exist.",
s:[["How antennas work","When an alternating electric current flows back and forth in an antenna, it makes electromagnetic waves that spread outward at the speed of light. When radio waves reach another antenna, they push electrons back and forth inside it, creating a tiny current that a receiver can amplify and decode."],["Matching the wavelength","Antennas work best when their length matches the wavelength. A common design, the half-wave dipole, is half as long as the wave. Long waves used for AM radio need big towers, while the short waves used by Wi-Fi and phones need antennas only a few centimeters long."],["Many designs","A Yagi antenna, invented in Japan in the 1920s, has rows of metal rods and points at a distant station, like old rooftop TV aerials. Dish antennas focus waves like a mirror focuses light, letting satellite TV and deep-space antennas catch faint signals. Phones use tiny antennas printed on circuit boards or built into their frames."],["Phased arrays","Some antennas are made of many small elements working together. By shifting the timing of signals in each element, a phased array can steer its beam electronically without moving. Modern radar, 5G phone towers and satellite internet dishes use this technology."]],
k:[["Job","Send and receive radio waves"],["First built by","Heinrich Hertz, 1880s"],["Key rule","Size matches wavelength"],["Common types","Dipole, Yagi, dish, phased array"],["Largest single dish","FAST, China, 500 m"]],
d:[["1886–1888","Heinrich Hertz builds antennas and detects radio waves"],["1901","Guglielmo Marconi receives a radio signal across the Atlantic"],["1926","Hidetsugu Yagi and Shintaro Uda describe the Yagi antenna"],["1960s","Giant dish antennas begin tracking spacecraft"],["2016","China completes FAST, a 500-meter radio telescope dish"]],
s2:[["Listening to space","Radio telescopes are giant antennas that pick up radio waves from stars, galaxies and the gas between them. NASA’s Deep Space Network uses dishes 70 meters wide in California, Spain and Australia to talk with spacecraft billions of kilometers away, including the Voyager probes."],["Antennas in your body’s world","A modern smartphone may contain several antennas for mobile networks, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, GPS and contactless payments. Engineers must squeeze them into a thin case and keep them working when a hand covers part of the phone."]],
sp:["Signals from Voyager","Voyager 1, launched in 1977, is more than 24 billion kilometers from Earth. Its radio transmitter has only about the power of a refrigerator light bulb, and by the time its signal reaches Earth it is incredibly faint. Only the huge dish antennas of the Deep Space Network can still pick it up."],
g:[["Antenna","A device that sends or receives radio waves"],["Wavelength","The distance between one wave peak and the next"],["Phased array","A group of antennas that steer a beam electronically"]],
f:["Heinrich Hertz built the first antennas in the 1880s.","Antenna size is matched to the wavelength it uses.","The Deep Space Network’s largest dishes are 70 meters wide."],
fun:"The radio meaning of the word antenna is credited to Guglielmo Marconi. In Italian, antenna means a pole, and he used it for the tall masts that held up his wires."});

ADDART({id:"apollo11",t:"Apollo 11",c:"Space",i:"👨‍🚀",w:"Apollo 11",r:["apollo","armstrong","apollo8","apollo13","moon","artemis"],
p:[["First Moon landing","Apollo 11 landed the first people on the Moon in July 1969."],["Three astronauts","Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin and Michael Collins flew the mission."],["One small step","Armstrong’s first words on the Moon became famous."]],
l:"Apollo 11 was the American space mission that landed the first humans on the Moon. It launched from Florida on 16 July 1969 on a Saturn V rocket, carrying astronauts Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin and Michael Collins. On 20 July, Armstrong and Aldrin landed the lunar module Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility, while Collins orbited above in the command module Columbia. Armstrong stepped onto the surface first, saying: That’s one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind. The two men spent about two and a half hours walking on the Moon, collected rocks, set up experiments and planted a US flag before returning safely to Earth on 24 July.",
s:[["The journey","The Saturn V, the most powerful rocket flown up to that time, sent the astronauts on a three-day trip to the Moon. After entering lunar orbit, Armstrong and Aldrin climbed into Eagle and separated from Columbia. Collins stayed behind, circling the Moon alone and losing radio contact with Earth each time he passed behind it."],["A tense landing","As Eagle descended, its computer flashed unfamiliar 1202 and 1201 alarms because it was overloaded, but mission controllers decided it was safe to continue. Armstrong saw the planned landing site was strewn with boulders and flew the lander manually to a safer spot, touching down with less than a minute of fuel left. He radioed: The Eagle has landed."],["On the surface","About six and a half hours later, Armstrong climbed down the ladder and stepped onto the Moon. Aldrin followed. They found it easy to move in the low gravity, collected about 21.5 kilograms of rock and soil, set up a laser reflector and a seismometer, and spoke by phone with US President Richard Nixon."],["Coming home","After about 21 hours on the Moon, Eagle lifted off and rejoined Columbia. The crew splashed down in the Pacific Ocean on 24 July 1969 and spent three weeks in quarantine in case they had brought back germs from the Moon. Around 650 million people are thought to have watched the first steps on television."]],
k:[["Launch","16 July 1969"],["Moon landing","20 July 1969"],["Crew","Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin, Michael Collins"],["Landing site","Sea of Tranquility"],["Samples returned","About 21.5 kg"]],
d:[["16 July 1969","Apollo 11 launches from Kennedy Space Center"],["19 July 1969","The spacecraft enters orbit around the Moon"],["20 July 1969","Eagle lands in the Sea of Tranquility"],["21 July 1969","Armstrong steps onto the Moon (UTC)"],["24 July 1969","The crew splashes down in the Pacific"]],
s2:[["What they left behind","The astronauts left a plaque reading: Here men from the planet Earth first set foot upon the Moon, July 1969 A.D. We came in peace for all mankind. They also left a disk with goodwill messages from world leaders, medals honoring astronauts and cosmonauts who had died, and the laser reflector, which scientists still bounce lasers off to measure the Moon’s distance."],["A race won","Apollo 11 fulfilled President John F. Kennedy’s 1961 goal of landing a man on the Moon before the end of the decade and was a high point of the space race with the Soviet Union. Six Apollo missions landed in all, and twelve astronauts walked on the Moon by the end of 1972."]],
sp:["The forgotten astronaut","Michael Collins never walked on the Moon, but his job was vital: if anything went wrong, he had to bring Columbia home. Alone behind the Moon, cut off from all contact, he later wrote that he felt not lonely but aware, and even joyful. He is sometimes called the loneliest man in history."],
g:[["Lunar module","The spacecraft that landed on the Moon"],["Command module","The spacecraft that carried the crew to the Moon and back"],["Quarantine","Keeping people apart to stop the spread of possible germs"]],
f:["Eagle landed with less than a minute of fuel to spare.","About 650 million people watched the first steps.","A laser reflector left by Apollo 11 is still used today."],
fun:"The footprints left by Armstrong and Aldrin are probably still there, because the Moon has no wind or rain to wipe them away."});

ADDART({id:"moonrocks",t:"Moon Rocks",c:"Space",i:"🪨",w:"Moon rock",r:["apollo","apollo11","changeprogram","lunaprogram","moon","meteorites"],
p:[["Pieces of the Moon","Moon rocks are samples brought back from the Moon or fallen as meteorites."],["Clues to the past","They reveal how the Moon and Earth formed."],["Rare treasures","Only a few hundred kilograms have ever been collected."]],
l:"Moon rocks are pieces of the Moon studied by scientists on Earth. Most were collected by the six Apollo missions that landed astronauts on the Moon between 1969 and 1972, which brought back about 382 kilograms of rock, soil and core samples. Robotic Soviet Luna probes returned small amounts in the 1970s, and China’s Chang’e 5 and Chang’e 6 missions brought back samples in 2020 and 2024, the latter from the Moon’s far side for the first time. Other pieces arrive on Earth naturally as lunar meteorites. Moon rocks show that the Moon is about 4.5 billion years old and support the idea that it formed after a giant collision with the young Earth.",
s:[["Kinds of rock","The Moon’s dark plains, called maria, are made of basalt, volcanic rock from ancient lava flows. The bright highlands are made mostly of anorthosite, a pale rock that floated to the top of a global ocean of molten rock when the Moon was young. Many samples are breccias, broken rock fragments welded together by impacts."],["Where the Moon came from","Moon rocks are remarkably similar to Earth rocks in the types of oxygen they contain, but they have almost no water and fewer easily evaporated elements. This supports the giant-impact hypothesis: that a Mars-sized body struck the early Earth about 4.5 billion years ago, and the Moon formed from the debris."],["Robot collectors","The Soviet Luna 16, 20 and 24 missions returned about 300 grams of soil between 1970 and 1976. China’s Chang’e 5 brought back about 1.7 kilograms of young volcanic rock in 2020. In 2024 Chang’e 6 returned about 1.9 kilograms from a huge crater on the far side, the first samples from the side always facing away from Earth."],["Protected and shared","Apollo samples are stored in special nitrogen-filled cabinets at NASA’s Johnson Space Center in Houston to keep them clean. Scientists around the world can borrow small pieces for research. Some samples were sealed in 1972 and opened only in 2019 and later, so that newer tools could study them."]],
k:[["Apollo samples","About 382 kg"],["Luna samples","About 300 g"],["Chang’e 5 (2020)","About 1.7 kg"],["Chang’e 6 (2024)","About 1.9 kg, from the far side"],["Age of the Moon","About 4.5 billion years"]],
d:[["1969","Apollo 11 returns the first Moon rocks"],["1970","Luna 16 makes the first robotic sample return"],["1972","Apollo 17 returns the last Apollo samples"],["1982","Scientists identify the first lunar meteorite, found in Antarctica"],["2020","Chang’e 5 returns samples"],["2024","Chang’e 6 returns the first far-side samples"]],
s2:[["The Genesis Rock","Among the most famous Moon rocks is a piece of anorthosite collected by Apollo 15 astronauts in 1971, nicknamed the Genesis Rock. More than 4 billion years old, it is a piece of the Moon’s early crust and helped scientists understand how the Moon’s surface formed."],["Lost gifts","After the Apollo 11 and Apollo 17 missions, the United States gave small pieces of Moon rock, mounted in displays, to more than 130 countries and to every US state. Many have since gone missing, been stolen or sold illegally, and volunteers have tracked down some of them."]],
sp:["Moon rocks from the sky","Not all Moon rocks came back on spacecraft. When asteroids strike the Moon, some rock is blasted into space, and a few pieces eventually fall to Earth as meteorites. More than 500 lunar meteorites have been found, many in the deserts of North Africa and on the ice of Antarctica, where dark rocks stand out against the white."],
g:[["Maria","The dark plains on the Moon, made of ancient lava"],["Anorthosite","A pale rock that makes up the Moon’s highlands"],["Breccia","Rock made of broken pieces cemented together"]],
f:["Apollo astronauts brought back about 382 kilograms of Moon rock.","Chang’e 6 returned the first samples from the Moon’s far side in 2024.","Some Apollo samples stayed sealed for nearly 50 years."],
fun:"Moon dust is so sharp and clingy that it scratched Apollo spacesuits and smelled, according to the astronauts, like spent gunpowder."});

ADDART({id:"artemisii",t:"Artemis II",c:"Space",i:"🌘",w:"Artemis II",r:["artemis","apollo8","apollo11","nasa","csa","artemisaccords"],
p:[["Back around the Moon","Artemis II carried four astronauts around the Moon in April 2026."],["First since Apollo","It was the first crewed flight beyond low Earth orbit since 1972."],["Record breaker","The crew traveled farther from Earth than any humans before."]],
l:"Artemis II was the first crewed mission of NASA’s Artemis program and the first time people had traveled to the vicinity of the Moon since Apollo 17 in 1972. Four astronauts, NASA’s Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover and Christina Koch and the Canadian Space Agency’s Jeremy Hansen, launched from Florida on 1 April 2026 aboard an Orion spacecraft on top of the Space Launch System rocket. They looped around the far side of the Moon on a free-return path, setting a new record for the farthest distance humans have traveled from Earth, about 406,800 kilometers, and splashed down in the Pacific Ocean on 10 April 2026. The flight tested Orion’s systems with people on board before future landings.",
s:[["The crew","Commander Reid Wiseman led the mission, with pilot Victor Glover and mission specialists Christina Koch and Jeremy Hansen. Koch became the first woman, Glover the first Black astronaut and Hansen the first non-American to travel around the Moon."],["The flight","After launch, Orion first circled Earth to check its systems, then fired its engine to head for the Moon. It swung around the Moon’s far side, using the Moon’s gravity to sling it back toward Earth without needing a big engine burn, a free-return trajectory like the one used by Apollo 13."],["A delayed start","Artemis II faced years of delays. A launch attempt in early 2026 was called off after a leak, and the rocket was rolled back for repairs before the successful launch on 1 April. The mission followed Artemis I, an uncrewed test flight around the Moon in 2022."],["Testing for landings","The crew tested Orion’s life support, navigation and communication systems, practiced flying the spacecraft by hand and took photographs and observations of the Moon. Orion’s heat shield protected them as they returned through Earth’s atmosphere at about 40,000 kilometers per hour."]],
k:[["Launch","1 April 2026"],["Splashdown","10 April 2026"],["Crew","Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover, Christina Koch, Jeremy Hansen"],["Spacecraft","Orion on the Space Launch System"],["Distance record","About 406,800 km from Earth"]],
d:[["2019","NASA names the Artemis program"],["2022","Artemis I flies an uncrewed Orion around the Moon"],["2023","The Artemis II crew is announced"],["1 April 2026","Artemis II launches"],["10 April 2026","The crew splashes down in the Pacific"]],
s2:[["What comes next","In February 2026 NASA reshaped its plans. Artemis III, targeted for 2027, will test docking Orion with a commercial lunar lander in orbit around Earth, and the first crewed landing near the Moon’s south pole is planned for Artemis IV in 2028."],["International teamwork","Artemis is a partnership. Canada provided an astronaut seat in exchange for building a robotic arm for a future lunar space station. The European Space Agency builds Orion’s service module, which provides power, propulsion, air and water."]],
sp:["Breaking Apollo 13’s record","For 56 years, the record for the farthest humans had ever traveled from Earth belonged to the crew of Apollo 13, who in 1970 swung around the far side of the Moon after an explosion forced them to abandon a landing. Artemis II’s crew passed that distance on their flight around the Moon."],
g:[["Free-return trajectory","A path that uses the Moon’s gravity to send a spacecraft back to Earth"],["Orion","NASA’s spacecraft for carrying astronauts beyond low Earth orbit"],["Heat shield","A layer that protects a spacecraft from heat during reentry"]],
f:["Artemis II was the first crewed trip near the Moon since 1972.","Christina Koch became the first woman to travel around the Moon.","The crew set a record for the farthest humans have traveled from Earth."],
fun:"From the far side of the Moon, the Artemis II astronauts could see the whole Moon close up with Earth appearing small behind it, a view no humans had seen for more than 50 years."});

ADDART({id:"spacemedicine",t:"Space Medicine",c:"Space",i:"🩺",w:"Space medicine",r:["astronauts","iss","spacetourism","spaceweather","telemedicine","heart"],
p:[["Health beyond Earth","Space medicine studies how space affects the human body."],["Weightless bodies","Without gravity, bones and muscles weaken."],["Protecting astronauts","Exercise, diet and shielding keep crews healthy."]],
l:"Space medicine is the branch of medicine that studies how spaceflight affects the human body and how to keep astronauts healthy. In weightlessness, bones lose minerals, muscles shrink, fluids shift toward the head, and the heart and eyes change. Astronauts are also exposed to more radiation than on Earth, sleep in a noisy, confined place and live far from hospitals. Doctors and scientists use exercise, special diets, medicines and careful monitoring to protect them. Research on the International Space Station has shown what happens to the body over months in space, knowledge needed before longer missions to the Moon and Mars.",
s:[["Bones and muscles","On Earth, gravity constantly loads bones and muscles. In space, astronauts can lose roughly 1 to 1.5 percent of the bone mass in their hips and spine each month. Muscles in the legs and back weaken. To slow these losses, astronauts on the space station exercise about two hours a day on a treadmill, a bike and a resistance machine."],["Fluids, heart and eyes","Without gravity pulling fluids down, blood and other fluids shift toward the head, making faces puffy and legs thin. The heart does not have to work as hard and can shrink slightly. Some astronauts develop swelling at the back of the eye that blurs vision, a condition still being studied."],["Radiation","Outside Earth’s protective atmosphere and magnetic field, astronauts receive much higher doses of radiation from the Sun and cosmic rays, raising their lifetime risk of cancer. Spacecraft have shielded areas where crews shelter during solar storms. Protecting crews from radiation is one of the biggest challenges for a mission to Mars."],["Mind and sleep","Living in a small space with the same few people, far from family, can be stressful. Astronauts see about 16 sunrises a day on the space station, which confuses the body clock. Careful schedules, lighting that mimics day and night, private calls home and psychological support help."]],
k:[["Bone loss in space","About 1–1.5% per month in some bones"],["Daily exercise on the ISS","About 2 hours"],["Longest single spaceflight","437 days, Valeri Polyakov (1994–95)"],["Main dangers","Weightlessness, radiation, isolation"],["Famous study","NASA Twins Study, 2015–2016"]],
d:[["1961","Yuri Gagarin shows that humans can survive spaceflight"],["1995","Valeri Polyakov completes a record 437 days in space"],["2016","Scott Kelly returns after nearly a year in space for the Twins Study"],["2019","Results of the Twins Study are published"],["2023","Frank Rubio returns after 371 days, a US record"]],
s2:[["The Twins Study","Astronaut Scott Kelly spent nearly a year on the space station while his identical twin brother Mark, also an astronaut, stayed on Earth. Comparing the twins showed changes in Scott’s genes’ activity, immune system and eyes, though most returned to normal after he came home."],["Benefits on Earth","Space medicine has helped people on the ground. Research on bone loss in astronauts has improved understanding of osteoporosis, and techniques for remote medical care in space have helped doctors treat patients in isolated places through telemedicine."]],
sp:["Back on their feet","After months in space, returning astronauts often feel dizzy and heavy, and some cannot walk steadily at first. They are often helped out of their capsule and spend weeks doing physical therapy to rebuild balance, bone and muscle. Many say even holding up their head feels hard on the first day."],
g:[["Microgravity","The near-weightlessness felt in orbit"],["Cosmic rays","High-energy particles from space"],["Osteoporosis","A condition in which bones become weak and brittle"]],
f:["Astronauts on the space station exercise about two hours a day.","They see about 16 sunrises a day.","The longest single spaceflight lasted 437 days."],
fun:"Astronauts can grow up to about 3 percent taller in space because their spines stretch without gravity, but they shrink back after returning to Earth."});

ADDART({id:"spacemining",t:"Space Mining",c:"Space",i:"⛏️",w:"Space mining",r:["asteroids","spacelaw","artemisaccords","moonbases","spacex","moonrocks"],
p:[["Resources from space","Space mining means using materials from the Moon, asteroids or planets."],["Water is the prize","Ice could provide drinking water, air and rocket fuel."],["Not yet begun","No commercial mining in space has happened so far."]],
l:"Space mining is the idea of extracting useful materials from the Moon, asteroids, comets or other worlds. The most valuable early target is probably water ice, found in permanently shadowed craters near the Moon’s poles. Water can be split into hydrogen and oxygen for rocket fuel and breathing air, so using it in space would be far cheaper than launching it from Earth. Some asteroids contain metals such as iron, nickel and platinum. No commercial mining has yet taken place beyond Earth, but space agencies have returned samples from asteroids, and companies and governments are developing technologies and rules for future mining.",
s:[["Ice on the Moon","In 2009 NASA’s LCROSS mission crashed a rocket stage into a dark crater near the Moon’s south pole and detected water in the plume of debris. Later studies confirmed ice in shadowed craters at both poles. This is a major reason why many countries plan to send astronauts and robots to the lunar south pole."],["Living off the land","Using local materials in space is called in-situ resource utilization. Besides water, lunar soil contains oxygen bound in minerals, and it could be melted or pressed into bricks for building. Experiments have already produced oxygen from simulated lunar soil and from Mars air, on NASA’s Perseverance rover."],["Asteroids","Asteroids are leftovers from the formation of the solar system. Some are rocky, some icy and some metal-rich. Japan’s Hayabusa2 and NASA’s OSIRIS-REx brought back samples from the asteroids Ryugu and Bennu, and NASA’s Psyche spacecraft is traveling to a metal-rich asteroid, arriving in 2029."],["Who owns space?","The 1967 Outer Space Treaty says no country can claim the Moon or other celestial bodies. Some countries, including the United States and Luxembourg, have passed laws letting their companies own resources they extract. The Artemis Accords, signed by many countries, support resource use, while others argue for a shared international system."]],
k:[["Main early target","Water ice at the Moon’s poles"],["Water uses","Drinking, breathing air, rocket fuel"],["Asteroid samples returned","Itokawa, Ryugu, Bennu"],["Key treaty","Outer Space Treaty, 1967"],["Commercial mining so far","None"]],
d:[["1967","The Outer Space Treaty is signed"],["2009","LCROSS detects water at the Moon’s south pole"],["2015","A US law lets American companies own resources they mine in space"],["2020","Hayabusa2 returns samples from asteroid Ryugu"],["2023","OSIRIS-REx returns samples from asteroid Bennu"]],
s2:[["A one-dollar deal","In 2020 NASA agreed to buy small amounts of lunar soil from private companies for prices from $1 to $15,000, to set an example that companies can collect and sell resources in space. The companies would collect the soil on future missions and transfer ownership to NASA on the Moon."],["Worries and limits","Critics warn that mining could damage scientifically valuable sites, create space debris and benefit only rich countries and companies. Bringing large amounts of material back to Earth would be extremely expensive, so most experts expect space resources to be used in space, at least at first."]],
sp:["A sample from Bennu","In September 2023 a capsule from NASA’s OSIRIS-REx spacecraft landed in the Utah desert with about 121 grams of rock and dust from the asteroid Bennu, the largest asteroid sample ever returned. Scientists found water-bearing minerals and building blocks of life, but extracting such materials on a large scale remains a challenge for the future."],
g:[["In-situ resource utilization","Using materials found where you are in space"],["Permanently shadowed crater","A crater never reached by sunlight"],["Regolith","The loose rock and dust covering the Moon or an asteroid"]],
f:["Water ice exists in dark craters at the Moon’s poles.","OSIRIS-REx brought back 121 grams of asteroid Bennu.","No commercial mining has happened in space yet."],
fun:"Rocket fuel made from water on the Moon could make the Moon a refueling station for missions going deeper into the solar system."});

ADDART({id:"satellites",t:"Satellites",c:"Space",i:"🛰️",w:"Satellite",r:["sputnik","gps","weathersatellites","megaconstellations","spacejunk","antennas"],
p:[["Machines in orbit","Artificial satellites are machines that circle Earth."],["Part of daily life","They bring us weather forecasts, maps, TV and internet."],["Getting crowded","More than 15,000 working satellites now orbit Earth."]],
l:"An artificial satellite is a machine placed in orbit around Earth or another body. The first, the Soviet Union’s Sputnik 1, was launched on 4 October 1957. Today satellites provide navigation through systems like GPS, weather forecasts, television, phone and internet links, images of Earth for maps and disaster response, and scientific observations of space. They orbit at different heights: low Earth orbit, a few hundred kilometers up, and geostationary orbit, about 35,800 kilometers up, where a satellite stays over the same spot. As of November 2026, more than 15,800 working satellites orbit Earth, about 11,000 of them belonging to SpaceX’s Starlink internet network.",
s:[["Staying in orbit","A satellite stays up because it moves sideways extremely fast, about 28,000 kilometers per hour in low orbit. It is always falling toward Earth, but it moves forward so quickly that Earth’s surface curves away beneath it. Higher orbits need less speed, and satellites there take longer to go around."],["Kinds of orbit","Low Earth orbit is home to the International Space Station, Earth-imaging satellites and internet constellations. Navigation satellites such as GPS orbit at about 20,000 kilometers. Geostationary satellites, 35,786 kilometers above the equator, match Earth’s rotation, so TV dishes can point at them without moving."],["Jobs satellites do","Communications satellites relay phone calls, TV and internet. Weather satellites track storms. Navigation satellites send precise timing signals that phones use to find their location. Observation satellites map forests, crops, cities and ice. Science satellites, like space telescopes, look out into the universe."],["Crowded skies","The number of satellites has soared since 2019 as companies launched large constellations for internet service. More satellites bring more risk of collisions and space debris, and their reflected light can streak across astronomers’ images. Rules for removing old satellites and avoiding collisions are being developed."]],
k:[["First satellite","Sputnik 1, 4 October 1957"],["Working satellites","More than 15,800 (November 2026)"],["Largest network","Starlink, about 11,000 satellites"],["Low orbit speed","About 28,000 km/h"],["Geostationary height","About 35,786 km"]],
d:[["1945","Arthur C. Clarke proposes geostationary communication satellites"],["1957","Sputnik 1 becomes the first artificial satellite"],["1962","Telstar relays the first live transatlantic TV"],["1978","The first GPS satellite is launched"],["2019","SpaceX begins launching Starlink satellites in large batches"]],
s2:[["Satellites in disasters","After earthquakes, floods and fires, satellites quickly photograph damaged areas, helping rescuers plan where to go. An international agreement, the International Charter on Space and Major Disasters, lets countries hit by disasters request free satellite images."],["Small satellites","Not all satellites are big. CubeSats, built from cubes 10 centimeters on a side, are cheap enough for universities and even schools to build. Hundreds have been launched to test new technology and study Earth and space."]],
sp:["Spotting satellites yourself","Many satellites can be seen with the naked eye on a clear night, shortly after sunset or before sunrise, as steady points of light moving across the sky. The International Space Station is often the brightest. Free apps and websites predict when satellites will pass overhead."],
g:[["Orbit","The curved path of an object around a planet or star"],["Geostationary orbit","An orbit where a satellite stays above one spot on Earth"],["Constellation","In spaceflight, a group of satellites working together"]],
f:["Sputnik 1 was launched in 1957.","More than 15,800 working satellites orbit Earth.","Geostationary satellites orbit about 35,786 kilometers up."],
fun:"Sputnik 1 was a metal ball about the size of a beach ball, and its simple beeping radio signal could be picked up by amateur radio operators around the world."});

ADDART({id:"banks",t:"Banks",c:"Society",i:"🏦",w:"Bank",r:["budgeting","interestdebt","digitalpay","creditcards","inflation","financialliteracy"],
p:[["Keeping money safe","Banks hold people’s money and lend it to others."],["Interest","Banks pay interest on savings and charge interest on loans."],["Central banks","A country’s central bank manages its money supply."]],
l:"A bank is a business that keeps people’s money safe, lends money and helps move money from place to place. When you deposit money in a bank, it can pay you interest, and it lends much of that money to people and businesses who need loans for homes, cars, schools or new companies, charging them higher interest. Banks also provide payment cards, transfers and online banking. A country’s central bank, such as the US Federal Reserve or the European Central Bank, manages the money supply, sets key interest rates and oversees other banks. Modern banking grew from moneylenders and merchants in medieval Italy, and the word bank comes from the Italian banco, meaning bench.",
s:[["How banks make money","Banks earn money mainly from the difference between the interest they charge borrowers and the interest they pay savers. They also charge fees for services such as transfers, cards and accounts. Because only some depositors want their money at the same time, banks can lend out most of what they hold, keeping a portion in reserve."],["From benches to branches","In medieval Italy, moneychangers sat at benches in markets to exchange coins and lend money. Wealthy families such as the Medici of Florence built banking networks across Europe. Banca Monte dei Paschi di Siena, founded in 1472, is often called the oldest bank still operating."],["Central banks","The Swedish Riksbank, founded in 1668, and the Bank of England, founded in 1694, are among the oldest central banks. Central banks issue banknotes, act as lenders of last resort when banks are in trouble and adjust interest rates to keep prices stable and support jobs."],["Bank runs and safety","If many depositors fear a bank will fail and rush to withdraw money at once, it can collapse, a bank run. After waves of bank failures in the Great Depression, many countries created deposit insurance, which guarantees savers’ money up to a limit. Governments also regulate how much banks must hold in reserve."]],
k:[["Main jobs","Holding deposits, lending, payments"],["Earn money from","Interest differences and fees"],["Oldest operating bank","Monte dei Paschi di Siena, 1472"],["Oldest central bank","Sveriges Riksbank, 1668"],["Word origin","Italian banco, bench"]],
d:[["1472","Monte dei Paschi di Siena is founded"],["1668","Sweden’s Riksbank is founded"],["1694","The Bank of England is founded"],["1933","The United States creates federal deposit insurance"],["1967","The first cash machine (ATM) opens in London"]],
s2:[["Banking for everyone","More than a billion adults around the world still do not have a bank account. Mobile money services, which let people store and send money with a basic phone, have spread banking to millions, especially in Africa. Microfinance banks such as Grameen Bank, founded in Bangladesh by Muhammad Yunus, make small loans to people without collateral."],["Digital banks","Many people now bank mostly through apps, checking balances, paying bills and sending money instantly. Some banks have no branches at all. This convenience brings new risks, such as scams and phishing, so banks use passwords, one-time codes and fraud alerts to protect customers."]],
sp:["The first cash machine","On 27 June 1967, the world’s first cash machine opened at a Barclays bank branch in Enfield, north London. Customers used special paper vouchers, not plastic cards, to withdraw cash. Its inventor, John Shepherd-Barron, said he came up with the idea after arriving at his bank just after it closed."],
g:[["Deposit","Money placed in a bank account"],["Interest","Money paid for borrowing or earned for saving"],["Central bank","A country’s main bank, which manages its money"]],
f:["The word bank comes from the Italian word for bench.","The world’s first cash machine opened in London in 1967.","Sweden’s Riksbank is the oldest central bank."],
fun:"The word bankrupt comes from the Italian banca rotta, broken bench, from the custom of breaking the bench of a moneylender who could not pay his debts."});

ADDART({id:"retirement",t:"Pensions and Retirement",c:"Society",i:"👵",w:"Pension",r:["aging","budgeting","banks","interestdebt","stockm","financialliteracy"],
p:[["Life after work","Retirement is when people stop working, usually in later life."],["Paying for it","Pensions and savings provide income after work ends."],["An aging world","As people live longer, countries are rethinking pensions."]],
l:"Retirement is the stage of life when people stop working full time, usually in their 60s. Retired people often live on a pension, regular payments from the government, a former employer or their own savings. Most countries have a state pension system paid for by taxes or contributions from workers, and many people also save in workplace or personal retirement plans that invest money over decades. Germany created one of the first national old-age pensions in 1889. Today, as people live longer and fewer babies are born, many countries face the challenge of paying for more retirees with fewer workers, and some are raising the age at which pensions begin.",
s:[["Kinds of pensions","State pensions are run by governments and often paid from the contributions of today’s workers. Workplace pensions are run by employers, sometimes promising a set income for life and sometimes building up a savings pot. Personal pensions and savings accounts let individuals put money aside themselves, often with tax benefits."],["The power of starting early","Money invested for retirement can grow through compound interest, earning returns on past returns. Someone who starts saving small amounts in their 20s may end up with more than someone who saves larger amounts starting in their 40s, because the money has longer to grow."],["Retirement ages","Pension ages vary by country, typically between about 60 and 67. Many countries are gradually raising them as life expectancy rises. Such changes are often controversial, and proposals to raise the retirement age have led to large protests in some countries."],["An aging world","In 1950 there were many working-age people for every person over 65. Today that ratio is much lower in countries such as Japan, Italy and South Korea, where more than one in five people is over 65. Countries are responding by raising pension ages, encouraging people to work longer, increasing contributions and attracting younger workers."]],
k:[["Usual age","About 60 to 67, varying by country"],["First national old-age pension","Germany, 1889"],["Main kinds","State, workplace and personal pensions"],["Key idea","Compound interest rewards saving early"],["Big challenge","Aging populations"]],
d:[["1889","Germany introduces old-age pensions under Otto von Bismarck"],["1908","Britain introduces a state pension"],["1935","The United States creates Social Security"],["1981","Chile introduces a system of private individual pension accounts"]],
s2:[["Life in retirement","Retirement can last 20 or 30 years. Many retired people travel, volunteer, care for grandchildren, learn new skills or start new projects. Staying active, connected to friends and engaged with interests is linked to better health and happiness in later life."],["Not everyone can retire","Hundreds of millions of older people around the world have no pension, especially those who worked in informal jobs without contracts. They may rely on family or keep working into old age. Some countries have created small universal pensions paid to all older people to reduce poverty."]],
sp:["Why 65?","The idea that retirement begins around 65 dates back more than a century. Germany’s first pensions in 1889 started at age 70, later lowered to 65, at a time when few people lived that long. Today many people live 20 years or more beyond 65, which is why governments are debating whether the old age limits still make sense."],
g:[["Pension","Regular payments made to a person after they retire"],["Compound interest","Interest earned on both savings and past interest"],["Life expectancy","The average number of years a person is expected to live"]],
f:["Germany introduced old-age pensions in 1889.","Pension ages usually range from about 60 to 67.","Saving early lets compound interest do more work."],
fun:"When Germany introduced old-age pensions in 1889, the starting age was 70, older than most workers of that time ever lived to."});

ADDART({id:"consumerrights",t:"Consumer Rights",c:"Society",i:"🛒",w:"Consumer protection",r:["ecommerce","onlinescams","advertising","costliving","budgeting","digitalpay"],
p:[["Fair deals for shoppers","Consumer rights protect people who buy goods and services."],["Safety and honesty","Products should be safe and described truthfully."],["Refunds and repairs","Faulty products can be returned, repaired or replaced."]],
l:"Consumer rights are protections that help people buying goods and services get safe products, honest information and fair treatment. They include the right to products that are safe and work as described, to clear prices and information, to choose between competing products, to complain and be heard, and to a repair, replacement or refund when something is faulty. Governments enforce these rights through laws and consumer protection agencies, and consumer groups test products and campaign for change. In 1962 US President John F. Kennedy set out four basic consumer rights, and 15 March is celebrated as World Consumer Rights Day.",
s:[["The basic rights","Kennedy named the right to safety, the right to be informed, the right to choose and the right to be heard. Consumer groups later added the right to redress, which means fixing problems; the right to consumer education; the right to a healthy environment; and the right to basic needs such as food, water and housing."],["When something goes wrong","In many countries, goods must be of reasonable quality, fit for their purpose and as described. If a new product breaks soon after purchase, buyers can usually ask for a repair, replacement or refund. Keeping receipts, checking warranties and contacting the seller first are useful steps."],["Shopping online","Online shopping has its own protections. In the European Union, buyers generally have 14 days to change their minds about most online purchases. Paying with methods that offer buyer protection, checking seller reviews and avoiding deals that seem too good to be true help shoppers avoid scams."],["Product safety","Governments set safety standards for things like toys, cars, food, electrical goods and medicines. When dangerous faults are found, companies must issue recalls and fix or replace products. Investigations by journalists and campaigners, such as Ralph Nader’s 1965 book on car safety, Unsafe at Any Speed, led to stronger safety laws."]],
k:[["Kennedy’s four rights","Safety, information, choice, being heard"],["World Consumer Rights Day","15 March"],["UN guidelines","1985"],["Common remedies","Repair, replacement or refund"],["EU online cooling-off period","Generally 14 days"]],
d:[["1906","The United States passes its first federal food and drug safety laws"],["1962","President Kennedy sets out four consumer rights"],["1965","Ralph Nader publishes Unsafe at Any Speed"],["1983","World Consumer Rights Day is first celebrated"],["1985","The United Nations adopts guidelines for consumer protection"]],
s2:[["Tricky designs","Some websites use dark patterns, designs that trick people into buying more, signing up for subscriptions or sharing data, such as hidden fees revealed only at checkout or making it very hard to cancel. Regulators in several countries have started fining companies for such tricks."],["The right to repair","A growing movement argues that people should be able to fix their own phones, cars and appliances, or take them to independent repair shops, instead of throwing them away. Laws in the European Union and several US states now require some manufacturers to make spare parts and repair information available."]],
sp:["Testing products","Independent consumer organizations, such as Consumer Reports in the United States and Which? in the United Kingdom, buy products anonymously and test them in laboratories for safety, quality and value. Their results help shoppers choose wisely and sometimes reveal dangers that lead to recalls."],
g:[["Consumer","A person who buys goods or services for their own use"],["Warranty","A promise to repair or replace a product if it fails"],["Recall","A request to return a product because of a safety problem"]],
f:["15 March is World Consumer Rights Day.","President Kennedy named four consumer rights in 1962.","EU shoppers generally have 14 days to cancel most online purchases."],
fun:"World Consumer Rights Day falls on 15 March because that is the date in 1962 when President Kennedy gave his speech on consumer rights to the US Congress."});

ADDART({id:"fanfiction",t:"Fan Fiction",c:"Arts and Culture",i:"✍️",w:"Fan fiction",r:["fandom","anime","superhero","cosplay","copyrightonline","onlinecommunities"],
p:[["Stories by fans","Fan fiction is writing by fans using characters from books, films or games."],["A creative hobby","Millions of people write and read it online."],["A path for writers","Many professional authors began by writing fan fiction."]],
l:"Fan fiction, often called fanfic, is writing by fans that uses characters, settings or ideas from existing books, films, TV shows, comics, anime or video games. Fans write new adventures, imagine what happened between scenes, explore minor characters, change endings or combine different stories. Fan fiction has a long history, from retellings of myths to fan magazines of the 1960s, but the internet made it hugely popular. Websites such as FanFiction.Net and Archive of Our Own host millions of stories. Writing fan fiction helps people practice storytelling and connect with others, though it raises questions about copyright.",
s:[["Old roots","Writers have always borrowed from earlier stories. Ancient poets retold the myths of Troy, and Shakespeare reworked older plays. In the 1890s fans wrote their own Sherlock Holmes stories. Modern fan fiction grew in the 1960s, when Star Trek fans began sharing stories in homemade magazines called fanzines."],["Going online","With the internet, fans could share stories instantly with readers worldwide. FanFiction.Net opened in 1998, Wattpad in 2006 and Archive of Our Own, run by volunteers of the Organization for Transformative Works, in 2008. Popular sources include Harry Potter, anime, superhero films and K-pop."],["The language of fanfic","Fan fiction has its own terms. Canon means the original story, while AU, or alternate universe, puts characters in a different world, such as a coffee shop or a school. A one-shot is a single complete story, and tags help readers find stories by characters, themes and content warnings."],["Copyright questions","Fan fiction uses characters owned by others, so its legal status is unclear in many places. Most fan writers do not charge money, which may help their work count as fair use. Some authors welcome fan fiction, while others ask fans not to write it."]],
k:[["Also called","Fanfic"],["Early modern form","Star Trek fanzines, 1960s"],["Major sites","FanFiction.Net, Archive of Our Own, Wattpad"],["Key term","Canon, the original story"],["Award","Archive of Our Own won a Hugo Award in 2019"]],
d:[["1967","Star Trek fans publish Spockanalia, an early fanzine with fan stories"],["1998","FanFiction.Net is launched"],["2006","Wattpad is launched"],["2008","Archive of Our Own opens"],["2019","Archive of Our Own wins a Hugo Award"]],
s2:[["From fanfic to bestsellers","Several professional writers began with fan fiction, and some published novels started as fan stories with the names changed. Writing for an audience of fans gives new writers practice, feedback and confidence."],["A welcoming community","Fan fiction communities are known for encouraging beginners, with readers leaving comments and kudos. Many writers are teenagers and young adults, and many are women, who have long been central to fan writing communities."]],
sp:["Writing your own","Anyone can try writing fan fiction. Many writers start by asking a what if question: What if a minor character were the hero? What happened after the last chapter? Reading widely, respecting the original story’s characters and asking friends for feedback are good first steps."],
g:[["Canon","The official original story and its facts"],["Fandom","The community of fans of a book, show or other work"],["Alternate universe (AU)","A fan story set in a different world from the original"]],
f:["Fan fiction grew from Star Trek fanzines in the 1960s.","Archive of Our Own won a Hugo Award in 2019.","Canon means the original story."],
fun:"Some of the earliest fan fiction was written about Sherlock Holmes, after fans were upset when Arthur Conan Doyle killed off the detective in 1893."});

ADDART({id:"livestreaming",t:"Livestreaming",c:"Arts and Culture",i:"🔴",w:"Livestreaming",r:["streaming","esports","influencers","youtube","onlinesafety","videogames"],
p:[["Live on the internet","Livestreaming means broadcasting video online as it happens."],["Games, music and chat","People stream games, concerts, cooking, sports and conversations."],["Talking back","Viewers can chat with streamers in real time."]],
l:"Livestreaming is broadcasting live video over the internet so that people can watch as events happen. Unlike regular videos that are recorded and uploaded, a livestream lets viewers interact in real time, sending messages in a chat that the streamer can read and answer. People livestream video games, music, concerts, sports, classes, news events, cooking and everyday conversations. Platforms such as Twitch, YouTube, TikTok, Kick and Facebook host millions of streams. Popular streamers can earn money from subscriptions, donations, advertising and sponsors. Livestreaming also brings risks, such as sharing personal information by mistake or meeting harmful behavior in chats.",
s:[["Early streams","In 1991 computer scientists at the University of Cambridge pointed a camera at their coffee pot so they could see from their desks whether it was empty; from 1993 anyone online could watch. In 1994 the Rolling Stones broadcast part of a concert live over the internet. Faster connections in the 2000s made live video practical for everyone."],["The rise of game streaming","Justin.tv, launched in 2007, let anyone broadcast live. Its gaming section became so popular that it was relaunched as Twitch in 2011, and Amazon bought it in 2014. Watching people play games, compete in esports and chat became a new kind of entertainment."],["Interaction","What makes livestreaming different is interaction. Viewers chat, ask questions, send emojis and support streamers with paid subscriptions or donations. Some streams let viewers vote on what happens next. Moderators help remove spam and rude messages."],["Streaming safely","Streamers can accidentally reveal where they live through things seen or heard on camera. Safety advice includes hiding personal details, using a nickname, setting chat rules, using moderation tools and never streaming without a parent’s permission for younger users. Platforms have minimum age rules."]],
k:[["What it is","Broadcasting live video online"],["Popular platforms","Twitch, YouTube, TikTok, Kick, Facebook"],["Twitch launched","2011"],["Key feature","Real-time chat with viewers"],["Ways to earn","Subscriptions, donations, ads, sponsors"]],
d:[["1993","Cambridge’s coffee pot camera goes on the World Wide Web"],["1994","The Rolling Stones stream part of a concert online"],["2007","Justin.tv launches"],["2011","Twitch launches"],["2014","Amazon buys Twitch"]],
s2:[["More than games","Livestreaming is used by teachers for online classes, by museums and zoos for virtual tours and animal cams, by scientists for rocket launches and eclipses, and by musicians for concerts. During the COVID-19 pandemic, livestreams let people attend religious services, weddings and events from home."],["The pressure of streaming live","Successful streamers often broadcast for many hours a day to keep their audience, which can lead to stress and burnout. Some have spoken about the pressure to always be online and entertaining, and the importance of taking breaks."]],
sp:["Animal cams","Some of the most watched livestreams show animals, not people. Webcams broadcast eagles nesting, bears catching salmon at Katmai National Park in Alaska, giant pandas in zoos and wildlife at African watering holes, letting millions of viewers watch nature live from home."],
g:[["Livestream","A video broadcast live over the internet"],["Streamer","A person who broadcasts livestreams"],["Moderator","A person who manages a stream’s chat"]],
f:["The first webcam watched a coffee pot at Cambridge.","Twitch grew out of Justin.tv.","Livestreams let viewers chat in real time."],
fun:"The Cambridge coffee pot that inspired one of the first webcams was later sold at auction for about £3,350."});

ADDART({id:"tradingcards",t:"Trading Cards",c:"Arts and Culture",i:"🃏",w:"Trading card",r:["boardgames","anime","fandom","videogames","baseball","collect"],
p:[["Collect and trade","Trading cards are cards made to be collected, swapped and played."],["Sports to monsters","They feature athletes, game characters, films and more."],["Big business","Rare cards can sell for millions of dollars."]],
l:"Trading cards are small printed cards designed to be collected, traded and, in some cases, used to play games. The first popular trading cards showed baseball players and were packed with cigarettes in the United States in the 1880s. Sports cards remain popular, but the biggest sellers today are trading card games, such as Magic: The Gathering, launched in 1993, the Pokémon Trading Card Game, launched in Japan in 1996, and Yu-Gi-Oh!. Players build decks of cards with different powers and battle each other. Cards are sold in random packs, so collecting rare ones is part of the fun. More than 85 billion Pokémon cards had been produced by March 2026.",
s:[["Sports cards","Tobacco companies put picture cards of baseball players in cigarette packs in the late 1800s, partly to stiffen the packs. Later, gum and candy companies took over, and Topps sold baseball cards with bubble gum from the 1950s. Collectors still value cards of famous players, especially rookie cards from a player’s first year."],["Trading card games","In 1993 mathematician Richard Garfield created Magic: The Gathering, the first modern trading card game, in which players act as wizards casting spells. Its success inspired many others. In the Pokémon Trading Card Game, players use Pokémon cards and energy cards to battle, and Yu-Gi-Oh! grew from a manga and anime."],["Rarity and value","Cards are printed in different quantities: common, uncommon, rare and special versions with shiny holographic designs. Professional grading companies examine cards and give them a score for condition, sealing them in plastic cases. A perfect grade can multiply a card’s value."],["Record prices","In 2022 a 1952 Topps Mickey Mantle baseball card sold for about $12.6 million, a record for a sports card. A rare 1909–1911 Honus Wagner card sold for about $7.25 million the same year. Rare Pokémon cards have also sold for millions of dollars."]],
k:[["First popular cards","Baseball cards with tobacco, 1880s"],["First modern trading card game","Magic: The Gathering, 1993"],["Pokémon TCG","Launched in Japan, 1996"],["Pokémon cards produced","More than 85 billion (March 2026)"],["Record sports card sale","About $12.6 million (2022)"]],
d:[["1880s","Baseball cards are packed with cigarettes"],["1952","Topps releases a famous baseball card set"],["1993","Magic: The Gathering is released"],["1996","The Pokémon Trading Card Game launches in Japan"],["1999","The Yu-Gi-Oh! card game launches"]],
s2:[["Tournaments","Trading card games have competitive tournaments around the world, from local game shops to world championships with large prizes. Players study strategy, build decks carefully and adapt to new card sets that are released several times a year."],["Collecting wisely","High demand for some cards has led to shortages and resellers buying up products, which frustrates young collectors. Fake cards are also common. Collectors are advised to buy from trusted sellers, learn to spot fakes and collect mainly for enjoyment rather than profit."]],
sp:["Digital cards","Many trading card games now have digital versions, such as Magic: The Gathering Arena and Pokémon TCG Live, where players collect and battle online. Digital cards cannot be lost or damaged, but they also cannot be held, traded in person or resold in the same way as physical cards."],
g:[["Deck","The set of cards a player uses in a game"],["Booster pack","A sealed pack of random cards"],["Grading","Professionally rating a card’s condition"]],
f:["The first popular trading cards came with cigarettes.","Magic: The Gathering was the first modern trading card game.","A Mickey Mantle card sold for about $12.6 million."],
fun:"Early tobacco cards were partly added to cigarette packs to stiffen them, protecting the cigarettes from being crushed."});

;
/* data/modern46.js */
// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 16): space.
ADDART({id:"lunarlanders",t:"Commercial Moon Landers",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌖",w:"Commercial Lunar Payload Services",r:["moon","commspace","artemis","changeprogram","moonbases"],
p:[["Companies go to the Moon","Private companies are now building spacecraft to land on the Moon."],["Hard to land","Several early attempts crashed or tipped over."],["A perfect landing","Firefly’s Blue Ghost made the first fully successful commercial Moon landing in 2025."]],
l:"Commercial Moon landers are robotic spacecraft built by private companies to deliver scientific instruments and cargo to the lunar surface, often for NASA and other space agencies. In 2018, NASA started the Commercial Lunar Payload Services (CLPS) program, buying delivery services from companies rather than building its own landers, to prepare for Artemis astronaut missions. Landing on the Moon is difficult because it has no atmosphere for parachutes, so landers must use rocket engines and accurate navigation to touch down gently. Israel’s privately funded Beresheet crashed in April 2019, and Japan’s ispace crashed in April 2023 and again in June 2025. Astrobotic’s Peregrine suffered a fuel leak in January 2024 and never attempted a landing. On 22 February 2024, Intuitive Machines’ Odysseus became the first commercial spacecraft to land on the Moon and the first American lander since 1972, though it tipped onto its side. On 2 March 2025, Firefly Aerospace’s Blue Ghost landed upright in Mare Crisium and completed its full two-week mission, the first fully successful commercial landing. Days later, Intuitive Machines’ Athena also tipped over near the south pole. More landers from Firefly, Intuitive Machines, Astrobotic, Blue Origin and others are planned.",
s:[["Why use companies?","NASA hopes that buying rides, like buying cargo deliveries to the space station, will be cheaper and faster than building its own landers, and that companies will also find other customers, from universities to other countries."],["Why landings fail","Landers must measure their height and speed precisely in the final minutes. Odysseus came down with a disabled laser rangefinder, ispace’s first lander misjudged its altitude, and uneven ground can cause landers to tip over."],["Blue Ghost’s mission","Blue Ghost carried ten NASA instruments, drilled into the soil, tested a lunar dust-repelling system and recorded high-definition video of a total solar eclipse as Earth passed in front of the Sun, seen from the Moon, on 14 March 2025."],["Toward Artemis","Commercial landers scout the lunar south pole, where astronauts may land, studying water ice, radiation and the ground. Larger landers are being built to carry rovers and heavy cargo."]],
k:[["NASA program","Commercial Lunar Payload Services (2018)"],["First commercial landing","Odysseus, 22 February 2024"],["First fully successful commercial landing","Blue Ghost, 2 March 2025"],["Main challenge","Precise, gentle touchdown"],["Key target","Lunar south pole"]],
d:[["2018","NASA starts the CLPS program"],["2019","Beresheet crashes on the Moon"],["2023","ispace’s first lander crashes"],["2024","Odysseus lands on its side"],["2025","Blue Ghost lands successfully; Athena tips over"],["2025","ispace’s second lander crashes"]],
s2:[["Eclipse from the Moon","On 14 March 2025, Blue Ghost filmed Earth blocking the Sun, a solar eclipse seen from the Moon, while people on Earth saw a lunar eclipse at the same time."],["Lunar laws","Commercial landers carry items from around the world, from art to time capsules. The Artemis Accords and international discussions aim to set rules for activities on the Moon."]],
sp:["‘Odie’ the lander","Odysseus, nicknamed ‘Odie’, carried a camera system built by university students that was meant to photograph the lander as it descended, but it was not released during the descent as planned."],
g:[["Lander","A spacecraft that touches down on a surface"],["CLPS","NASA’s Commercial Lunar Payload Services program"],["Payload","Cargo carried by a spacecraft"],["Rangefinder","A device that measures distance"]],
f:["Odysseus was the first commercial Moon lander, in 2024.","Blue Ghost made the first fully successful commercial landing.","Several private landers have crashed."],
fun:"In 2025, a commercial lander filmed a solar eclipse from the Moon as Earth passed in front of the Sun."});

ADDART({id:"shuttledisasters",t:"The Challenger and Columbia Disasters",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🕯️",w:"Space Shuttle Columbia disaster",r:["spaceshuttle","spaceagencies","astronauts","womeninspace","iss"],
p:[["Two tragedies","Two Space Shuttles were lost with their crews, in 1986 and 2003."],["Challenger","Challenger broke apart 73 seconds after launch."],["Columbia","Columbia broke apart while returning to Earth."]],
l:"Two of NASA’s five Space Shuttle orbiters were destroyed in accidents that killed their seven-person crews. On 28 January 1986, the Space Shuttle Challenger broke apart 73 seconds after launch from Florida, watched live by millions, including schoolchildren following teacher Christa McAuliffe, who was to be the first teacher in space. An investigation by the Rogers Commission found that a rubber O-ring seal in one of the solid rocket boosters had failed in unusually cold weather, letting hot gas escape and burn through to the external fuel tank. Engineers had warned of the danger the night before, but managers approved the launch. Shuttle flights stopped for nearly three years. On 1 February 2003, the Space Shuttle Columbia broke apart over Texas as it returned from a 16-day science mission. During launch, a piece of insulating foam from the external tank had struck the left wing, damaging the heat shield; hot gases entered during reentry and destroyed the wing. The crew included Ilan Ramon, Israel’s first astronaut, and Indian-born Kalpana Chawla. Both investigations criticized NASA’s safety culture. The disasters led NASA to retire the shuttle in 2011 and design safer spacecraft with escape systems.",
s:[["Challenger’s crew","Challenger’s crew were Francis Scobee, Michael Smith, Judith Resnik, Ellison Onizuka, Ronald McNair, Gregory Jarvis and Christa McAuliffe. McAuliffe’s planned lessons from space were later taught by astronauts on the space station in 2018."],["Feynman’s demonstration","During a televised hearing, physicist Richard Feynman, a commission member, dipped a piece of O-ring material into a glass of ice water and showed that it lost its springiness in the cold, a simple demonstration of the cause."],["Columbia’s crew","Columbia’s crew were Rick Husband, William McCool, Michael Anderson, Kalpana Chawla, David Brown, Laurel Clark and Ilan Ramon. Debris fell across Texas and Louisiana and was gathered by thousands of searchers."],["Lessons","Investigators said NASA had become used to warning signs, such as earlier foam strikes and O-ring damage, treating them as normal. The term ‘normalization of deviance’ is now taught in engineering and management courses."]],
k:[["Challenger disaster","28 January 1986"],["Cause (Challenger)","Failed O-ring seal in cold weather"],["Columbia disaster","1 February 2003"],["Cause (Columbia)","Foam strike damaged the wing’s heat shield"],["Lives lost","14 astronauts"]],
d:[["1981","First Space Shuttle flight"],["1986","Challenger disaster"],["1988","Shuttle flights resume"],["2003","Columbia disaster"],["2005","Shuttle flights resume"],["2011","Space Shuttle retired"]],
s2:[["Memorials","The Space Mirror Memorial at Kennedy Space Center honors astronauts who died in the line of duty. Craters on the Moon and hills on Mars are named after the Challenger and Columbia crews."],["Kalpana Chawla","Kalpana Chawla, born in Karnal, India, was the first woman of Indian origin to fly in space, in 1997. Schools, scholarships and a satellite have been named in her honor."]],
sp:["Worms survived","Tiny worms called C. elegans, part of an experiment aboard Columbia, were found alive in their containers among the debris, an extraordinary survival story."],
g:[["O-ring","A rubber ring that seals a joint"],["Heat shield","Protection against the heat of reentry"],["Reentry","Returning into Earth’s atmosphere"],["Safety culture","An organization’s attitudes toward safety"]],
f:["Challenger was lost in 1986.","Columbia was lost in 2003.","Both disasters killed seven astronauts."],
fun:"Tiny worms from a science experiment aboard Columbia survived the disaster and were found alive in the debris."});

ADDART({id:"apollo8",t:"Apollo 8",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌍",w:"Apollo 8",r:["apollo","moon","saturnv","space","earthplanet"],
p:[["First humans around the Moon","Apollo 8 carried the first people to orbit the Moon in December 1968."],["Earthrise","The crew took the famous photograph of Earth rising over the Moon."],["Christmas Eve","They read from the Book of Genesis on live television."]],
l:"Apollo 8 was the first crewed spacecraft to leave Earth’s orbit, reach the Moon and orbit it. Launched on 21 December 1968 on the first crewed flight of the giant Saturn V rocket, it carried astronauts Frank Borman, Jim Lovell and William Anders. NASA had planned the mission to test the lunar module in Earth orbit, but because the lunar module was delayed and there were fears the Soviet Union might send cosmonauts around the Moon first, managers boldly changed it to a lunar mission with just four months’ notice. On 24 December, Apollo 8 entered orbit around the Moon, becoming the first time humans saw the Moon’s far side with their own eyes. During its fourth orbit, Anders photographed Earth rising above the lunar horizon, an image called Earthrise that became one of the most famous photographs ever taken and helped inspire the environmental movement. On Christmas Eve, the crew read the first verses of the Book of Genesis during a television broadcast watched by an estimated one billion people. After ten orbits, they fired their engine to return home, splashing down in the Pacific on 27 December. Apollo 8 paved the way for the Apollo 11 landing seven months later.",
s:[["A bold decision","Sending Apollo 8 to the Moon was a big risk: the Saturn V had flown only twice without crew, and the second flight had problems. Success proved that the spacecraft and navigation could carry humans to the Moon and back."],["Earthrise","Anders took the photo with a handheld camera after the crew suddenly saw Earth appearing over the Moon’s edge. The image of a small blue planet in the blackness of space changed how many people thought about Earth."],["Firsts","The Apollo 8 astronauts were the first humans to travel beyond low Earth orbit, to experience the Moon’s gravity, to see the whole Earth as a sphere and to see the far side of the Moon."],["‘The best Christmas present’","After the mission, a telegram to Borman reportedly said ‘Thank you Apollo 8. You saved 1968’, a year marked by war, assassinations and unrest. The crew were named Time magazine’s Men of the Year."]],
k:[["Launched","21 December 1968"],["Crew","Frank Borman, Jim Lovell, William Anders"],["Lunar orbits","10"],["Famous photo","Earthrise, 24 December 1968"],["Splashdown","27 December 1968"]],
d:[["1968","Apollo 8 launched on the first crewed Saturn V"],["1968","Enters orbit around the Moon on 24 December"],["1968","Earthrise photographed; Genesis reading broadcast"],["1968","Splashes down in the Pacific"],["1969","Apollo 11 lands on the Moon"]],
s2:[["Jim Lovell","Lovell later commanded Apollo 13, which suffered an explosion on the way to the Moon in 1970, so he traveled to the Moon twice without ever landing on it."],["Earthrise stamp","The Earthrise photograph appeared on a US postage stamp in 1969, and the first Earth Day in 1970 was inspired partly by images of Earth from space."]],
sp:["Santa Claus","When Apollo 8 emerged from behind the Moon after its engine burn to head home on Christmas Day, Jim Lovell radioed, ‘Please be informed, there is a Santa Claus.’"],
g:[["Lunar orbit","Circling the Moon"],["Far side","The side of the Moon that always faces away from Earth"],["Splashdown","Landing a spacecraft in the ocean"],["Earthrise","Earth appearing over the Moon’s horizon"]],
f:["Apollo 8 was the first crewed flight to the Moon.","Its crew took the Earthrise photo.","It orbited the Moon ten times."],
fun:"On Christmas Day 1968, Jim Lovell told Mission Control from lunar orbit, ‘Please be informed, there is a Santa Claus.’"});

ADDART({id:"rangersurveyor",t:"Ranger, Surveyor and Lunar Orbiter",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌕",w:"Surveyor program",r:["moon","apollo","lunaprogram","lro","space"],
p:[["Scouting the Moon","Robot spacecraft prepared the way for Apollo astronauts."],["Crash and photograph","Ranger probes sent close-up pictures before crashing."],["Soft landings","Surveyor landers showed the Moon’s surface could support a spacecraft."]],
l:"Before astronauts could land on the Moon, NASA sent three series of robotic spacecraft to study it. The Ranger program (1961–1965) aimed spacecraft straight at the Moon, taking pictures until they crashed. After six failures, Ranger 7 succeeded on 31 July 1964, sending back more than 4,300 images showing craters as small as a meter across; Ranger 9’s pictures in March 1965 were broadcast live on television. Lunar Orbiter spacecraft (1966–1967) circled the Moon and mapped about 99 percent of its surface, helping choose Apollo landing sites; Lunar Orbiter 1 also took the first picture of Earth from the Moon’s vicinity. The Surveyor program (1966–1968) made soft landings to test the surface. Surveyor 1 landed on 2 June 1966, a few months after the Soviet Luna 9 made the first soft landing, and showed that the ground was firm enough to support a lander, ending fears that spacecraft might sink into deep dust. Five of the seven Surveyors succeeded, testing soil with scoops and chemical analyzers. In November 1969, Apollo 12 astronauts landed near Surveyor 3 and brought parts of it back to Earth, allowing scientists to study how materials aged on the Moon.",
s:[["Ranger’s struggles","The first six Ranger missions failed because of rocket, camera and electronics problems, nearly ending the program. Ranger 7’s success restored confidence and gave the first close-up views of the Moon."],["Lunar Orbiter","The Lunar Orbiter cameras used film that was developed on board, scanned and transmitted to Earth. In 2008, a project recovered and restored the original data, producing much sharper versions of the 1966 pictures."],["Surveyor","Surveyor landers carried television cameras that sent back thousands of images. Some had robotic scoops that dug trenches, and later ones measured the chemistry of the soil."],["Surveyor 3 and Apollo 12","Apollo 12 landed within about 200 meters of Surveyor 3. Astronauts Pete Conrad and Alan Bean walked to it and removed its camera and other parts, now displayed in museums."]],
k:[["Ranger program","1961–1965"],["First successful Ranger","Ranger 7, 31 July 1964"],["Lunar Orbiter program","1966–1967"],["Surveyor 1 landing","2 June 1966"],["Successful Surveyors","5 of 7"]],
d:[["1964","Ranger 7 sends close-up images before impact"],["1965","Ranger 9 images broadcast live"],["1966","Surveyor 1 soft-lands on the Moon"],["1966","Lunar Orbiter 1 photographs Earth from near the Moon"],["1967","Surveyor 3 lands"],["1969","Apollo 12 visits Surveyor 3"]],
s2:[["The germ controversy","Scientists reported finding bacteria inside Surveyor 3’s camera after its return, suggesting microbes had survived on the Moon. Later studies concluded the bacteria probably came from contamination after the camera returned to Earth."],["Luna 9","The Soviet Luna 9 made the first soft landing on the Moon on 3 February 1966 and sent the first photographs from the lunar surface."]],
sp:["Live from the Moon","Ranger 9’s final pictures as it plunged into the crater Alphonsus in 1965 were shown live on US television with the caption ‘Live from the Moon’."],
g:[["Soft landing","Touching down gently"],["Impactor","A spacecraft designed to crash into a target"],["Regolith","The loose dust and rock on the Moon’s surface"],["Landing site","A chosen place to touch down"]],
f:["Ranger 7 sent the first close-up Moon images in 1964.","Surveyor 1 soft-landed in 1966.","Apollo 12 astronauts visited Surveyor 3."],
fun:"Apollo 12 astronauts landed next to the robotic Surveyor 3 and took parts of it home."});

ADDART({id:"lunokhod",t:"The Lunokhod Moon Rovers",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛞",w:"Lunokhod programme",r:["lunaprogram","moon","marsrovers","lro","space"],
p:[["First rovers on another world","Soviet Lunokhod rovers explored the Moon in the 1970s."],["Driven from Earth","Teams on Earth steered them by remote control."],["Still useful","Their reflectors are still used to measure the distance to the Moon."]],
l:"Lunokhod (meaning ‘Moonwalker’ in Russian) was a series of Soviet robotic rovers that explored the Moon, the first remote-controlled vehicles to travel on another world. Lunokhod 1 was carried by the Luna 17 lander, which touched down in the Sea of Rains on 17 November 1970. Looking like a bathtub on eight wheels, with a lid covered in solar cells, the rover was driven by a team of five operators in the Soviet Union, who watched slow television images and dealt with a delay of several seconds. Lunokhod 1 worked for about ten months, far longer than planned, traveled about 10.5 kilometers, took more than 20,000 pictures and tested the soil. Lunokhod 2, landed by Luna 21 in January 1973, traveled about 39 kilometers in four months, a record for distance on another world that stood until NASA’s Opportunity rover passed it on Mars in 2014. Both rovers carried French-made laser reflectors. After Lunokhod 1’s location was lost for decades, NASA’s Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter photographed it in 2010, and scientists bounced lasers off its reflector, which still helps measure the Moon’s distance precisely.",
s:[["How it worked","Lunokhod’s lid opened during the day to collect sunlight with solar cells and closed at night to keep in heat. A small radioactive heater kept its electronics warm during the two-week lunar nights, when temperatures fall below −150°C."],["Driving on the Moon","Operators sat at consoles in Crimea, steering the rover in short moves. Because the TV images arrived slowly, they had to anticipate obstacles, a difficult and tiring job. The team was kept secret for years."],["Lunokhod 2’s end","Lunokhod 2 ended its mission in May 1973, apparently after it brushed against a crater wall and soil covered its radiator, causing it to overheat."],["A private owner","In 1993, Lunokhod 2 and the Luna 21 lander were sold at a Sotheby’s auction to American video game developer Richard Garriott, who became the owner of an object on the Moon."]],
k:[["First rover on another world","Lunokhod 1, landed 17 November 1970"],["Lunokhod 1 distance","About 10.5 km"],["Lunokhod 2 distance","About 39 km"],["Control","Remote control from Earth"],["Still in use","Laser reflectors"]],
d:[["1970","Lunokhod 1 lands on the Moon"],["1971","Lunokhod 1 mission ends"],["1973","Lunokhod 2 lands and travels about 39 km"],["1993","Lunokhod 2 sold at auction"],["2010","LRO finds Lunokhod 1"],["2014","Opportunity breaks Lunokhod 2’s distance record"]],
s2:[["Chinese and Indian rovers","After Lunokhod, no rover drove on the Moon until China’s Yutu in 2013. China’s Yutu-2 became the first rover on the Moon’s far side in 2019, and India’s Pragyan rover explored near the south pole in 2023."],["Lunar Roving Vehicle","Unlike the robotic Lunokhods, the Lunar Roving Vehicles of Apollo 15, 16 and 17 were driven by astronauts sitting in them."]],
sp:["A rover’s chair","Lunokhod operators worked in teams, with a commander, driver, navigator, engineer and antenna operator, like the crew of a ship."],
g:[["Rover","A vehicle that travels over a surface"],["Remote control","Operating a machine from a distance"],["Laser reflector","A device that bounces laser light back"],["Lunar night","The two-week dark period on the Moon"]],
f:["Lunokhod 1 was the first rover on another world.","Lunokhod 2 traveled about 39 kilometers.","Their laser reflectors are still used."],
fun:"An American video game developer bought Lunokhod 2 at auction in 1993, so he owns a robot on the Moon."});

ADDART({id:"marslanders",t:"Mars Landers",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔴",w:"Phoenix (spacecraft)",r:["mars","marsrovers","vikinglanders","insight","marsorbiters"],
p:[["Touching down on Mars","Landers are spacecraft that set down on Mars’s surface."],["Bouncing airbags","Mars Pathfinder landed in 1997 by bouncing on airbags."],["Ice at the pole","Phoenix dug up water ice near Mars’s north pole in 2008."]],
l:"Mars landers are spacecraft that descend through the Martian atmosphere and touch down on its surface to study the planet up close. Landing on Mars is notoriously hard: the atmosphere is thick enough to heat a spacecraft but too thin for parachutes alone to slow it, so landers combine heat shields, parachutes and rockets or airbags in about seven minutes of descent. The Soviet Mars 3 made the first soft landing on 2 December 1971 but stopped transmitting after about 20 seconds. NASA’s Viking 1 and 2 landed successfully in 1976 and worked for years. On 4 July 1997, Mars Pathfinder bounced to a stop wrapped in airbags and released Sojourner, the first rover on Mars. Several missions failed, including NASA’s Mars Polar Lander in 1999, Britain’s Beagle 2 in 2003 and Europe’s Schiaparelli in 2016. On 25 May 2008, NASA’s Phoenix landed in the Martian Arctic and used its robotic arm to dig up water ice and discover perchlorate salts in the soil. Later landers include InSight (2018), which listened for marsquakes, and the rover-carrying landers of Curiosity, Perseverance and China’s Zhurong. About half of all attempts to land on Mars have failed.",
s:[["Seven minutes of terror","Engineers call a Mars landing ‘seven minutes of terror’ because the spacecraft must slow from about 20,000 kilometers per hour to a safe landing on its own; radio signals take several minutes to reach Earth, so controllers cannot intervene."],["Pathfinder’s airbags","Pathfinder used a cluster of airbags that inflated around it, bounced up to about 15 meters high and rolled before stopping. The same method landed the Spirit and Opportunity rovers in 2004."],["Phoenix","Phoenix scraped soil and watched white chunks of ice vanish over a few days as they turned directly into gas. It also saw snow falling from clouds, though it evaporated before reaching the ground."],["Beagle 2","Beagle 2, carried by Europe’s Mars Express, disappeared during landing on Christmas Day 2003. In 2015, the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter found it on the surface, partly unfolded, showing it had landed but its solar panels had not fully opened."]],
k:[["First soft landing","Mars 3, 2 December 1971"],["First successful long-term landers","Viking 1 and 2, 1976"],["First rover","Sojourner, 1997"],["Phoenix landing","25 May 2008"],["Failure rate","About half of attempts"]],
d:[["1971","Mars 3 makes the first soft landing"],["1976","Viking 1 and 2 land"],["1997","Mars Pathfinder lands with Sojourner"],["1999","Mars Polar Lander lost"],["2003","Beagle 2 lost during landing"],["2008","Phoenix lands near the north pole"],["2016","Schiaparelli crashes"]],
s2:[["Perchlorates","Phoenix discovered perchlorate salts, which are toxic to humans but could lower the freezing point of water, allowing liquid brines to exist briefly. Some microbes on Earth can use perchlorate for energy."],["Sky crane","For heavier rovers, NASA developed the sky crane, a rocket-powered platform that lowers the rover on cables and then flies away, used for Curiosity in 2012 and Perseverance in 2021."]],
sp:["A DVD on Mars","Phoenix carried a DVD called ‘Visions of Mars’ with stories, art and the names of a quarter of a million people, intended as a library for future human explorers."],
g:[["Lander","A spacecraft that touches down on a surface"],["Heat shield","Protection against heat during atmospheric entry"],["Perchlorate","A salt containing chlorine and oxygen"],["Sky crane","A system that lowers a rover on cables"]],
f:["Mars 3 made the first soft landing in 1971.","Pathfinder bounced to a stop on airbags.","Phoenix dug up water ice in 2008."],
fun:"Mars Pathfinder bounced on airbags about 15 meters high before rolling to a stop on Mars."});

ADDART({id:"dawnmission",t:"The Dawn Mission",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌄",w:"Dawn (spacecraft)",r:["ceres","vesta","asteroidbelt","rocketengines","dwarfplanets"],
p:[["Two worlds in one mission","Dawn orbited the giant asteroid Vesta and the dwarf planet Ceres."],["Ion engines","It used gentle but efficient ion engines."],["Bright spots","It solved the mystery of Ceres’s bright spots."]],
l:"Dawn was a NASA spacecraft that explored the two largest objects in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter: the giant asteroid Vesta and the dwarf planet Ceres. Launched on 27 September 2007, it became the first spacecraft to orbit two different bodies beyond Earth and the first to orbit a dwarf planet. Dawn used ion engines, which accelerate charged xenon atoms with electricity from large solar panels. Their push was tiny, about the weight of a sheet of paper resting in your hand, but they could run for years, giving Dawn a total change in speed greater than any earlier spacecraft’s own engines. Dawn orbited Vesta from July 2011 to September 2012, revealing a world with a huge impact basin at its south pole and a mountain more than twice as tall as Mount Everest. It then traveled to Ceres, arriving on 6 March 2015. Ceres turned out to have bright spots in Occator crater, which Dawn found were deposits of salts, mainly sodium carbonate, left by salty water rising from below, suggesting Ceres may still have brine underground. Dawn ran out of fuel for steering in November 2018 and remains in orbit around Ceres.",
s:[["Ion propulsion","Chemical rockets give a big push for a few minutes, while ion engines give a gentle push for months. Dawn’s engines took four days to accelerate from 0 to 100 kilometers per hour, but over years they changed its speed by about 11 kilometers per second."],["Vesta","Vesta’s southern Rheasilvia basin is about 500 kilometers wide, with a central peak about 22 kilometers high. The impact that made it threw out rocks, some of which fell to Earth as meteorites, linking Vesta to about 6 percent of meteorites found on Earth."],["Ceres","Ceres, about 940 kilometers across, contains much water ice and clay minerals. Ahuna Mons, a lonely mountain about 4 kilometers tall, appears to be an ice volcano that erupted salty mud."],["The end","Dawn’s mission ended on 1 November 2018 when it ran out of hydrazine for pointing. It was left in a stable orbit to avoid crashing onto Ceres, which could contaminate a world that might hold clues to life’s chemistry."]],
k:[["Launched","27 September 2007"],["Vesta orbit","July 2011–September 2012"],["Ceres arrival","6 March 2015"],["Propulsion","Ion engines using xenon"],["Mission ended","1 November 2018"]],
d:[["2007","Dawn launched"],["2009","Mars gravity assist"],["2011","Enters orbit around Vesta"],["2012","Departs Vesta"],["2015","Enters orbit around Ceres"],["2018","Mission ends"]],
s2:[["Why ‘Dawn’?","The mission was named Dawn because Vesta and Ceres are relics from the dawn of the solar system, preserving information about how planets formed."],["Ceres in history","Ceres was discovered in 1801 by Giuseppe Piazzi and was first called a planet, then an asteroid, and since 2006 a dwarf planet."]],
sp:["Gentle push","Dawn’s ion engine pushed with about the same force as the weight of a single sheet of paper, yet it carried the spacecraft between worlds."],
g:[["Ion engine","An engine that accelerates charged atoms with electricity"],["Xenon","A heavy gas used as ion engine fuel"],["Dwarf planet","A small planet-like body"],["Cryovolcano","An ice volcano"]],
f:["Dawn orbited Vesta and Ceres.","It used ion engines.","Ceres’s bright spots are salt deposits."],
fun:"Dawn’s ion engine pushed only as hard as the weight of a sheet of paper, but it ran for years."});

ADDART({id:"ulysses",t:"Ulysses: Over the Sun’s Poles",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☀️",w:"Ulysses (spacecraft)",r:["sun","solarwind","jupiter","solarobservatories","spaceweather"],
p:[["Flying over the Sun","Ulysses was the first spacecraft to fly over the Sun’s poles."],["A boost from Jupiter","It used Jupiter’s gravity to swing out of the planets’ plane."],["Studying the solar wind","It mapped the solar wind in three dimensions."]],
l:"Ulysses was a joint mission of the European Space Agency and NASA that became the first spacecraft to study the Sun from high above and below its poles. Most spacecraft travel close to the ecliptic, the flat plane in which the planets orbit, so the Sun’s polar regions had never been observed from overhead. Leaving that plane takes enormous energy, so after its launch from the Space Shuttle Discovery on 6 October 1990, Ulysses first flew to Jupiter. In February 1992, Jupiter’s gravity flung it into a steeply tilted orbit around the Sun, passing over the Sun’s poles. Ulysses flew over the south pole in 1994 and the north pole in 1995, and repeated the passes in 2000–2001 and 2007–2008, observing the Sun at both quiet and active times in its 11-year cycle. It found that fast solar wind streams from the poles and that the Sun’s magnetic field reaches into space differently than expected. It also measured interstellar dust and gas flowing into the solar system and unexpectedly flew through the tails of several comets. Planned for five years, Ulysses operated for more than 18 years until 30 June 2009, when its power supply weakened.",
s:[["Why the poles?","The Sun’s magnetic field and solar wind behave differently at its poles than near its equator. Ulysses carried instruments to measure particles and magnetic fields rather than cameras, sampling the space around the Sun directly."],["Comet tails","In 1996, Ulysses passed through the ion tail of Comet Hyakutake, more than 500 million kilometers from the comet’s head, showing that comet tails are far longer than previously known."],["Solar cycle","Ulysses saw that during solar minimum, fast wind flows steadily from the poles, but during solar maximum, the wind is mixed and chaotic at all latitudes."],["Powered by plutonium","Ulysses was too far from the Sun at times for solar panels, so it used a radioisotope generator. As the generator weakened, its fuel lines began to freeze, ending the mission."]],
k:[["Agencies","ESA and NASA"],["Launched","6 October 1990"],["Jupiter gravity assist","February 1992"],["First polar pass","Sun’s south pole, 1994"],["Mission ended","30 June 2009"]],
d:[["1990","Ulysses launched from the Space Shuttle"],["1992","Jupiter flyby sends it out of the ecliptic"],["1994","First pass over the Sun’s south pole"],["1995","First pass over the Sun’s north pole"],["1996","Flies through Comet Hyakutake’s tail"],["2009","Mission ends"]],
s2:[["The name","Ulysses is named after the Latin name of Odysseus, the hero of Homer’s Odyssey, and the character in Dante’s Inferno who sails into unknown regions."],["Solar Orbiter","ESA’s Solar Orbiter, launched in 2020, carries cameras and is gradually tilting its orbit to photograph the Sun’s poles, building on Ulysses’s discoveries."]],
sp:["Long-distance flight","To reach the Sun’s poles, Ulysses traveled out to Jupiter, more than five times Earth’s distance from the Sun, showing that sometimes the best route to the Sun is to fly away from it first."],
g:[["Ecliptic","The plane in which the planets orbit"],["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change a spacecraft’s path"],["Solar cycle","The Sun’s 11-year activity cycle"],["Solar wind","Charged particles flowing from the Sun"]],
f:["Ulysses was the first to fly over the Sun’s poles.","It used Jupiter’s gravity.","It operated for more than 18 years."],
fun:"To fly over the Sun’s poles, Ulysses first had to travel all the way out to Jupiter."});

ADDART({id:"fermitelescope",t:"Gamma-Ray Space Telescopes",c:"Space Exploration",i:"⚡",w:"Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope",r:["gammaraybursts","bh","pulsar","gravwaves","spitzer"],
p:[["The most energetic light","Gamma rays are light with the highest energy."],["Compton and Fermi","NASA’s Compton and Fermi observatories mapped the gamma-ray sky."],["Giant bubbles","Fermi discovered huge gamma-ray bubbles above and below our galaxy."]],
l:"Gamma rays are the most energetic form of light, produced by the most violent events in the universe, such as exploding stars, black holes devouring matter and colliding neutron stars. Earth’s atmosphere blocks gamma rays, so they must be observed from space. NASA’s Compton Gamma Ray Observatory, launched by the Space Shuttle Atlantis on 5 April 1991, was one of the four Great Observatories and, at about 17 metric tons, among the heaviest science satellites ever flown. It showed that gamma-ray bursts come from all directions in the sky, evidence that they originate in distant galaxies. Compton was deliberately brought down into the Pacific Ocean in June 2000. NASA’s Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope, launched on 11 June 2008 and named after physicist Enrico Fermi, scans the entire sky every three hours. In 2010, Fermi scientists discovered two enormous bubbles of gamma rays extending tens of thousands of light-years above and below the center of the Milky Way, probably blown out by past activity of its central black hole. On 17 August 2017, Fermi detected a burst of gamma rays two seconds after gravitational wave detectors recorded colliding neutron stars, a landmark in multi-messenger astronomy.",
s:[["Detecting gamma rays","Gamma rays cannot be focused with mirrors like visible light. Fermi’s main instrument detects them when they turn into pairs of electrons and positrons inside layers of tungsten, tracking the particles to find each gamma ray’s direction and energy."],["Gamma-ray bursts","Gamma-ray bursts are brief flashes, lasting from milliseconds to minutes, that can outshine whole galaxies. Long bursts come from collapsing massive stars, and short bursts from merging neutron stars. Fermi detects about 240 bursts a year."],["Thunderstorms","Fermi has detected terrestrial gamma-ray flashes from thunderstorms on Earth and even beams of antimatter produced by lightning, a surprising discovery."],["Swift","NASA’s Swift observatory, launched in 2004, quickly swings around to observe gamma-ray bursts with X-ray and ultraviolet telescopes, helping pinpoint their locations within minutes."]],
k:[["Compton launched","5 April 1991"],["Compton mass","About 17 metric tons"],["Fermi launched","11 June 2008"],["Fermi bubbles discovered","2010"],["Neutron star merger burst","17 August 2017"]],
d:[["1967","Spy satellites detect the first gamma-ray bursts"],["1991","Compton Gamma Ray Observatory launched"],["2000","Compton deorbited"],["2004","Swift launched"],["2008","Fermi launched"],["2010","Fermi bubbles discovered"],["2017","Gamma rays detected from merging neutron stars"]],
s2:[["Spy satellite discovery","Gamma-ray bursts were discovered in 1967 by US Vela satellites built to watch for secret nuclear weapons tests. The flashes came from space, not bombs, and the discovery was kept secret until 1973."],["Enrico Fermi","Enrico Fermi, an Italian-American physicist, built the first nuclear reactor in 1942 and proposed how cosmic particles could be accelerated to high energies, a process now named after him."]],
sp:["Antimatter from lightning","Fermi found that thunderstorms can produce beams of positrons, the antimatter version of electrons, which shoot into space along Earth’s magnetic field."],
g:[["Gamma ray","The highest-energy form of light"],["Gamma-ray burst","A brief, intense flash of gamma rays"],["Multi-messenger astronomy","Studying events with light and other signals"],["Positron","The antimatter version of an electron"]],
f:["Gamma rays are the most energetic light.","Fermi scans the whole sky every three hours.","Fermi discovered giant bubbles around our galaxy."],
fun:"Gamma-ray bursts were discovered by spy satellites looking for secret nuclear bomb tests."});

ADDART({id:"fastradiobursts",t:"Fast Radio Bursts",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"📻",w:"Fast radio burst",r:["radioastronomy","magnetars","pulsar","seti","galaxies"],
p:[["Mysterious flashes","Fast radio bursts are brief, powerful flashes of radio waves from space."],["Over in milliseconds","Each lasts only a few thousandths of a second."],["Magnetars","At least some come from highly magnetic dead stars."]],
l:"Fast radio bursts (FRBs) are extremely brief and powerful flashes of radio waves from distant space, each lasting only milliseconds yet releasing as much energy as the Sun does over days or even years. The first was found in 2007 by astronomers Duncan Lorimer and his student David Narkevic, who discovered a strange signal in archived data recorded in 2001 by the Parkes radio telescope in Australia, now called the Lorimer burst. At first some astronomers doubted FRBs were real, especially after other strange signals at Parkes, called perytons, turned out to come from staff opening a microwave oven door too early. However, more bursts were found, and in 2016 astronomers discovered one source that repeats, allowing them to trace it to a small galaxy about 3 billion light-years away. Most FRBs come from far beyond the Milky Way. On 28 April 2020, telescopes detected an FRB-like burst from a magnetar, a highly magnetic neutron star, in our own galaxy, showing that magnetars can produce them. Canada’s CHIME radio telescope now detects several bursts a day, and thousands are known. Astronomers also use FRBs to measure the gas between galaxies, helping find ‘missing’ ordinary matter.",
s:[["How they are found","Radio telescopes search for short pulses whose lower frequencies arrive slightly later than higher frequencies, a delay caused by traveling through clouds of electrons in space. The bigger the delay, the farther the burst has traveled."],["Repeaters","Most FRBs have been seen only once, but some repeat, sometimes in irregular bursts and sometimes in regular cycles. One repeater flares in a 16-day pattern, possibly because its source orbits a companion star."],["Perytons","In 2015, astronomers discovered that signals called perytons, which had puzzled them for years, came from a microwave oven at the Parkes observatory when someone opened its door before it finished heating."],["Not aliens","Because FRBs are bright and mysterious, people have asked whether they could be signals from aliens. Astronomers think natural explanations, especially magnetars, are far more likely."]],
k:[["First discovered","2007 (in 2001 data)"],["Duration","Milliseconds"],["First repeating source","Found 2016"],["Galactic burst from a magnetar","28 April 2020"],["Main detector","CHIME telescope, Canada"]],
d:[["2001","Parkes records the first known burst"],["2007","Lorimer burst discovered"],["2015","Perytons traced to a microwave oven"],["2016","First repeating FRB identified"],["2018","CHIME telescope begins detecting bursts"],["2020","FRB-like burst from a magnetar in the Milky Way"]],
s2:[["CHIME","The Canadian Hydrogen Intensity Mapping Experiment (CHIME), in British Columbia, looks like four giant half-pipes and has no moving parts, scanning the sky as Earth rotates. It has found thousands of FRBs."],["Missing matter","The delays in FRB signals revealed that much of the universe’s ordinary matter is spread thinly between galaxies, solving a long-standing puzzle about where it was hiding."]],
sp:["A microwave mystery","For years, astronomers puzzled over radio signals at Parkes that turned out to come from impatient staff opening a microwave oven at lunchtime."],
g:[["Fast radio burst","A millisecond flash of radio waves from space"],["Magnetar","A neutron star with an extremely strong magnetic field"],["Repeater","An FRB source that flashes more than once"],["Dispersion","Spreading of a signal by frequency"]],
f:["The first FRB was discovered in 2007.","FRBs last only milliseconds.","Some FRBs come from magnetars."],
fun:"Some strange radio signals once thought to be from space turned out to come from a microwave oven at the observatory."});

ADDART({id:"verarubin",t:"The Vera C. Rubin Observatory",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🎥",w:"Vera C. Rubin Observatory",r:["groundtelescopes","darkmatter","planetnine","chilecountry","asteroidbelt"],
p:[["A movie of the sky","Rubin Observatory photographs the entire southern sky every few nights."],["The biggest camera","It has the largest digital camera ever built for astronomy."],["Survey begins","Its 10-year survey began in 2025–2026."]],
l:"The Vera C. Rubin Observatory is an astronomical observatory on Cerro Pachón, a mountain in the Andes of northern Chile, designed to make a 10-year time-lapse movie of the southern sky called the Legacy Survey of Space and Time (LSST). Funded by the US National Science Foundation and Department of Energy, it is named after American astronomer Vera Rubin, whose studies of how galaxies rotate provided key evidence for dark matter. Its 8.4-meter telescope can swing quickly across the sky, and its camera, with 3,200 megapixels, is the largest digital camera ever built for astronomy; each image covers an area of sky about 45 times that of the full Moon. Rubin will photograph the whole visible sky every few nights, detecting anything that moves or changes, such as asteroids, comets, exploding stars and variable stars, and sending millions of alerts to astronomers each night. Its first images, released on 23 June 2025, showed glowing nebulae and millions of galaxies, and included more than 2,000 newly discovered asteroids. The 10-year survey officially began in mid-2026. Rubin is expected to catalog about 20 billion galaxies, find millions of asteroids and help study dark energy and dark matter, and might even find a distant ‘Planet Nine’.",
s:[["The camera","The LSST Camera is about the size of a small car and weighs about 3 metric tons. Its detectors are cooled to about −100°C. A single image would need hundreds of ultra-high-definition televisions to display at full resolution."],["Data","Rubin will produce about 20 terabytes of data every night. Computers compare each new image with earlier ones and send alerts within about a minute of spotting changes."],["Protecting Earth","Rubin is expected to discover most near-Earth asteroids larger than 140 meters, helping planetary defense experts identify any that might threaten Earth."],["Satellite streaks","Growing numbers of bright satellites in low orbit leave streaks across telescope images. Rubin’s wide, deep images are especially affected, and astronomers are working with satellite companies to reduce brightness."]],
k:[["Location","Cerro Pachón, Chile"],["Telescope mirror","8.4 meters"],["Camera","3,200 megapixels"],["First images","23 June 2025"],["Survey length","10 years"]],
d:[["1970s","Vera Rubin’s galaxy rotation studies point to dark matter"],["2015","Construction begins"],["2019","Observatory renamed for Vera Rubin"],["2025","LSST Camera installed on the telescope; first images released"],["2026","10-year survey officially begins"]],
s2:[["Vera Rubin","Vera Rubin (1928–2016) faced discrimination as a woman in astronomy; Princeton would not admit women to its graduate astronomy program when she applied. Her work on galaxy rotation became some of the strongest evidence for dark matter."],["Simonyi Survey Telescope","The telescope is named after software pioneer Charles Simonyi, whose family foundation helped fund it. Its primary and tertiary mirrors were cast as a single piece of glass."]],
sp:["Asteroid haul","In just ten hours of test observations, Rubin discovered more than 2,000 new asteroids, a taste of the millions it is expected to find."],
g:[["Survey","A systematic observation of a large area"],["Megapixel","One million pixels"],["Alert","A notice sent when something changes in the sky"],["Near-Earth asteroid","An asteroid whose orbit comes close to Earth’s"]],
f:["Rubin has the largest astronomy camera ever built.","Its first images were released in June 2025.","It will survey the sky for 10 years."],
fun:"Rubin found more than 2,000 new asteroids in its first ten hours of test observations."});

ADDART({id:"alma",t:"ALMA: The Atacama Large Millimeter Array",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"📡",w:"Atacama Large Millimeter Array",r:["radioastronomy","starformation","eht","chilecountry","solarform"],
p:[["Sixty-six dishes in the desert","ALMA is a radio telescope made of 66 antennas in Chile."],["High and dry","It sits at 5,000 meters, one of the driest places on Earth."],["Baby planets","It has photographed planets forming around young stars."]],
l:"The Atacama Large Millimeter/submillimeter Array (ALMA) is a powerful radio telescope made up of 66 high-precision antennas on the Chajnantor plateau in Chile’s Atacama Desert, at an altitude of about 5,000 meters. It observes light with wavelengths of about a millimeter, between infrared and radio waves, which comes from some of the coldest objects in the universe, such as clouds of gas and dust where stars and planets form, and from very distant galaxies. Water vapor in the air absorbs this light, which is why ALMA was built in one of the highest and driest places on Earth. Its antennas, 12 or 7 meters across, can be moved by giant transporter vehicles to spread them up to 16 kilometers apart, working together as one huge telescope through interferometry. ALMA is an international partnership of Europe, North America and East Asia with Chile, and was inaugurated on 13 March 2013. In 2014, it produced a stunning image of HL Tauri, a young star surrounded by a disk with gaps likely carved by forming planets. ALMA has also detected complex organic molecules around young stars, studied the earliest galaxies and served as a key part of the Event Horizon Telescope that imaged black holes.",
s:[["Working at altitude","At 5,000 meters, the air has about half the oxygen of sea level. Most staff work at a base camp at 2,900 meters and control the telescope remotely; those who go up carry oxygen and undergo health checks."],["Moving antennas","Two giant transporters, named Otto and Lore, each with 28 tires, carry the 100-ton antennas between pads, changing the array’s layout to zoom in or see wider areas."],["Planet formation","ALMA’s images of disks around young stars show rings, gaps and spirals, giving astronomers their first detailed views of solar systems in the making."],["The early universe","ALMA detects light from galaxies that existed less than a billion years after the Big Bang, revealing dust and gas in some of the first galaxies."]],
k:[["Antennas","66"],["Altitude","About 5,000 meters"],["Inaugurated","13 March 2013"],["Maximum spread","About 16 kilometers"],["Famous image","HL Tauri disk (2014)"]],
d:[["2003","Construction begins"],["2011","Early science observations start"],["2013","ALMA inaugurated"],["2014","HL Tauri image released"],["2017","Takes part in Event Horizon Telescope observations"]],
s2:[["Chajnantor","Chajnantor means ‘place of departure’ in the Kunza language of the local Atacameño people. The plateau also hosts other telescopes because of its dry, clear skies."],["Mars on Earth","The Atacama Desert is so dry that some parts have gone years without rain, and NASA has tested Mars rovers there."]],
sp:["Giant trucks","Each of ALMA’s antenna transporters is about 20 meters long and 10 meters wide and rolls on 28 tires."],
g:[["Interferometry","Combining signals from several antennas"],["Millimeter wave","Light with wavelengths around a millimeter"],["Protoplanetary disk","A disk of gas and dust where planets form"],["Plateau","A high, flat area of land"]],
f:["ALMA has 66 antennas.","It sits at about 5,000 meters.","It photographed a disk forming planets in 2014."],
fun:"ALMA’s 100-ton antennas are moved around the desert by giant trucks named Otto and Lore."});

ADDART({id:"arecibo",t:"Arecibo and FAST",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"📡",w:"Arecibo Telescope",r:["radioastronomy","seti","pulsar","chinacountry","exoplanet"],
p:[["Giant dishes","Arecibo and FAST are the largest single-dish radio telescopes ever built."],["Arecibo’s fall","Puerto Rico’s Arecibo telescope collapsed in 2020."],["China’s giant","China’s FAST, 500 meters wide, is now the largest."]],
l:"The Arecibo Telescope in Puerto Rico and the Five-hundred-meter Aperture Spherical radio Telescope (FAST) in China are the largest single-dish radio telescopes ever built, each set into a natural bowl in the landscape. Arecibo, completed in 1963, had a dish 305 meters across built into a sinkhole, with a 900-ton platform of instruments hanging above it from cables. For 53 years it was the world’s largest single-dish telescope. It discovered that Mercury rotates in 59 days rather than 88, found the first binary pulsar in 1974, a discovery that earned Russell Hulse and Joseph Taylor the 1993 Nobel Prize in Physics, and was used by Aleksander Wolszczan and Dale Frail to find the first confirmed planets beyond the solar system, orbiting a pulsar, in 1992. It also beamed the famous Arecibo message toward a star cluster in 1974 and tracked asteroids with radar. After cables failed in 2020, the platform crashed into the dish on 1 December 2020. FAST, in Guizhou province in southern China, was completed in September 2016. Its 500-meter dish, made of 4,450 panels that can change shape, has discovered more than a thousand new pulsars.",
s:[["Building in a sinkhole","Arecibo’s builders used a natural limestone sinkhole as a ready-made bowl, saving the cost of building a support structure. FAST also sits in a natural depression in karst terrain, and thousands of residents were relocated to keep the area radio-quiet."],["Radar astronomy","Arecibo’s powerful radar bounced signals off planets and asteroids, mapping the surface of Venus through its clouds and measuring the shapes and spins of near-Earth asteroids."],["The collapse","In August and November 2020, cables supporting Arecibo’s platform broke. Engineers decided the telescope could not be safely repaired, and before a planned controlled demolition, the platform fell on its own, destroying the dish."],["FAST","FAST’s surface is made of panels moved by actuators, forming a 300-meter-wide reflecting area aimed at different parts of the sky. A radio-quiet zone surrounds it, and visitors must leave phones and cameras behind."]],
k:[["Arecibo dish","305 meters"],["Arecibo completed","1963"],["Arecibo collapsed","1 December 2020"],["FAST dish","500 meters"],["FAST completed","September 2016"]],
d:[["1963","Arecibo Telescope begins operating"],["1965","Arecibo measures Mercury’s rotation"],["1974","Arecibo message sent; first binary pulsar found"],["1992","First exoplanets found using Arecibo"],["2016","FAST completed"],["2020","Arecibo collapses"]],
s2:[["Arecibo in films","Arecibo appeared in the James Bond film GoldenEye (1995) and in Contact (1997), making it one of the world’s most famous telescopes."],["Puerto Rico’s pride","Arecibo trained generations of Puerto Rican scientists and drew many visitors. After the collapse, plans focused on education and science activities at the site."]],
sp:["Planets around a dead star","The first confirmed planets beyond the solar system, found with Arecibo in 1992, orbit a pulsar, a spinning dead star, an unexpected place for planets."],
g:[["Radio telescope","A telescope that detects radio waves"],["Sinkhole","A natural hole formed by dissolving rock"],["Radar","Bouncing radio waves off objects to study them"],["Pulsar","A rapidly spinning neutron star"]],
f:["Arecibo’s dish was 305 meters wide.","Arecibo collapsed in 2020.","FAST’s dish is 500 meters wide."],
fun:"The first planets found beyond our solar system, discovered with Arecibo, orbit a dead star called a pulsar."});

ADDART({id:"cargoships",t:"Space Station Cargo Ships",c:"Space Exploration",i:"📦",w:"Cargo spacecraft",r:["iss","spacex","soyuz","tiangong","salyut"],
p:[["Deliveries to orbit","Uncrewed cargo ships carry food, water, fuel and equipment to space stations."],["Progress first","The Soviet Progress began deliveries in 1978."],["Trash on the way back","Most cargo ships burn up on reentry, carrying away garbage."]],
l:"Cargo spacecraft are uncrewed vehicles that deliver supplies, such as food, water, air, fuel, clothing, spare parts and science experiments, to space stations, allowing crews to stay in orbit for months. The Soviet Union launched the first, Progress 1, on 20 January 1978 to resupply the Salyut 6 station, and improved Progress ships still serve the International Space Station. Europe’s Automated Transfer Vehicle (ATV), the size of a London double-decker bus, made five flights from 2008 to 2014, and Japan’s H-II Transfer Vehicle (HTV) made nine flights from 2009 to 2020, followed by the upgraded HTV-X, first launched in October 2025. In 2012, SpaceX’s Dragon became the first commercial spacecraft to deliver cargo to the space station and remains the only cargo ship able to bring large amounts of material back to Earth. Northrop Grumman’s Cygnus has flown since 2013, and China’s Tianzhou ships, first flown in 2017, supply the Tiangong station. Sierra Space’s Dream Chaser spaceplane has faced long delays. Most cargo ships are filled with trash after unloading and then burn up in Earth’s atmosphere, while some also use their engines to raise the station’s orbit.",
s:[["What gets delivered","A typical delivery carries several metric tons, including freeze-dried and fresh food, clothes, oxygen tanks, water, scientific experiments, spare equipment and sometimes treats such as ice cream or fresh fruit."],["Docking and berthing","Progress and Tianzhou dock automatically, while Cygnus and HTV approached the station and were captured by astronauts using the robotic arm Canadarm2, then attached to a port, a process called berthing. Dragon now docks automatically."],["Returning science","Because Dragon survives reentry and splashes down, it returns experiments, such as biological samples kept frozen, to scientists on Earth."],["Taking out the trash","The space station has no garbage truck, so crews pack waste into departing cargo ships, which burn up over the remote South Pacific Ocean."]],
k:[["First cargo ship","Progress 1, 20 January 1978"],["First commercial cargo delivery","SpaceX Dragon, 2012"],["Returns cargo to Earth","Dragon"],["Japan’s new ship","HTV-X (first flight October 2025)"],["China’s cargo ship","Tianzhou (since 2017)"]],
d:[["1978","Progress 1 resupplies Salyut 6"],["2008","Europe’s first ATV, Jules Verne"],["2009","Japan’s first HTV"],["2012","Dragon delivers cargo to the ISS"],["2013","First Cygnus mission"],["2017","China’s first Tianzhou"],["2025","Japan’s first HTV-X"]],
s2:[["Named after people","Northrop Grumman names each Cygnus after a person who contributed to spaceflight, and Europe named its ATVs after Jules Verne, Johannes Kepler, Edoardo Amaldi, Albert Einstein and Georges Lemaître."],["Fresh food","Astronauts look forward to cargo deliveries that bring fresh apples, oranges and onions, often sent by space agencies as a morale boost."]],
sp:["Garbage fireworks","Cargo ships filled with trash make a fiery reentry, sometimes photographed by astronauts from the station as streaks of light in the atmosphere."],
g:[["Cargo spacecraft","An uncrewed supply vehicle"],["Berthing","Attaching a spacecraft with a robotic arm"],["Reboost","Raising a station’s orbit with engine burns"],["Uncrewed","Without people aboard"]],
f:["Progress 1 made the first cargo delivery in 1978.","Dragon can bring cargo back to Earth.","Most cargo ships burn up carrying trash."],
fun:"Space station crews pack their trash into cargo ships, which then burn up in the atmosphere."});

ADDART({id:"shenzhou",t:"Shenzhou: China’s Crewed Spacecraft",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🇨🇳",w:"Shenzhou (spacecraft)",r:["tiangong","longmarch","chinacountry","womeninspace","soyuz"],
p:[["China’s space capsule","Shenzhou spacecraft carry Chinese astronauts to orbit."],["Yang Liwei, 2003","China became the third country to launch a person into space."],["Crews to Tiangong","Shenzhou crews live aboard the Tiangong space station."]],
l:"Shenzhou, meaning ‘divine vessel’, is China’s crewed spacecraft, which carries astronauts, often called taikonauts, to orbit and to the Tiangong space station. Based partly on the Russian Soyuz design but larger, it has an orbital module, a reentry capsule and a service module with solar panels. After four uncrewed test flights beginning in 1999, Shenzhou 5 carried Yang Liwei into orbit on 15 October 2003, making China the third country, after the Soviet Union and the United States, to launch a person into space independently. Zhai Zhigang made China’s first spacewalk in 2008, and in 2012 Liu Yang became the first Chinese woman in space. Since 2021, Shenzhou crews have lived aboard Tiangong for about six months at a time. In November 2025, a crack caused by a tiny piece of space debris was found in a window of Shenzhou 20, so its crew returned on Shenzhou 21, and China launched the uncrewed Shenzhou 22 as a replacement lifeboat, an unprecedented emergency response. In May 2026, Shenzhou 23 carried China’s first astronaut from Hong Kong and began a planned year-long stay. China is developing a new spacecraft, Mengzhou, for Moon missions.",
s:[["The spacecraft","Shenzhou can carry three astronauts. Its orbital module, which can stay in orbit after the crew returns, gives extra living space. The bell-shaped reentry capsule lands under a parachute in the Gobi Desert or Inner Mongolia."],["Firsts","Wang Yaping became the first Chinese woman to walk in space in 2021. In 2023, Gui Haichao became China’s first civilian in space, and China has trained astronauts from other countries, including Pakistan."],["The debris scare","Before Shenzhou 20’s planned return in November 2025, the crew found cracks in a window, likely caused by space debris. Officials kept the crew safe aboard Tiangong and brought them home on Shenzhou 21, showing the value of having backup spacecraft."],["Mengzhou","China’s next-generation crew spacecraft, Mengzhou (‘dream vessel’), is designed to carry astronauts to the Moon on the Long March 10 rocket, with a crewed lunar landing planned before 2030."]],
k:[["Meaning","Divine vessel"],["First crewed flight","Shenzhou 5, 15 October 2003"],["First Chinese astronaut","Yang Liwei"],["Crew","Up to 3"],["Destination","Tiangong space station"]],
d:[["1999","Shenzhou 1 uncrewed test flight"],["2003","Yang Liwei flies on Shenzhou 5"],["2008","First Chinese spacewalk"],["2012","Liu Yang becomes the first Chinese woman in space"],["2021","Crews begin living on Tiangong"],["2025","Shenzhou 20 damaged by debris; Shenzhou 22 launched as replacement"],["2026","Shenzhou 23 begins a year-long mission"]],
s2:[["Yang Liwei","Yang Liwei, a former fighter pilot, orbited Earth 14 times in about 21 hours. He later said he heard a mysterious knocking sound during the flight, which has never been fully explained."],["Taikonaut","The word ‘taikonaut’ combines the Chinese word taikong, meaning ‘space’, with ‘astronaut’. In Chinese, astronauts are called hangtianyuan."]],
sp:["A mysterious knock","During Shenzhou 5, Yang Liwei reported hearing a sound like someone knocking on the spacecraft with a wooden hammer; later astronauts reported similar noises, perhaps caused by the spacecraft expanding and contracting."],
g:[["Taikonaut","A Chinese astronaut"],["Reentry capsule","The part of a spacecraft that returns to Earth"],["Orbital module","A living compartment used in orbit"],["Lifeboat","A spacecraft kept ready for emergency return"]],
f:["Yang Liwei flew on Shenzhou 5 in 2003.","Liu Yang was the first Chinese woman in space.","Shenzhou crews live on Tiangong."],
fun:"China’s first astronaut reported hearing a mysterious knocking sound outside his spacecraft."});

ADDART({id:"hera",t:"Hera: Revisiting DART’s Asteroid",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🎯",w:"Hera (space mission)",r:["planetdefense","asteroidbelt","spaceagencies","mars","phobosdeimos"],
p:[["Checking the crash","Hera is a European mission to study the asteroid NASA’s DART spacecraft hit."],["Planetary defense","It helps learn how to push dangerous asteroids off course."],["Arriving in 2026","Hera was due to reach the Didymos asteroids in November 2026."]],
l:"Hera is a European Space Agency mission to visit the double asteroid system Didymos and its small moon Dimorphos, the target of NASA’s Double Asteroid Redirection Test (DART). On 26 September 2022, DART deliberately crashed into Dimorphos, about 160 meters wide, at about 22,000 kilometers per hour, shortening its orbit around Didymos by about 32 minutes. It was the first test of the ‘kinetic impactor’ method of planetary defense, which could one day push an asteroid on a collision course with Earth onto a safer path. Hera, launched on a SpaceX Falcon 9 on 7 October 2024, is designed to perform a detailed ‘crime scene investigation’, measuring the crater, the mass and makeup of Dimorphos and how much the impact changed it. This will let scientists calculate exactly how much push the impact delivered, so the method can be used reliably in the future. On its way, Hera flew past Mars in March 2025, taking images of the small moon Deimos. It carries two small CubeSats, Milani, which will map the asteroids’ composition, and Juventas, which will use radar to look inside Dimorphos, the first radar probe of an asteroid’s interior. Hera was due to reach the Didymos system in November 2026, about a month earlier than first planned.",
s:[["DART’s impact","DART’s crash threw out a huge plume of rock and dust that formed a tail thousands of kilometers long. The recoil from the ejected material gave Dimorphos an extra push, making the impact more effective than expected."],["Why Hera matters","From Earth, scientists could measure the change in Dimorphos’s orbit but not its mass or the shape of the crater. Hera will provide these details, turning a one-off experiment into a well-understood technique."],["The CubeSats","Milani and Juventas, each about the size of a shoebox, will fly closer than Hera and eventually try to land on the asteroids, gathering data on their surfaces."],["Not a threat","Didymos and Dimorphos pose no danger to Earth; they were chosen because a double asteroid lets scientists measure orbit changes precisely."]],
k:[["Agency","European Space Agency"],["Launched","7 October 2024"],["Target","Didymos and its moon Dimorphos"],["DART impact","26 September 2022"],["Orbit change from DART","About 32 minutes shorter"]],
d:[["2021","NASA launches DART"],["2022","DART strikes Dimorphos"],["2024","Hera launched"],["2025","Hera flies past Mars and Deimos"],["2026","Hera due to arrive at Didymos"]],
s2:[["Hera and Juno","Hera is named after the Greek goddess of marriage. Didymos means ‘twin’ in Greek, and Dimorphos means ‘having two forms’, referring to how DART changed it."],["Apophis","Another asteroid, Apophis, will pass very close to Earth on 13 April 2029, closer than some satellites. ESA’s RAMSES mission and NASA’s OSIRIS-APEX plan to study it."]],
sp:["A boulder-covered rubble pile","DART’s final images showed that Dimorphos is a ‘rubble pile’ covered in boulders, loosely held together by weak gravity."],
g:[["Kinetic impactor","A spacecraft that hits an asteroid to change its path"],["Planetary defense","Protecting Earth from asteroid impacts"],["Binary asteroid","Two asteroids orbiting each other"],["CubeSat","A small standardized satellite"]],
f:["DART hit Dimorphos in 2022.","Hera launched in 2024.","Hera carries two CubeSats."],
fun:"NASA’s DART crash shortened the orbit of the asteroid moon Dimorphos by about 32 minutes."});

ADDART({id:"zerogravityflights",t:"Zero-Gravity Flights",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛫",w:"Reduced-gravity aircraft",r:["grav","astronauts","spacebody","flight","apollo13"],
p:[["Weightless in an airplane","Special airplane flights create about 20 seconds of weightlessness."],["The Vomit Comet","NASA’s training plane earned a famous nickname."],["Training and research","Astronauts train and scientists test experiments on these flights."]],
l:"Zero-gravity flights, also called parabolic or reduced-gravity flights, use specially modified airplanes to give passengers short periods of weightlessness without going to space. The plane climbs steeply, then the pilots reduce thrust so that the aircraft follows the same curved path, called a parabola, as a thrown ball. During the top of the arc, everything inside falls together with the plane, so passengers float freely for about 20 to 25 seconds. The plane then pulls out of its dive, pressing passengers down with nearly twice normal gravity, and repeats the maneuver dozens of times per flight. NASA began such flights in the late 1950s to prepare astronauts for spaceflight, and its KC-135 aircraft became known as the ‘Vomit Comet’ because many passengers became sick. Scientists use parabolic flights to test equipment and study how fluids, flames and living things behave without gravity. The weightless scenes in the 1995 film Apollo 13 were filmed aboard NASA’s plane. Today, companies in the United States and Europe offer flights to the public, and on 26 April 2007, physicist Stephen Hawking, who used a wheelchair because of motor neurone disease, floated freely on a zero-gravity flight.",
s:[["How it works","Weightlessness happens whenever objects are in free fall. Astronauts in orbit are also falling, continuously around Earth. A parabolic flight creates the same free fall for a short time by flying a ballistic arc."],["Partial gravity","By flying shallower arcs, pilots can simulate the Moon’s gravity, about one-sixth of Earth’s, or Mars’s, about three-eighths, letting astronauts practice moving in spacesuits on other worlds."],["Research","Europe’s Airbus A310 Zero-G, operated by the French company Novespace, carries dozens of experiments a year, from medical tests to new spacecraft technologies. Drop towers, such as one 146 meters tall in Bremen, Germany, give a few seconds of weightlessness for experiments."],["Motion sickness","Many people feel sick because the inner ear’s balance sense gets confusing signals. Passengers are often given medicine and told to keep their heads still during transitions."]],
k:[["Weightless time per parabola","About 20–25 seconds"],["Parabolas per flight","Often 15–40"],["NASA nickname","Vomit Comet"],["Film shot aboard","Apollo 13 (1995)"],["Stephen Hawking’s flight","26 April 2007"]],
d:[["1950s","Early parabolic flights for research"],["1959","NASA begins astronaut training flights"],["1973","KC-135 becomes NASA’s main reduced-gravity plane"],["1995","Apollo 13 film scenes shot in weightlessness"],["2007","Stephen Hawking floats on a zero-gravity flight"]],
s2:[["Hawking’s float","Stephen Hawking made eight parabolas during his 2007 flight and said he hoped it would encourage public interest in space. He called the experience amazing."],["Weddings and art","People have held weddings, performed dances and made art in weightlessness on commercial zero-gravity flights."]],
sp:["Not really zero","Gravity is never actually turned off on these flights; passengers float because they and the plane fall at exactly the same rate."],
g:[["Parabola","A U-shaped curve like the path of a thrown ball"],["Free fall","Falling under gravity alone"],["Microgravity","Very weak apparent gravity"],["Motion sickness","Nausea caused by confusing motion signals"]],
f:["Parabolic flights give about 20 seconds of weightlessness.","NASA’s plane was called the Vomit Comet.","Stephen Hawking floated on a flight in 2007."],
fun:"The floating scenes in the film Apollo 13 were filmed in real weightlessness aboard NASA’s ‘Vomit Comet’."});

ADDART({id:"spaceradiation",t:"Radiation in Space",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☢️",w:"Health threat from cosmic rays",r:["cosmicrays","spacebody","magnet","solarwind","astronauts"],
p:[["An invisible danger","Space is filled with radiation that Earth’s atmosphere and magnetic field block."],["Van Allen belts","Belts of trapped radiation surround Earth."],["A challenge for Mars","Radiation is one of the biggest risks for astronauts going to Mars."]],
l:"Radiation in space is one of the main dangers for astronauts and spacecraft. On Earth, our thick atmosphere and magnetic field shield us from most of it, but in space there are three main sources: galactic cosmic rays, high-energy particles from exploding stars far away; solar particle events, bursts of protons from the Sun during solar storms; and particles trapped in the Van Allen radiation belts around Earth, discovered in 1958 by James Van Allen using instruments on Explorer 1, the first US satellite. Radiation can damage DNA, raising the long-term risk of cancer, and very large doses can cause radiation sickness. Astronauts on the International Space Station, inside Earth’s magnetic field, receive in a single day about as much radiation as people on Earth get from natural sources in one to three months. A trip to Mars would expose astronauts to much more: NASA’s Curiosity rover measured that a round trip would give a dose about equal to the maximum allowed for an astronaut’s entire career under some guidelines. Engineers protect crews with shielding made of materials rich in hydrogen, such as water and plastic, and storm shelters, while spacecraft electronics are hardened against radiation.",
s:[["Light flashes","Since Apollo, astronauts have reported seeing flashes of light with their eyes closed. These are caused by cosmic ray particles passing through their eyes or brains."],["Solar storms","A large solar storm in August 1972, between the Apollo 16 and 17 missions, would have been dangerous for astronauts on the Moon. Future crews will rely on space weather forecasts and shelters."],["Shielding","Heavy metals can create showers of secondary radiation when hit by cosmic rays, so lighter, hydrogen-rich materials work better. Spacecraft designers may place water tanks and supplies around crew quarters."],["Testing on Artemis I","Two mannequin torsos, Helga and Zohar, flew around the Moon on Artemis I in 2022 with radiation detectors; one wore a protective vest to test how well it reduced exposure."]],
k:[["Main sources","Cosmic rays, solar particles, Van Allen belts"],["Van Allen belts discovered","1958, Explorer 1"],["Main long-term risk","Cancer"],["Best shielding","Hydrogen-rich materials such as water"],["Measured on the way to Mars","Curiosity rover, 2011–2012"]],
d:[["1912","Victor Hess discovers cosmic rays using balloons"],["1958","Van Allen belts discovered"],["1969","Apollo astronauts report light flashes"],["1972","Major solar storm between Apollo missions"],["2013","Curiosity’s radiation measurements on the way to Mars published"],["2022","Artemis I mannequins measure radiation around the Moon"]],
s2:[["Through the belts","Apollo astronauts crossed the Van Allen belts quickly on paths that avoided the most intense regions, receiving modest doses."],["Electronics","Radiation can flip bits in computer memory or damage chips. Spacecraft use radiation-hardened processors and error-checking software to keep working."]],
sp:["Cosmic ray sparkles","Astronauts describe the light flashes caused by cosmic rays as streaks, dots or starbursts, seen most often when they are trying to sleep."],
g:[["Cosmic rays","High-energy particles from space"],["Van Allen belts","Zones of trapped radiation around Earth"],["Shielding","Material that blocks radiation"],["Dose","An amount of radiation absorbed"]],
f:["Earth’s magnetic field protects us from much space radiation.","The Van Allen belts were found in 1958.","Water makes good radiation shielding."],
fun:"Astronauts sometimes see flashes of light with their eyes closed, caused by cosmic ray particles passing through their eyes."});

ADDART({id:"hubbleservicing",t:"Hubble’s Servicing Missions",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔧",w:"Hubble Space Telescope servicing missions",r:["hubblest","spaceshuttle","spacewalks","astronauts","shuttledisasters"],
p:[["Repairing a telescope in space","Astronauts visited Hubble five times to fix and upgrade it."],["Fixing blurry vision","The first mission in 1993 corrected Hubble’s flawed mirror."],["Spacewalking mechanics","Astronauts used special tools on long spacewalks."]],
l:"The Hubble Space Telescope, launched on 24 April 1990, was designed to be visited and upgraded by Space Shuttle astronauts, and five servicing missions between 1993 and 2009 kept it working and improved it. Soon after launch, scientists discovered that Hubble’s 2.4-meter main mirror had been ground to slightly the wrong shape, an error of about 2 micrometers, a fiftieth of the width of a human hair, which made its images blurry. In December 1993, the crew of the Space Shuttle Endeavour, on mission STS-61, installed a device called COSTAR containing corrective mirrors, a bit like eyeglasses, plus a new camera with built-in correction, during five spacewalks. The repair was a triumph, and Hubble’s sharp images soon amazed the world. Later missions in 1997, 1999, 2002 and 2009 replaced gyroscopes, batteries, solar panels and computers and installed new cameras and spectrometers, making Hubble far more powerful than at launch. After the Columbia disaster in 2003, NASA canceled the final servicing mission as too risky, but public and scientific pressure led NASA to restore it, and Space Shuttle Atlantis visited Hubble in May 2009. No further visits have been made, and since 2024 Hubble has operated using a single gyroscope to save its remaining ones.",
s:[["The flawed mirror","A measuring device used during mirror manufacturing had been assembled with a lens 1.3 millimeters out of place, causing the mirror to be polished with a tiny error. Because the flaw was precisely known, engineers could design corrective optics."],["Spacewalk work","Servicing missions involved some of the most complex spacewalks ever. Astronauts used more than 100 specially designed tools, some of them developed just to remove tiny screws while wearing bulky gloves."],["The final mission","During the 2009 mission, astronauts repaired two instruments never designed to be fixed in space, removing more than a hundred screws. The mission left Hubble in its best condition ever."],["Hubble’s future","Hubble has no means of being serviced now that the shuttle has retired. Its orbit is slowly decaying, and NASA studied but did not proceed with a 2022 private proposal to boost it higher."]],
k:[["Hubble launched","24 April 1990"],["Mirror flaw","About 2 micrometers"],["First servicing mission","STS-61, December 1993"],["Servicing missions","Five (1993–2009)"],["Final visit","Atlantis, May 2009"]],
d:[["1990","Hubble launched; mirror flaw discovered"],["1993","First servicing mission fixes Hubble’s vision"],["1997","Second servicing mission"],["1999","Third servicing mission (part A)"],["2002","Servicing mission 3B"],["2009","Final servicing mission"],["2024","Hubble switches to one-gyroscope mode"]],
s2:[["The scientist-astronaut","Astronaut John Grunsfeld, an astronomer, flew on three Hubble servicing missions and later led NASA’s science programs."],["Celebrated images","After the 1993 repair, Hubble’s images, such as the ‘Pillars of Creation’ in 1995, became some of the most famous scientific pictures ever."]],
sp:["Glasses for a telescope","The corrective device COSTAR worked like a pair of glasses for Hubble’s instruments. It was removed in 2009 because all newer instruments had built-in correction."],
g:[["Servicing mission","A mission to repair or upgrade a spacecraft"],["Spherical aberration","A blur caused by a wrongly shaped mirror or lens"],["Gyroscope","A device that helps a spacecraft point accurately"],["COSTAR","Hubble’s corrective optics device"]],
f:["Astronauts serviced Hubble five times.","The 1993 mission fixed Hubble’s blurry vision.","The last servicing mission was in 2009."],
fun:"Hubble’s main mirror was off by only about a fiftieth of the width of a human hair, but that was enough to blur its images."});

ADDART({id:"artemisaccords",t:"The Artemis Accords",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🤝",w:"Artemis Accords",r:["artemis","spacelaw","moon","spaceagencies","asteroidmining","spacemining"],
p:[["Rules for the Moon","The Artemis Accords are principles for peaceful exploration of the Moon and beyond."],["Dozens of countries","Seventy-six countries had signed by September 2026."],["Not everyone","China and Russia have not signed and lead a separate lunar program."]],
l:"The Artemis Accords are a set of non-binding principles for the peaceful, safe and cooperative exploration of the Moon, Mars, comets and asteroids, created by the United States in connection with NASA’s Artemis program. The accords were first signed on 13 October 2020 by eight countries: Australia, Canada, Italy, Japan, Luxembourg, the United Arab Emirates, the United Kingdom and the United States. They build on the 1967 Outer Space Treaty and commit signatories to principles including peaceful purposes, transparency about their activities, making their systems compatible, helping astronauts in distress, registering space objects, sharing scientific data, protecting historic sites such as the Apollo landing sites, using space resources in line with international law, avoiding harmful interference through ‘safety zones’ and limiting space debris. Many countries joined in the following years, including the Philippines, Hungary and Malaysia in December 2025, and by September 2026, when San Marino signed, the accords had 76 signatories. China and Russia have not signed; they are developing their own International Lunar Research Station and have invited other countries to join. Supporters see the accords as practical rules for a busy Moon, while critics question whether resource rules favor wealthy nations.",
s:[["What the principles say","The accords stress that space activities should be peaceful and transparent. Signatories agree to share information about where they operate, to coordinate to avoid accidents and to release scientific data publicly."],["Space resources","The accords state that extracting resources such as water ice from the Moon does not in itself amount to claiming territory, which is banned by the Outer Space Treaty. Some legal experts and countries dispute this interpretation."],["Heritage sites","Signatories agree to preserve historically significant sites, such as the Apollo 11 landing site with Neil Armstrong’s footprints and the remains of early landers, from damage by future missions."],["A rival vision","China and Russia’s International Lunar Research Station plans a base near the lunar south pole in the 2030s, with partners including several countries. The two groups reflect wider geopolitical competition in space."]],
k:[["First signed","13 October 2020"],["Founding signatories","8"],["Signatories (September 2026)","76"],["Based on","Outer Space Treaty (1967)"],["Not signed","China and Russia"]],
d:[["1967","Outer Space Treaty signed"],["2020","Artemis Accords signed by eight countries"],["2022","Accords reach 20 signatories"],["2025","Philippines, Hungary and Malaysia sign"],["2026","San Marino becomes the 76th signatory"]],
s2:[["Non-binding","The accords are political commitments rather than a treaty, so they do not need approval by national legislatures and carry no legal penalties, which made it easier for many countries to join."],["Annual meetings","Signatories meet regularly to discuss how to put the principles into practice, such as how to share information about lunar activities and handle debris."]],
sp:["Named after a goddess","Artemis is the Greek goddess of the Moon and twin sister of Apollo, linking NASA’s new program to the Apollo missions of the 1960s and 1970s."],
g:[["Accords","Agreements"],["Non-binding","Not legally enforceable"],["Signatory","A country that has signed an agreement"],["Safety zone","An area where operators coordinate to avoid interference"]],
f:["The Artemis Accords were first signed in 2020.","76 countries had signed by September 2026.","China and Russia have not signed."],
fun:"The Artemis Accords include a promise to protect historic sites on the Moon, such as Neil Armstrong’s footprints."});

;
/* data/body8.js */
// The Human Body: hair and nails, vitamins, fever, and hiccups, sneezes and yawns.
ADDART({id:"hair",t:"Hair and Nails",c:"Human Body",i:"💇",w:"Hair",r:["skin","nutri","hygiene","dna","aging"],
p:[["Made of keratin","Hair and nails are made of a tough protein called keratin."],["Always growing","Scalp hair grows about a centimeter a month."],["Color from melanin","Hair color comes from the pigment melanin."]],
l:"Hair and nails are structures that grow from the skin and are made mostly of keratin, a tough protein also found in animal horns, hooves and feathers. Hair covers almost the entire human body except the palms, soles and lips, though most body hair is fine and short. A typical scalp has around 100,000 hairs, each growing from a follicle in the skin. Hair protects the scalp from sunlight, helps keep the body warm and senses touch, while eyebrows and eyelashes protect the eyes. Nails protect the tips of fingers and toes and help us pick up small objects. Hair color, texture and growth depend on genes, age, hormones and health. Hair and nails can also reveal signs of illness or poor nutrition.",
s:[["How hair grows","Each hair grows from a follicle, a tiny pocket in the skin. At its base, cells divide quickly and push older cells upward, where they harden into the hair shaft. Scalp hair grows about 1 centimeter a month in cycles: a growing phase that can last years, a short resting phase, and a shedding phase. Losing about 50 to 100 hairs a day is normal."],["Hair color and texture","Hair color comes from two forms of the pigment melanin: one producing brown and black, the other red and yellow. As people age, follicles make less pigment, and hair turns gray or white. The shape of the follicle affects texture: round follicles tend to produce straight hair, while oval or flat ones produce wavy or curly hair."],["Nails","Fingernails grow about 3 millimeters a month, faster than toenails. The visible nail plate is dead keratin, growing from living tissue under the skin at its base. A healthy nail is smooth and pink. Changes in color, shape or texture can sometimes signal health problems, such as anemia or fungal infections."],["What hair does","Scalp hair shields against sunlight and helps keep heat in. Eyelashes and eyebrows keep dust, sweat and rain out of the eyes. Hairs in the nose and ears trap dust. Tiny muscles attached to body hairs make them stand up when we are cold or frightened, causing goosebumps."]],
k:[["Made of","Keratin"],["Scalp hairs","About 100,000"],["Hair growth","About 1 cm per month"],["Fingernail growth","About 3 mm per month"],["Color pigment","Melanin"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Ancient Egyptians wear wigs and use henna"],["1905","The permanent wave for curling hair is demonstrated in London"],["1907","Synthetic hair dye is sold commercially in France"],["1950s","Scientists study the structure of keratin"],["1980s","Hair analysis is used in forensic science"],["2020s","Researchers study why hair turns gray"]],
s2:[["Head lice","Head lice, called kuto in Filipino, are tiny insects that live on the scalp and lay eggs, called nits, on hair shafts. They are common among schoolchildren worldwide and spread by head-to-head contact, not because of poor hygiene. Regular combing with a fine-toothed comb and appropriate treatments can remove them. Parents in the Philippines traditionally check children’s hair with a suyod, a fine comb."],["Hair in culture","Hairstyles carry cultural meanings in many societies, from braids and headwraps to shaved heads in religious traditions. In precolonial Philippines, both men and women often wore long hair, and some groups tied it in elaborate styles. Hair is cut or kept as part of ceremonies around the world, from first haircuts to mourning rituals."],["Hair loss","Hair loss can be caused by genes, hormones, stress, illness, certain medicines or nutritional problems. Male-pattern baldness is common as men age and is mainly inherited. Some hair loss after illness or childbirth is temporary. Doctors can help identify causes and treatments."],["Hair as evidence","Hair can reveal information about a person. Strands with roots contain DNA that can identify individuals. Hair also records traces of substances taken over months, such as medicines and drugs, and even long-ago diets, which helps forensic scientists and archaeologists."],["Why does hair turn gray?","Gray hair happens when the pigment-making cells in follicles stop working. Genes largely determine when it begins. Studies in mice have shown that intense stress can speed up graying by affecting the stem cells that produce pigment cells, which may help explain stories of hair turning gray during hard times."],["Nails and health","Doctors sometimes look at nails for clues. Very pale nails may suggest anemia, spoon-shaped nails can indicate iron deficiency, and dark lines may need checking. Biting nails can damage them and spread germs. Keeping nails clean and trimmed is part of good hygiene."]],
sp:["The comb and the suyod","On a sunny afternoon in a Philippine province, a grandmother sits on the steps of her house with her granddaughter leaning on her lap. Using a suyod, a comb with very fine teeth, she carefully works through the child’s long hair, section by section, looking for kuto and their tiny eggs. She chats and hums as she works. The ritual is familiar to many Filipino families, a mix of care and conversation passed down through generations. Health workers say such regular combing really does help control head lice, which are common among schoolchildren everywhere and nothing to be ashamed of."],
g:[["Keratin","A tough protein in hair and nails"],["Follicle","A tiny pocket in the skin where hair grows"],["Melanin","The pigment that colors hair and skin"],["Kuto","The Filipino word for head lice"]],
f:["Hair and nails are made of keratin.","Losing 50 to 100 hairs a day is normal.","Fingernails grow faster than toenails."],
fun:"Hair, fingernails, animal horns and bird feathers are all made from forms of the same protein, keratin."});

ADDART({id:"vitamins",t:"Vitamins and Minerals",c:"Health",i:"🥕",w:"Vitamin",r:["nutri","anemia","bones","phfood","fruits"],
p:[["Tiny but vital","The body needs small amounts of vitamins and minerals to work properly."],["Diseases of lack","Missing vitamins can cause diseases such as scurvy and rickets."],["From food","Most people get them from a varied diet."]],
l:"Vitamins are organic substances that the body needs in small amounts to grow, function and stay healthy, but cannot make enough of on its own, so they must come from food. There are 13 vitamins: A, C, D, E, K and eight B vitamins. Minerals such as iron, calcium, iodine and zinc are elements the body also needs. Together they are called micronutrients. They help build bones, make blood, fight infections, turn food into energy and keep eyes, skin and nerves healthy. A lack of particular vitamins or minerals causes deficiency diseases, such as scurvy, rickets, beriberi, anemia and goiter. Most people can get enough from a varied diet with fruits, vegetables, whole grains, beans, fish and other foods. In the Philippines, programs add vitamins and minerals to foods and give supplements to children.",
s:[["Two kinds of vitamins","Fat-soluble vitamins, A, D, E and K, are stored in the body’s fat and liver and can build up to harmful levels if taken in large doses. Water-soluble vitamins, C and the B vitamins, are not stored for long and need to be eaten regularly; excess amounts usually leave the body in urine."],["What vitamins do","Vitamin A helps vision and immunity. B vitamins help turn food into energy and keep nerves healthy. Vitamin C helps heal wounds and absorb iron. Vitamin D helps the body absorb calcium for strong bones and is made in the skin with sunlight. Vitamin K helps blood clot."],["Important minerals","Calcium builds bones and teeth. Iron is needed to make hemoglobin, which carries oxygen in blood. Iodine is needed by the thyroid gland; lack of it can cause goiter and harm brain development. Zinc supports growth and immunity."],["Food sources","Green leafy vegetables like malunggay and kangkong, orange fruits and vegetables like mango, papaya and squash, citrus such as calamansi, fish, eggs, beans, nuts and whole grains are rich in vitamins and minerals. Iodized salt provides iodine, and fortified rice and flour add nutrients to everyday foods."]],
k:[["Number of vitamins","13"],["Fat-soluble vitamins","A, D, E, K"],["Word coined","1912, by Casimir Funk"],["Philippine law","Food Fortification Act of 2000"],["Common deficiency","Iron, causing anemia"]],
d:[["1747","James Lind tests citrus fruit against scurvy"],["1897","Christiaan Eijkman links beriberi to polished rice"],["1912","Casimir Funk coins the word vitamine"],["1920s","Vitamin D is linked to preventing rickets"],["2000","The Philippines passes the Food Fortification Act"],["2021","The Philippines approves Golden Rice for planting"]],
s2:[["Scurvy and the sailors","On long sea voyages in the 1500s to 1700s, many sailors suffered from scurvy, with bleeding gums, weakness and slow-healing wounds, and many died. In 1747 the Scottish naval surgeon James Lind gave different treatments to sick sailors and found that those given oranges and lemons recovered quickly. Scurvy is caused by a lack of vitamin C."],["Beriberi and rice","In the late 1800s, beriberi, a disease causing weakness and heart problems, was common in Asia, including the Philippines, where people ate white, polished rice. The Dutch doctor Christiaan Eijkman, working in Java, found that chickens fed polished rice developed similar symptoms, which were cured by rice with its husk. The cause was later identified as a lack of vitamin B1, or thiamine."],["Micronutrient programs in the Philippines","Philippine health programs have given vitamin A capsules to young children, iron supplements to pregnant women and promoted iodized salt to prevent goiter. The Food Fortification Act of 2000 requires adding nutrients such as vitamin A, iron and iodine to some foods, including flour, cooking oil and sugar. The national nutrition council monitors malnutrition and anemia rates."],["The Golden Rice debate","Golden Rice is a genetically modified rice that produces beta-carotene, which the body turns into vitamin A. It was developed to help prevent vitamin A deficiency, which can cause blindness. In 2021 the Philippines became the first country to approve it for commercial planting. Supporters say it can save sight and lives, while opponents raise concerns about safety, farmers’ independence and whether better diets would be more effective. In 2024 a Philippine court halted its commercial planting pending further review."],["Do we need supplements?","Many people take vitamin pills. Doctors recommend supplements in specific situations, such as folic acid before and during pregnancy, vitamin D for people with little sun exposure, or iron for those with anemia. For most healthy people, a balanced diet provides enough. Very high doses of some vitamins can be harmful, so experts advise following medical advice."],["Hidden hunger","A person can eat enough calories but still lack vitamins and minerals, a condition sometimes called hidden hunger. It affects many people worldwide, especially children and women, and can harm growth, learning and immunity. Diverse diets, fortified foods and supplements help address it."]],
sp:["The chickens of Batavia","In the 1880s the Dutch army doctor Christiaan Eijkman was sent to Batavia, now Jakarta, to find the cause of beriberi, which was killing soldiers and prisoners. He believed it was caused by germs. Then he noticed something odd: chickens kept at his laboratory suddenly developed weakness and paralysis similar to beriberi. He discovered that a cook had been feeding them leftover polished white rice from the hospital. When a new cook switched back to cheaper unpolished rice, the chickens recovered. Eijkman realized that something in the rice husk prevented the disease. His work helped lead to the discovery of vitamins, and in 1929 he shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."],
g:[["Vitamin","A substance the body needs in small amounts"],["Mineral","An element such as iron or calcium needed by the body"],["Deficiency","A lack of something needed"],["Fortification","Adding nutrients to food"]],
f:["There are 13 vitamins.","Scurvy is caused by lack of vitamin C.","Vitamin D can be made in the skin with sunlight."],
fun:"The discovery of a vitamin began with chickens that got sick from eating polished rice."});

ADDART({id:"fever",t:"Fever",c:"Health",i:"🌡️",w:"Fever",r:["immune","flu","dengue","firstaid","measles"],
p:[["A raised temperature","A fever is a body temperature higher than normal."],["Fighting infection","Fever is often a sign the body is fighting germs."],["When to worry","Some fevers need urgent medical attention."]],
l:"A fever is a body temperature higher than the normal range, usually 38 degrees Celsius or above when measured with a thermometer. Normal body temperature is around 37 degrees Celsius, though it varies slightly between people and during the day. Fever is not a disease itself but a sign that the body is responding to something, most often an infection by viruses or bacteria, such as the common cold, influenza, dengue or COVID-19. A part of the brain called the hypothalamus acts as the body’s thermostat and raises the set temperature to help the immune system fight germs. Most fevers go away on their own in a few days, but very high fevers, fevers in young babies, and fevers with warning signs need prompt medical care.",
s:[["How fever happens","When the immune system detects germs, cells release chemicals called pyrogens that travel to the hypothalamus in the brain. The hypothalamus raises the body’s temperature set point. The body then shivers, narrows blood vessels in the skin and feels cold, which raises temperature. When the fever breaks, people often sweat as the body cools down."],["Why fever may help","Many germs grow best at normal body temperature, and a fever can slow them down. Higher temperatures also speed up some immune responses. Animals, including lizards that move to warm places when infected, also develop fevers, suggesting fever evolved as a defense."],["Measuring temperature","Thermometers can measure temperature in the mouth, armpit, ear, forehead or rectum, and readings differ slightly by site. Digital thermometers are common; old glass mercury thermometers are being phased out because mercury is toxic if they break."],["Caring for a fever","Rest, drinking plenty of fluids and wearing light clothing help a person with a fever feel better. Medicines such as paracetamol can lower fever and ease discomfort. Doses must follow the product label or a doctor’s advice, especially for children. Aspirin should not be given to children and teenagers because of the risk of a rare but serious condition."]],
k:[["Fever threshold","About 38 °C"],["Normal temperature","About 37 °C"],["Body thermostat","Hypothalamus"],["Common causes","Viral and bacterial infections"],["Common medicine","Paracetamol"]],
d:[["c. 400 BC","Hippocrates describes fever as a sign of illness"],["1593","Galileo builds an early thermoscope"],["1612","Santorio Santorio measures human temperature with an instrument"],["1868","Carl Wunderlich publishes millions of temperature readings"],["1970s","Scientists show that fever helps animals fight infection"],["2020","A study suggests average body temperature has fallen slightly over time"]],
s2:[["Warning signs","Health experts advise seeking urgent medical help for a fever in a baby younger than three months, a very high fever, a fever lasting more than a few days, or a fever with warning signs such as difficulty breathing, stiff neck, confusion, seizures, a rash that does not fade, severe headache, repeated vomiting or signs of dehydration. In dengue, warning signs include severe stomach pain, bleeding and extreme tiredness."],["Fever and dengue","In the Philippines, fever during the rainy season can be a sign of dengue, spread by mosquitoes. Dengue fever often comes with headache, body pain and rash. Some cases become severe, with bleeding and shock, often as the fever drops. Doctors advise people with fever during dengue season to see a health worker, drink fluids and avoid certain painkillers such as ibuprofen and aspirin unless advised."],["Febrile seizures","Some young children, usually between six months and five years old, have seizures when they have a fever. These febrile seizures can be frightening but are usually brief and do not cause lasting harm. Parents should place the child on their side, not put anything in the mouth, and seek medical advice."],["Is 37 still normal?","In 1868 the German doctor Carl Wunderlich analyzed huge numbers of temperature readings and declared 37 degrees Celsius the normal human temperature. Recent studies in the United States and elsewhere suggest that average body temperatures may now be slightly lower, possibly because people today have fewer chronic infections. Doctors note that normal temperature varies from person to person."],["Fever folk remedies","Many cultures have traditional remedies for fever. In the Philippines, families may sponge the patient with water, place damp cloths on the forehead or give herbal drinks. Lukewarm sponging can help comfort, but experts warn against using alcohol rubs or ice-cold water, which can cause shivering or other harm."],["Fever in history","Before modern medicine, fevers from diseases such as malaria, typhoid and yellow fever killed millions. Doctors once tried treating fevers by bloodletting, which did more harm than good. In the 1800s, the discovery of germs and later of antibiotics and vaccines greatly reduced deaths from fever-causing diseases."]],
sp:["The doctor with a million readings","In the mid-1800s, doctors had no agreed idea of what a normal human temperature was. The German physician Carl Wunderlich decided to find out. Over many years at his hospital in Leipzig, he and his staff took temperature readings from about 25,000 patients, using large thermometers held in the armpit for up to 20 minutes. In 1868 he published his findings, based on more than a million measurements, concluding that normal temperature averaged about 37 degrees Celsius and that fever was a sign of disease rather than a disease itself. His work made the thermometer a standard tool of medicine."],
g:[["Fever","A body temperature above normal"],["Hypothalamus","The part of the brain that controls body temperature"],["Pyrogen","A substance that causes fever"],["Febrile seizure","A seizure in a child caused by fever"]],
f:["Fever is usually a sign of infection.","Normal body temperature is about 37 °C.","Babies under three months with fever need urgent care."],
fun:"Some cold-blooded lizards give themselves a fever by moving to warm spots when they are infected."});

ADDART({id:"hiccups",t:"Hiccups, Sneezes and Yawns",c:"Human Body",i:"🤧",w:"Hiccup",r:["reflexes","lungs","nervous","brain","allergy"],
p:[["Automatic actions","Hiccups, sneezes and yawns happen without us choosing them."],["Contagious yawns","Seeing someone yawn can make you yawn too."],["Sun sneezes","Some people sneeze when they look at bright light."]],
l:"Hiccups, sneezes and yawns are reflexes, automatic actions controlled by the nervous system. A hiccup is a sudden contraction of the diaphragm, the muscle below the lungs, followed by the quick closing of the vocal cords, producing the familiar hic sound. A sneeze is a powerful burst of air through the nose and mouth that clears irritants such as dust, pollen or germs from the nose. A yawn is a deep, slow breath with the mouth wide open, often when tired or bored, and is famously contagious. Although these actions are common, scientists still debate some of their purposes. Most episodes are harmless and brief, but hiccups that last for days or frequent sneezing may need medical attention.",
s:[["Hiccups","Hiccups can be triggered by eating too fast, swallowing air, fizzy drinks, spicy food, excitement or sudden temperature changes. The diaphragm contracts suddenly, pulling air in, and the vocal cords snap shut about a third of a second later, causing the sound. Most hiccups stop within minutes. Babies hiccup often, even before birth."],["Sneezes","When nerves in the nose sense an irritant, they send signals to the brain stem, which coordinates a sneeze. The eyes close, chest muscles tighten and air bursts out, carrying droplets that can spread germs several meters. Covering sneezes with a tissue or elbow helps prevent the spread of colds and flu."],["Yawns","People yawn when tired, bored, waking up or before sleep. Some scientists suggest yawning helps cool the brain or increases alertness by stretching muscles and raising heart rate. The old idea that yawning brings more oxygen to the body has not been supported by studies."],["Contagious yawning","Watching, hearing or even reading about yawning can make people yawn. Contagious yawning is common in humans and has been seen in chimpanzees and dogs. Some researchers link it to empathy and social bonding, since people are more likely to catch yawns from family and friends."]],
k:[["Type of action","Reflexes"],["Hiccup muscle","Diaphragm"],["Purpose of sneezing","Clearing the nose"],["Contagious","Yawning"],["Longest hiccups","About 68 years"]],
d:[["c. 400 BC","Hippocrates notes that sneezing can be a sign in illness"],["1922","Charles Osborne begins hiccupping for 68 years"],["1964","Scientists describe the photic sneeze reflex"],["1990","Osborne’s hiccups finally stop"],["2000s","Studies link contagious yawning with empathy"],["2010s","Research suggests yawning may help cool the brain"]],
s2:[["Cures for hiccups","People try many remedies for hiccups: holding their breath, drinking water quickly or upside down, swallowing sugar, being startled or pulling on the tongue. Some may work by stimulating nerves involved in the reflex, though scientific evidence is limited. In the Philippines, a common folk remedy for babies with hiccups, called sinok, is to place a small piece of thread moistened with saliva on the baby’s forehead."],["The man who hiccupped for 68 years","In 1922 Charles Osborne, a farmer in the United States, began hiccupping while lifting a pig, and he continued for about 68 years, until 1990. At first he hiccupped dozens of times a minute. Despite this, he married twice and had eight children. He holds the record for the longest attack of hiccups."],["Sun sneezing","Some people sneeze when they suddenly step into bright sunlight. This photic sneeze reflex affects a significant minority of people and tends to run in families. Scientists think nerves carrying signals from the eyes and nose may cross-talk in the brain, triggering a sneeze."],["Bless you","Many cultures have words to say after someone sneezes. English speakers say bless you, Spanish speakers say salud, meaning health, and Filipinos often say God bless or Kaawaan ka ng Diyos. Some of these customs may come from old beliefs that sneezing could release the soul or was linked to illness."],["Should you hold a sneeze?","Doctors advise against holding in a sneeze by pinching the nose and closing the mouth, because the pressure can occasionally injure the ears, throat or blood vessels. It is better to sneeze into a tissue or the elbow."],["When to see a doctor","Hiccups lasting more than two days, or that interfere with eating or sleeping, can sometimes be a sign of other medical problems and should be checked. Frequent sneezing with a runny nose may be due to allergies. Excessive yawning without tiredness can occasionally be linked to medical conditions."]],
sp:["The thread on the forehead","When a Filipino baby starts hiccupping after feeding, a grandmother may reach for a sewing basket. She pulls out a short piece of thread, moistens it with a little saliva and gently places it on the baby’s forehead. Sometimes, she says, the hiccups stop soon after. Doctors explain that baby hiccups usually stop on their own within minutes and are harmless, and that burping a baby during and after feeding can help prevent them. Still, the thread remedy remains a familiar part of Filipino family tradition, passed from generation to generation along with many other home remedies."],
g:[["Reflex","An automatic action controlled by the nervous system"],["Diaphragm","The dome-shaped breathing muscle below the lungs"],["Photic sneeze","Sneezing triggered by bright light"],["Contagious","Spreading from one person to another"]],
f:["Hiccups are caused by sudden contractions of the diaphragm.","Yawning is contagious.","Some people sneeze in bright light."],
fun:"One man hiccupped for about 68 years."});

;
