// The Human Body: the voice, balance, polio and blood donation.
ADDART({id:"voice",t:"The Voice and Speech",c:"Human Body",i:"🗣️",w:"Human voice",r:["lungs","ear","brain","langevo","opera"],
p:[["Vibrating folds","Our voices come from vocal folds vibrating in the throat."],["Shaping sounds","Tongue, lips and teeth turn sound into speech."],["Changing voices","Voices change during puberty and with age."]],
l:"The human voice is the sound produced when air from the lungs passes through the larynx, or voice box, in the throat, making two bands of tissue called vocal folds, or vocal cords, vibrate. The throat, mouth and nose shape this sound, and the tongue, lips, teeth and palate turn it into the vowels and consonants of speech. The voice lets people speak, sing, laugh and shout, and carries emotions, identity and meaning. Each person’s voice is unique, depending on the size and shape of their vocal folds and vocal tract. Voices change during puberty, especially in boys, and with aging. Teachers, singers and others who use their voices a lot must take care to avoid strain and damage.",
s:[["The larynx","The larynx sits at the top of the windpipe and is protected by cartilage, including the thyroid cartilage, which forms the Adam’s apple. Inside are the vocal folds. When we breathe, they open; when we speak, they close and vibrate as air pushes through, opening and closing hundreds of times a second."],["Pitch and volume","Pitch depends on how fast the vocal folds vibrate. Longer, thicker folds vibrate more slowly, producing lower voices, which is why adult men usually have deeper voices than women and children. Tightening the folds raises pitch. Pushing more air through makes the voice louder."],["Shaping speech","The sound from the vocal folds alone is a buzz. The throat, mouth and nose act as resonating chambers, shaping it. Moving the tongue, lips and jaw changes the shape of these chambers, creating different vowels, while blocking or narrowing the airflow creates consonants such as p, t, s and m."],["The brain and speech","Speaking requires precise control by the brain. Areas such as Broca’s area plan speech movements, while Wernicke’s area helps understand language. Damage to these areas, for example from a stroke, can affect a person’s ability to speak or understand."]],
k:[["Voice box","Larynx"],["Sound source","Vocal folds"],["Pitch depends on","Vibration speed"],["Speech area in brain","Broca’s area"],["Common problem","Laryngitis"]],
d:[["1854","Singing teacher Manuel García views his own vocal folds with mirrors"],["1861","Paul Broca identifies a brain area linked to speech"],["1877","Thomas Edison records a human voice on his phonograph"],["1940s","Sound spectrographs make voices visible as patterns"],["1998","Surgeons perform the first full larynx transplant"],["2020s","Artificial intelligence can clone human voices"]],
s2:[["Voice change in puberty","During puberty, hormones cause the larynx to grow and the vocal folds to lengthen and thicken, especially in boys. This lowers the voice, and for a while it may crack or break unexpectedly as the person learns to control the new size. Girls’ voices also deepen slightly. The Adam’s apple becomes more visible in many boys."],["Caring for the voice","Teachers, call center agents, singers and others who use their voices for long hours are at risk of voice strain, hoarseness and nodules on the vocal folds. Experts recommend drinking water, resting the voice, avoiding shouting, using microphones when needed and avoiding smoking. In the Philippines, where many people work in call centers and classrooms, voice care is an important workplace health issue."],["Laryngitis","Laryngitis is inflammation of the larynx, often caused by colds, overuse or irritation, making the voice hoarse or temporarily lost. Most cases go away with rest. Hoarseness lasting more than a few weeks, especially in smokers, should be checked by a doctor, since it can sometimes signal serious problems."],["The singing voice","Singers train to control breathing, pitch and resonance. Classical singers are grouped by voice range, such as soprano, alto, tenor and bass. Some singers can reach very high notes using a special register called the whistle register. Filipinos are known for their love of singing, from choirs and kundiman to karaoke and videoke gatherings."],["Seeing the voice","In 1854 Manuel García, a Spanish singing teacher in London, used a dental mirror and sunlight to observe his own vocal folds while singing, inventing the laryngoscope. Today doctors use small cameras and strobe lights to watch vocal folds vibrate in slow motion and diagnose voice problems."],["Voices and technology","Microphones, telephones, radio and recording changed how voices travel. Today voice assistants recognize speech, and artificial intelligence can create synthetic voices or copy real ones. This helps people who have lost their voices to speak with devices, but voice cloning has also been misused in scams and deepfakes."]],
sp:["The teacher with a mirror","In 1854 Manuel García, a famous Spanish singing teacher working in London, wished he could see what happened inside his throat when he sang. One sunny day in Paris, he placed a small dental mirror at the back of his mouth and held a hand mirror to reflect sunlight onto it. To his amazement, he could see his own vocal folds opening, closing and vibrating as he sang different notes. He presented his observations to the Royal Society the following year. Doctors quickly adopted his method, called laryngoscopy, to examine patients’ throats. García lived to be 101 years old and is honored as a founder of the science of the voice."],
g:[["Larynx","The voice box in the throat"],["Vocal folds","Bands of tissue that vibrate to make sound"],["Pitch","How high or low a sound is"],["Laryngitis","Inflammation of the larynx"]],
f:["Vocal folds vibrate to produce the voice.","Voices deepen during puberty.","The tongue and lips shape speech sounds."],
fun:"The inventor of the laryngoscope first saw his own vocal folds using a dental mirror and sunlight."});

ADDART({id:"balance",t:"Balance and the Inner Ear",c:"Human Body",i:"⚖️",w:"Vestibular system",r:["ear","brain","reflexes","astronauts","eye"],
p:[["A hidden sense","Balance is controlled by tiny organs in the inner ear."],["Feeling motion","Fluid-filled canals sense when the head turns."],["Mixed signals","Confusing signals can make us feel sick on boats and cars."]],
l:"Balance, or equilibrium, is the body’s ability to stay upright and steady while standing still or moving. It depends on the vestibular system, a set of tiny organs in the inner ear that sense head movement and gravity, working together with the eyes and with sensors in muscles and joints that tell the brain about body position. The brain combines these signals and adjusts muscles automatically to keep us balanced. When the signals disagree, people may feel dizzy or experience motion sickness, such as on boats, buses or cars. Problems with the inner ear can cause vertigo, a spinning sensation. Astronauts in space must adapt because the vestibular system no longer senses gravity in the usual way.",
s:[["The vestibular system","Deep inside each inner ear, next to the hearing organ, are three looping semicircular canals filled with fluid, set at right angles to one another. When the head turns, the fluid moves and bends tiny hair cells, signaling the direction and speed of rotation. Two other organs, the utricle and saccule, contain tiny crystals that sense gravity and straight-line movement."],["Eyes and body sensors","Vision tells the brain where the body is in relation to its surroundings, and sensors in muscles, tendons and joints report the position of limbs, a sense called proprioception. Standing on one leg with eyes closed is much harder than with eyes open, because the brain loses visual information."],["Dizziness after spinning","When you spin around and stop suddenly, the fluid in the semicircular canals keeps moving for a moment, so the brain thinks you are still turning. This causes dizziness, and the eyes may flick back and forth. The feeling fades as the fluid settles."],["Motion sickness","Motion sickness happens when the inner ear senses motion that the eyes do not see, or the reverse, such as when reading in a moving car or sitting inside a rocking ship’s cabin. Symptoms include nausea, sweating and vomiting. Looking at the horizon, getting fresh air and sitting where motion is smallest can help."]],
k:[["Main balance organ","Vestibular system in the inner ear"],["Semicircular canals","Three in each ear"],["Gravity sensors","Utricle and saccule"],["Body position sense","Proprioception"],["Spinning sensation","Vertigo"]],
d:[["1861","Prosper Ménière links vertigo to the inner ear"],["1914","Róbert Bárány wins the Nobel Prize for work on the vestibular system"],["1961","Cosmonaut Gherman Titov experiences space sickness"],["1980","John Epley develops a maneuver to treat a common vertigo"],["1990s","Vestibular rehabilitation therapy becomes widely used"],["2020s","Scientists study balance problems in astronauts on long missions"]],
s2:[["Seasickness in an archipelago","In the Philippines, many people travel by ferry and banca between islands, and seasickness is a common experience on rough seas. Travelers are often advised to sit in the middle of the boat, look at the horizon, avoid reading and eat a light meal before the trip. Motion sickness medicines can help but may cause drowsiness."],["Ear crystals and vertigo","The most common cause of vertigo is benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, in which tiny crystals in the inner ear come loose and drift into a semicircular canal. Turning the head or lying down then causes brief spinning spells. In 1980 the American doctor John Epley developed a series of head movements, the Epley maneuver, that guides the crystals back into place, often curing the problem."],["Balance in space","In space, the vestibular organs no longer sense gravity normally, so many astronauts feel disoriented and sick during their first days, a condition called space adaptation syndrome. Their brains gradually adapt. When they return to Earth, they may feel unsteady for days or weeks as they readjust to gravity."],["Balance and aging","As people age, the vestibular system, vision and muscle strength may decline, increasing the risk of falls, a leading cause of injury among older adults. Exercises such as tai chi, walking and strength training can improve balance. Removing tripping hazards at home also helps prevent falls."],["Dancers and spinning","Ballet dancers and figure skaters spin rapidly without getting very dizzy. They use a technique called spotting, fixing their eyes on a point and whipping the head around quickly. Studies suggest that years of training also change how their brains process signals from the inner ear."],["Ménière’s disease","In 1861 the French doctor Prosper Ménière showed that vertigo could come from the inner ear rather than the brain, which most doctors had believed. The condition named after him, Ménière’s disease, causes attacks of vertigo, ringing in the ears and hearing loss, probably due to fluid buildup in the inner ear."]],
sp:["The doctor who moved crystals","In the late 1970s, John Epley, an ear specialist in Portland, Oregon, kept seeing patients with brief, violent spells of spinning whenever they rolled over in bed or looked up. He became convinced that tiny loose crystals were drifting in their inner ear canals. Working with models, he designed a series of slow head turns that would move the crystals out of the canal, like rolling a marble through a maze. Many colleagues were skeptical, and he struggled to get his work published. But patients improved, often after a single treatment. Today the Epley maneuver is a standard, simple treatment for one of the most common causes of dizziness."],
g:[["Vestibular system","The balance organs of the inner ear"],["Semicircular canal","A fluid-filled loop that senses rotation"],["Proprioception","The sense of body position"],["Vertigo","A false feeling of spinning"]],
f:["Balance organs are in the inner ear.","Motion sickness comes from mixed signals.","Astronauts must adapt their balance in space."],
fun:"Dancers avoid dizziness by fixing their eyes on one spot as they spin."});

ADDART({id:"polio",t:"Polio",c:"Health",i:"💉",w:"Polio",r:["vacc","pandemic","measles","who","immune"],
p:[["A paralyzing disease","Polio is a virus that can cause permanent paralysis."],["Nearly eradicated","Vaccines have eliminated polio from most of the world."],["Philippine outbreak","Polio returned to the Philippines briefly in 2019."]],
l:"Polio, or poliomyelitis, is a disease caused by the poliovirus, which spreads mainly through contaminated water and food or contact with an infected person’s feces. Most infections cause no symptoms or only mild illness, but in a small share of cases the virus attacks the nerves of the spinal cord and brain, causing permanent paralysis, usually of the legs, and sometimes death when breathing muscles are affected. Polio mostly affects children under five. In the early 20th century, polio epidemics terrified families worldwide. Vaccines developed in the 1950s and 1960s have reduced cases by more than 99 percent. The Philippines was declared polio-free in 2000, but an outbreak caused by a vaccine-derived virus occurred in 2019, leading to mass vaccination campaigns.",
s:[["How polio spreads","Poliovirus lives in the throat and intestines and is shed in feces. It spreads where sanitation is poor, through contaminated hands, water and food. Infected people without symptoms can still spread it. Clean water, sanitation and handwashing reduce transmission, but vaccination is the main protection."],["Paralysis","In about 1 in 200 infections, the virus invades the nervous system and destroys nerve cells that control muscles, leading to sudden weakness or paralysis. Some people recover partly, but many are left with lifelong disability. Decades later, some survivors develop post-polio syndrome, with new weakness and fatigue."],["Two vaccines","The inactivated polio vaccine, developed by Jonas Salk and introduced in 1955, is given by injection. The oral polio vaccine, developed by Albert Sabin and introduced in the early 1960s, is given as drops by mouth and is cheap and easy to use in mass campaigns. Both have been crucial in fighting polio."],["Vaccine-derived polio","The oral vaccine contains weakened live virus. In communities where too few children are vaccinated, this weakened virus can spread from child to child and, rarely, change into a form that causes paralysis. This is called vaccine-derived polio. The solution is to vaccinate enough children so the virus cannot circulate."]],
k:[["Cause","Poliovirus"],["Mostly affects","Children under five"],["Injected vaccine","Salk, 1955"],["Oral vaccine","Sabin, early 1960s"],["Philippines declared polio-free","2000"]],
d:[["1916","A major polio epidemic strikes New York City"],["1928","The iron lung is introduced to help patients breathe"],["1955","The Salk polio vaccine is declared safe and effective"],["1988","The Global Polio Eradication Initiative is launched"],["2000","The Western Pacific region, including the Philippines, is certified polio-free"],["2019","Polio reappears in the Philippines"]],
s2:[["The 2019 Philippine outbreak","In September 2019, nearly two decades after the country was declared polio-free, the Philippines confirmed cases of vaccine-derived polio in children in Lanao del Sur and other areas. Health officials pointed to low vaccination rates, partly linked to declining public trust in vaccines after controversy over a dengue vaccine. The government, UNICEF and WHO launched large campaigns called Sabayang Patak Kontra Polio, giving oral vaccine to millions of children. The outbreak was declared over in 2021."],["The iron lung","When polio paralyzed the breathing muscles, patients could suffocate. In 1928 engineers Philip Drinker and Louis Agassiz Shaw introduced the iron lung, a large metal tank in which a patient lay with only the head outside, while changing air pressure pushed air in and out of the lungs. In the 1950s, hospital wards were filled with rows of iron lungs."],["Fear in the summer","Before vaccines, polio epidemics often struck in summer in temperate countries. Parents kept children away from swimming pools, beaches and crowds. Polio affected rich and poor alike; US President Franklin D. Roosevelt was paralyzed by an illness diagnosed as polio in 1921. Public fundraising campaigns, such as the March of Dimes, supported research."],["Eradication","In 1988, when the Global Polio Eradication Initiative began, polio paralyzed hundreds of thousands of children each year in over 125 countries. Thanks to vaccination campaigns, wild poliovirus types 2 and 3 have been eradicated, and wild type 1 survives only in Pakistan and Afghanistan. Challenges include conflict, misinformation and reaching every child."],["Salk’s gift","When Jonas Salk’s vaccine was announced as safe and effective on April 12, 1955, church bells rang and people celebrated in the streets. Asked in a television interview who owned the patent, Salk famously replied that there was no patent, comparing it to trying to patent the sun. His words have been widely quoted, though legal experts note that the vaccine might not have been patentable anyway."],["Living with polio","Millions of polio survivors live around the world, some using braces, crutches or wheelchairs. Many have become advocates for disability rights and vaccination. Their experiences remind communities why maintaining high vaccination rates matters even when a disease seems to have disappeared."]],
sp:["Drops for every child","In late 2019, health workers in Mindanao set out with cooler boxes full of vaccine vials. They walked from house to house in villages and city neighborhoods, asking parents if they had children under five. Each child received two drops of oral polio vaccine on the tongue, and a finger was marked with purple ink to show they had been vaccinated. Some parents were hesitant, worried by stories they had heard about vaccines; health workers, local leaders and religious figures explained why the drops were important. Over several rounds, millions of children across the Philippines were vaccinated, and by 2021 the outbreak was declared over."],
g:[["Polio","A disease caused by the poliovirus"],["Paralysis","Loss of the ability to move muscles"],["Eradication","Eliminating a disease worldwide"],["Iron lung","A machine that helped polio patients breathe"]],
f:["Polio can cause permanent paralysis.","The Salk vaccine was introduced in 1955.","Polio reappeared in the Philippines in 2019."],
fun:"When the polio vaccine was announced in 1955, church bells rang across the United States."});

ADDART({id:"blooddonation",t:"Blood Donation",c:"Health",i:"🩸",w:"Blood donation",r:["blood","bloodtypes","dengue","surgery","volunteering"],
p:[["Giving life","Donated blood saves lives in surgery, accidents and illness."],["Safe and quick","Donating takes about an hour, with the blood draw itself only minutes."],["Always needed","Blood cannot be manufactured, so donors are always needed."]],
l:"Blood donation is the voluntary giving of blood so that it can be transfused into patients who need it. Donated blood is used in surgeries, for accident victims, women who bleed heavily during childbirth, people with anemia, cancer patients and those with diseases such as dengue or thalassemia. Blood cannot be manufactured, so hospitals depend on healthy donors. A typical whole-blood donation takes about 450 milliliters, and the body replaces the fluid within a day and the red cells within weeks. Donated blood is tested for infections, sorted by blood type and separated into components such as red cells, plasma and platelets. In the Philippines, the Philippine Red Cross, the Department of Health and hospitals run blood services, and voluntary donation is promoted by law.",
s:[["Who can donate","Donors generally must be healthy adults within age and weight limits set by blood services. In the Philippines, donors are usually between 18 and 65 years old, or 16 and 17 with parental consent, and weigh at least 50 kilograms. Donors are screened with questions and a quick check of blood pressure and hemoglobin to make sure donating is safe for them and the patient."],["The donation process","After registering and screening, the donor sits or lies down while a trained staff member inserts a sterile needle into a vein in the arm. Collection usually takes about 10 minutes. Afterward, donors rest, eat a snack and drink fluids. Mild dizziness can occur, but serious problems are rare."],["Blood components","Donated blood is separated into parts. Red blood cells carry oxygen and are given to patients with blood loss or anemia. Platelets help blood clot and are vital for dengue patients with low platelet counts and cancer patients. Plasma, the liquid part, contains proteins that help clotting and fight infections."],["Matching blood types","Blood must be matched between donor and patient according to the ABO and Rh blood group systems; giving the wrong type can cause dangerous reactions. Type O negative red cells can be given to almost anyone in emergencies, so donors with this type are especially valued."]],
k:[["Typical donation","About 450 mL"],["Collection time","About 10 minutes"],["World Blood Donor Day","June 14"],["Philippine law","National Blood Services Act of 1994"],["Universal red cell donor","Type O negative"]],
d:[["1901","Karl Landsteiner discovers the ABO blood groups"],["1914","Methods to store blood using anticoagulants are developed"],["1937","The first hospital blood bank opens in Chicago"],["1994","The Philippines passes the National Blood Services Act"],["2005","The first World Blood Donor Day is held"],["2025","Australian donor James Harrison, who saved many babies, dies"]],
s2:[["Blood donation in the Philippines","The National Blood Services Act of 1994 promotes voluntary, unpaid blood donation and aims to phase out commercial blood banks. The Philippine Red Cross, Department of Health blood centers and hospitals hold blood drives in schools, offices, malls and churches. Shortages often occur during dengue season, when demand for platelets rises, and during holidays, when fewer people donate."],["Why voluntary donation?","Health organizations, including the World Health Organization, promote voluntary, unpaid donation because volunteers who give for altruistic reasons are less likely to hide health risks than paid donors, making the blood supply safer. In the past, some countries relied on paid donors, which was linked to the spread of infections such as hepatitis."],["The first blood banks","During the early 20th century, scientists learned to store blood by adding chemicals that prevent clotting and keeping it cold. In 1937 Dr. Bernard Fantus opened a blood bank at Cook County Hospital in Chicago, coining the term. During World War II, large-scale blood collection saved many wounded soldiers."],["The man with the golden arm","James Harrison of Australia donated blood plasma more than 1,100 times over about 60 years, beginning in 1954. His plasma contained a rare antibody used to make a treatment that prevents a dangerous condition in which a pregnant mother’s immune system attacks her baby’s blood. His donations are credited with helping protect millions of babies. He died in 2025."],["World Blood Donor Day","Every year on June 14, the birthday of Karl Landsteiner, who discovered blood groups, the world celebrates World Blood Donor Day to thank donors and encourage new ones. Events in the Philippines include blood drives and recognition of regular donors."],["Myths and facts","Some people fear that donating blood will make them weak, cause weight gain or infection. In reality, healthy donors recover quickly, and sterile, single-use needles mean donors cannot catch infections from donating. Experts encourage people to learn the facts and become regular donors if they are eligible."]],
sp:["A blood drive in a school gym","On a Saturday morning, a university gym in Iloilo is filled with rows of beds and volunteers in red vests. Students line up to register, answer health questions and have a finger prick test for hemoglobin. One first-time donor, nervous, holds a friend’s hand as the nurse inserts the needle. Ten minutes later, she is sitting up, eating biscuits and drinking juice, proudly wearing a sticker that says she has donated blood. By the end of the day, the blood bags will be tested, separated and sent to hospitals, where they may help a mother after childbirth, a child with dengue or a patient in surgery."],
g:[["Blood donation","Giving blood to help others"],["Transfusion","Giving blood to a patient"],["Platelets","Blood cells that help clotting"],["Plasma","The liquid part of blood"]],
f:["Blood cannot be manufactured.","World Blood Donor Day is June 14.","Type O negative red cells can be given to almost anyone."],
fun:"One Australian man donated plasma more than 1,100 times over about 60 years."});

;
/* data/earth8.js */
// Planet Earth: the Ring of Fire, Kanlaon Volcano, Earth's layers, and El Niño and La Niña.
ADDART({id:"ringoffire",t:"The Pacific Ring of Fire",c:"Geology",i:"🔥",w:"Ring of Fire",r:["tect","volc","quake","tsunami","pinatubo"],
p:[["A ring of volcanoes","The Ring of Fire is a belt of volcanoes and earthquakes around the Pacific."],["Most active zone","It has most of the world’s active volcanoes and strongest earthquakes."],["The Philippines lies on it","The Philippines sits on the western side of the ring."]],
l:"The Pacific Ring of Fire is a horseshoe-shaped belt about 40,000 kilometers long around the edges of the Pacific Ocean, where many of the world’s volcanoes and earthquakes occur. It stretches from New Zealand and Indonesia through the Philippines, Japan and the Kamchatka Peninsula, across the Aleutian Islands to Alaska, and down the western coasts of North, Central and South America. Roughly three-quarters of Earth’s active volcanoes and about 90 percent of its earthquakes occur along it. The Ring of Fire forms where tectonic plates meet, especially where ocean plates slide beneath other plates in a process called subduction. The Philippines lies on the western part of the ring, which explains its many active volcanoes, such as Mayon, Taal, Pinatubo and Kanlaon, and its frequent earthquakes.",
s:[["Subduction","Most of the Ring of Fire marks subduction zones, where a dense oceanic plate sinks beneath another plate into the mantle. As the sinking plate descends, water released from it lowers the melting point of the surrounding rock, creating magma that rises to form chains of volcanoes. Deep ocean trenches form where the plate bends downward."],["Earthquakes","The movement of plates along subduction zones builds up enormous stress, released suddenly in earthquakes. The largest earthquakes ever recorded, called megathrust earthquakes, happened on the Ring of Fire, such as the 1960 Chile earthquake, magnitude 9.5, and the 2011 Tōhoku earthquake in Japan, magnitude 9.0–9.1."],["Tsunamis","When an undersea earthquake suddenly lifts or drops the seafloor, it can push up the water above, creating tsunamis that cross entire oceans. The Pacific Tsunami Warning Center, set up in 1949, monitors earthquakes and warns countries around the Pacific, including the Philippines."],["Volcanic arcs","Chains of volcanoes called volcanic arcs line the Ring of Fire. Some form island arcs, such as the Aleutians, the Philippines and Japan, while others form mountain ranges on land, such as the Andes and the Cascades. Many of these volcanoes are tall, steep stratovolcanoes that can erupt explosively."]],
k:[["Length","About 40,000 km"],["Share of active volcanoes","About three-quarters"],["Share of earthquakes","About 90 percent"],["Main process","Subduction"],["Largest recorded earthquake","Chile, 1960, magnitude 9.5"]],
d:[["1883","Krakatoa erupts in Indonesia"],["1960","The largest recorded earthquake strikes Chile"],["1980","Mount St. Helens erupts in the United States"],["1991","Mount Pinatubo erupts in the Philippines"],["2011","An earthquake and tsunami strike Japan"],["2022","Hunga Tonga–Hunga Ha’apai erupts in Tonga"]],
s2:[["The Philippines on the ring","The Philippines is caught between the Philippine Sea Plate to the east and the Eurasian Plate and smaller plates to the west. Subduction zones such as the Philippine Trench and the Manila Trench, along with the long Philippine Fault, make the country one of the most seismically active in the world. The Philippine Institute of Volcanology and Seismology, or PHIVOLCS, monitors active volcanoes and earthquakes."],["Living with risk","Around the Ring of Fire, hundreds of millions of people live near active volcanoes or fault lines, often because volcanic soils are fertile and coasts offer good harbors. Countries such as Japan, Chile and the Philippines invest in building codes, warning systems, evacuation drills and public education to reduce disaster risk."],["The 2022 Tonga eruption","In January 2022 the underwater volcano Hunga Tonga–Hunga Ha’apai in the South Pacific erupted with enormous force, sending a plume into the upper atmosphere and creating a pressure wave that circled the globe several times. It triggered tsunamis across the Pacific and injected huge amounts of water vapor into the stratosphere. Tonga was cut off from the world for days when its undersea internet cable was damaged."],["Benefits of volcanoes","Volcanic activity also brings benefits. Volcanic soils are rich in minerals, supporting farming in places such as the slopes of Mayon and in Java. Underground heat provides geothermal energy, and the Philippines is one of the world’s leading producers of geothermal power. Volcanic landscapes attract tourists."],["Not a perfect ring","The Ring of Fire is not a continuous circle. It includes many separate plate boundaries, and some parts, such as the edge of Antarctica, have little activity. Some scientists prefer to call it a belt of plate boundaries rather than a ring."],["Famous eruptions","The 1815 eruption of Tambora in Indonesia, on the Sunda arc often linked to the Ring of Fire, was the deadliest recorded eruption and led to the Year Without a Summer in 1816. The 1991 eruption of Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines was one of the largest of the 20th century and cooled global temperatures slightly for about two years."]],
sp:["The day Pinatubo awoke","For about 500 years, Mount Pinatubo in Zambales had been quiet, covered in forest and home to Aeta communities. In early 1991, steam explosions and earthquakes began. Scientists from PHIVOLCS and the United States Geological Survey installed instruments, studied the volcano’s past eruptions and warned that a large eruption was likely. Tens of thousands of people were evacuated, including from the nearby US Clark Air Base. On June 15, 1991, Pinatubo erupted with massive force, while Typhoon Yunya struck at the same time, mixing ash with rain. Although hundreds died, mostly from roofs collapsing under wet ash, the warnings and evacuations saved many thousands of lives, a lesson for volcano monitoring around the Ring of Fire."],
g:[["Ring of Fire","A belt of volcanoes and earthquakes around the Pacific"],["Subduction","One plate sinking beneath another"],["Volcanic arc","A chain of volcanoes formed by subduction"],["Megathrust earthquake","A very large earthquake at a subduction zone"]],
f:["The Ring of Fire is about 40,000 km long.","Most of the world’s earthquakes occur on it.","The Philippines lies on the Ring of Fire."],
fun:"The pressure wave from the 2022 Tonga eruption circled the Earth several times."});

ADDART({id:"kanlaon",t:"Kanlaon Volcano",c:"Geology",i:"🌋",w:"Kanlaon",r:["volc","taal","mayon","ringoffire","phfolk"],
p:[["Negros’s giant","Kanlaon is the highest mountain in the Visayas."],["Very active","It is one of the most active volcanoes in the Philippines."],["Recent eruptions","Eruptions since 2024 have forced thousands to evacuate."]],
l:"Kanlaon, also spelled Canlaon, is an active stratovolcano on the island of Negros in the central Philippines, rising about 2,435 meters above sea level, making it the highest peak in the Visayas. It lies between the provinces of Negros Occidental and Negros Oriental and is surrounded by forests protected as Mount Kanlaon Natural Park. Kanlaon is one of the most active volcanoes in the country, with frequent ash explosions, and is monitored by PHIVOLCS. An explosive eruption in June 2024 and a larger one in December 2024 led authorities to raise the alert level and evacuate tens of thousands of residents from nearby towns, including Canlaon City and La Castellana. Moderately explosive eruptions continued into 2025 and 2026. The volcano is also sacred in local traditions.",
s:[["The volcano","Kanlaon is a stratovolcano built by layers of lava and ash, with several craters at its summit. Its eruptions are mostly short, explosive bursts of steam and ash, sometimes producing pyroclastic density currents, fast-moving clouds of hot ash and gas, and lahars, mudflows of volcanic material carried by rain."],["Recent activity","On June 3, 2024, Kanlaon erupted explosively, sending an ash plume kilometers into the sky. On December 9, 2024, a stronger eruption sent ash and pyroclastic flows down its slopes, and PHIVOLCS raised the alert to level 3, ordering evacuations within a wide danger zone. The volcano produced further explosive eruptions in 2025. The alert was later lowered to level 2, under which it remained into mid-2026 amid occasional moderately explosive eruptions and ash emissions."],["Effects on communities","Ash fall has damaged crops, closed schools and disrupted flights, and lahars threaten towns along river channels. Evacuees stayed for months in evacuation centers, and farmers lost income from sugarcane, vegetables and livestock. Local governments, the national government and aid groups provided food, shelter and support."],["Mount Kanlaon Natural Park","The forests around Kanlaon are protected as a natural park and are home to rare wildlife, including the Visayan spotted deer, the Visayan warty pig, hornbills and many endemic plants. Before recent eruptions, hikers climbed its trails, though climbing is restricted whenever volcanic activity increases."]],
k:[["Location","Negros Island"],["Height","About 2,435 m"],["Type","Stratovolcano"],["Highest peak in","The Visayas"],["Monitored by","PHIVOLCS"]],
d:[["1866","The earliest recorded eruption of Kanlaon"],["1934","Mount Kanlaon National Park is established"],["1996","A sudden eruption kills hikers near the summit"],["2024","Explosive eruptions in June and December force evacuations"],["2025","Further explosive eruptions occur"],["2026","Kanlaon remains under Alert Level 2 with occasional eruptions"]],
s2:[["The 1996 tragedy","In August 1996, a group of hikers was climbing near Kanlaon’s summit when the volcano suddenly erupted with little warning, showering the area with rocks and ash. Three hikers died and others were injured. The disaster led to stricter rules on climbing active volcanoes and highlighted how dangerous even small eruptions can be."],["Kanlaon in legend","In the traditions of the people of Negros, Kanlaon is linked to Laon, a powerful deity, and the mountain was considered sacred. One legend tells of a hero who defeated a fierce dragon or monster living on the mountain. Some indigenous and local communities still regard the mountain with respect and perform rituals."],["Monitoring Kanlaon","PHIVOLCS watches Kanlaon using seismometers to detect volcanic earthquakes, instruments that measure ground swelling, cameras and gas measurements of sulfur dioxide. Sharp increases in sulfur dioxide or swelling can signal rising magma. Alert levels range from 0 to 5 and tell local governments what actions to take."],["Lahars","After eruptions, ash deposits on the volcano’s slopes can be washed down by heavy rain, forming lahars that flood rivers and bury roads and farms. Lahars from Kanlaon have threatened communities in La Castellana, Canlaon City and other towns, especially during typhoons and the rainy season."],["Living near a volcano","Thousands of families farm the fertile slopes around Kanlaon, growing vegetables, coffee and sugarcane. Many are reluctant to leave their homes and livestock, even during eruptions. Officials work to balance safety with people’s livelihoods, using drills, early warnings and evacuation plans."],["Negros’s other volcanoes","Negros has other volcanic features, including Mount Talinis, also called Cuernos de Negros, near Dumaguete, which has geothermal power plants. The island’s volcanic soils have helped make it the center of the Philippine sugar industry."]],
sp:["The December eruption","On the afternoon of December 9, 2024, residents of towns around Kanlaon heard a roar as the volcano erupted, sending a towering column of ash several kilometers into the sky. Pyroclastic flows rushed down its southern slopes. Within hours, PHIVOLCS raised the alert level to 3, and local officials began evacuating families within the danger zone. Buses and trucks carried people, along with some of their pigs, goats and belongings, to schools and gyms turned into evacuation centers. Ash fell on towns and farms, and some flights were canceled. Many evacuees stayed for weeks or months as the volcano remained restless, waiting for the day they could return home."],
g:[["Stratovolcano","A steep volcano built of layers of lava and ash"],["Pyroclastic flow","A fast cloud of hot ash and gas"],["Lahar","A volcanic mudflow"],["Alert level","A warning level for volcanic activity"]],
f:["Kanlaon is the highest peak in the Visayas.","It erupted explosively in 2024.","PHIVOLCS monitors Kanlaon."],
fun:"Kanlaon’s name is linked to Laon, a powerful deity in the traditions of Negros."});

ADDART({id:"earthlayers",t:"Inside the Earth",c:"Geology",i:"🌍",w:"Structure of Earth",r:["tect","volc","quake","magnet","rocks","pangaea"],
p:[["Layers like an onion","Earth has a crust, mantle, outer core and inner core."],["A hot center","The inner core is about as hot as the Sun’s surface."],["Studied with earthquakes","Scientists learn about Earth’s inside from earthquake waves."]],
l:"Earth is made of layers. The outermost is the crust, a thin shell of rock beneath our feet, between about 5 and 70 kilometers thick. Below it is the mantle, a thick layer of hot rock nearly 2,900 kilometers deep that slowly flows over millions of years. Deeper still is the core, made mostly of iron and nickel: a liquid outer core and a solid inner core with temperatures of around 5,000 degrees Celsius or more, similar to the surface of the Sun. No one has ever drilled beyond the crust; the deepest hole ever drilled reached just over 12 kilometers. Scientists learn about Earth’s interior mainly by studying how earthquake waves travel through it. The movement of the liquid outer core generates Earth’s magnetic field.",
s:[["The crust","There are two types of crust. Oceanic crust, under the oceans, is thin, dense and made mostly of basalt. Continental crust is thicker, lighter and older, made largely of granite-like rocks. The crust and the uppermost mantle together form rigid tectonic plates that move slowly across Earth’s surface."],["The mantle","The mantle makes up most of Earth’s volume. Though made of solid rock, it is so hot and under such pressure that it flows extremely slowly, like very thick putty. Heat rising from the core drives slow currents in the mantle that help move tectonic plates and feed volcanoes."],["The outer core","The outer core is a layer of liquid iron and nickel about 2,200 kilometers thick. As this molten metal swirls, driven by heat and Earth’s rotation, it acts like a giant dynamo, generating Earth’s magnetic field, which protects life from harmful solar particles and guides compasses."],["The inner core","At Earth’s center is the inner core, a solid ball of iron and nickel about 1,220 kilometers in radius. Although it is extremely hot, enormous pressure keeps it solid. It is slowly growing as the outer core cools and freezes onto it."]],
k:[["Layers","Crust, mantle, outer core, inner core"],["Crust thickness","About 5–70 km"],["Depth to core","About 2,900 km"],["Core metals","Iron and nickel"],["Deepest drill hole","About 12.3 km, Kola, Russia"]],
d:[["1909","Andrija Mohorovičić discovers the boundary between crust and mantle"],["1914","Beno Gutenberg calculates the depth of the core"],["1936","Inge Lehmann discovers the solid inner core"],["1989","The Kola Superdeep Borehole reaches about 12.3 km"],["2005","Japan’s drilling ship Chikyu begins operations"],["2023","Scientists report that the inner core’s rotation may have changed"]],
s2:[["Reading earthquake waves","Earthquakes send waves through Earth. P waves can travel through solids and liquids, while S waves cannot pass through liquids. By recording these waves at stations around the world, scientists discovered that the outer core is liquid, because S waves disappear in a shadow zone on the far side of the planet. Changes in wave speed reveal boundaries between layers."],["Inge Lehmann’s discovery","In 1936 the Danish seismologist Inge Lehmann studied waves from a large earthquake in New Zealand and noticed faint waves arriving where none were expected. She concluded that Earth must have a solid inner core inside the liquid outer core, reflecting waves. Her discovery, published in a paper with one of the shortest titles in science, P′, was later confirmed and made her one of the most important geophysicists of the 20th century."],["The deepest hole","From 1970 to the early 1990s, Soviet scientists drilled the Kola Superdeep Borehole in northwestern Russia, reaching a depth of about 12.3 kilometers in 1989, the deepest artificial point on Earth. They found temperatures of about 180 degrees Celsius, much hotter than expected, and water deep in the rock. Still, they had only penetrated about a third of the way through the continental crust."],["Mantle rocks in the Philippines","In some places, pieces of oceanic crust and mantle have been pushed up onto land by tectonic forces, forming rock sequences called ophiolites. The Zambales Ophiolite in Luzon is one of the best-studied examples in the world, allowing Filipino and foreign geologists to examine rocks that formed deep beneath an ancient ocean. These rocks also contain minerals such as chromite and nickel."],["A spinning inner core","The inner core may rotate at a slightly different speed from the rest of the planet. In 2023 Chinese scientists reported evidence that its rotation relative to the mantle had paused and might be reversing, part of a possible cycle lasting decades. Other scientists disagree about how to interpret the data, and research continues."],["Journey to the center","In 1864 the French writer Jules Verne published Journey to the Center of the Earth, imagining explorers descending through volcanic tunnels to a hidden world. In reality, the heat and pressure deep inside Earth make such a journey impossible, but scientists continue to explore the interior using waves, lab experiments and computer models."]],
sp:["A shadow on the far side","In the early 1900s, seismologists around the world began recording earthquakes on new instruments. They noticed something puzzling: when a large earthquake struck, stations on the opposite side of Earth did not receive certain waves, called S waves, which cannot travel through liquids. In 1914 the German scientist Beno Gutenberg calculated that this shadow was caused by a core about 2,900 kilometers below the surface. Later, scientists realized that the outer core must be liquid. In 1936 Inge Lehmann found that faint waves bounced off something solid inside it, revealing the inner core. Without ever visiting, scientists had mapped the heart of the planet using the shaking of earthquakes."],
g:[["Crust","Earth’s thin outer layer"],["Mantle","The thick layer of hot rock beneath the crust"],["Core","Earth’s iron-rich center"],["Ophiolite","A section of oceanic crust and mantle pushed onto land"]],
f:["Earth’s outer core is liquid.","The inner core is solid.","No one has drilled through the crust."],
fun:"The deepest hole ever drilled is only about one-third of the way through the continental crust."});

ADDART({id:"elnino",t:"El Niño and La Niña",c:"Weather",i:"🌊",w:"El Niño–Southern Oscillation",r:["climate","pacific","phtyph","monsoon","rice"],
p:[["Pacific seesaw","El Niño and La Niña are changes in Pacific Ocean temperatures."],["Worldwide weather","They change weather patterns around the world."],["Dry or wet years","El Niño often brings drought to the Philippines, La Niña more rain."]],
l:"El Niño and La Niña are opposite phases of a natural climate pattern called the El Niño–Southern Oscillation, or ENSO, which involves changes in sea surface temperatures and winds across the tropical Pacific Ocean. During El Niño, the central and eastern Pacific become unusually warm, while during La Niña they become unusually cool. These shifts occur every few years and can last from several months to over a year. Because the Pacific is so vast, ENSO affects weather around the world, influencing rainfall, droughts, floods, typhoons, crops and fisheries. In the Philippines, El Niño usually brings below-normal rainfall and drought, harming rice and corn harvests and water supplies, while La Niña tends to bring heavier rains and more tropical cyclones.",
s:[["Normal conditions","Normally, trade winds blow from east to west across the tropical Pacific, pushing warm surface water toward Asia and Australia. Around the Philippines and Indonesia, the warm water fuels clouds and heavy rain. Off South America, cold, nutrient-rich water rises from the deep, supporting rich fisheries."],["El Niño","During El Niño, trade winds weaken or reverse. Warm water spreads eastward toward the Americas. Rain clouds shift east, so places such as the Philippines, Indonesia and Australia often become drier, while Peru and Ecuador may get floods. The upwelling of cold water off South America weakens, harming fisheries."],["La Niña","During La Niña, trade winds become stronger than normal, pushing even more warm water toward Asia. The western Pacific, including the Philippines, often gets heavier rain, flooding and landslides, while parts of South America may face drought. La Niña can also affect the number and paths of typhoons."],["Forecasting ENSO","Scientists monitor ENSO using buoys across the Pacific, satellites and computer models. Weather agencies, including PAGASA in the Philippines, issue El Niño and La Niña watches and alerts months in advance, helping farmers, water managers and governments prepare."]],
k:[["Full name","El Niño–Southern Oscillation"],["El Niño","Warmer eastern Pacific"],["La Niña","Cooler eastern Pacific"],["Typical cycle","Every 2 to 7 years"],["Philippine monitor","PAGASA"]],
d:[["1890s","Peruvian scientists describe the warm current fishermen call El Niño"],["1920s","Gilbert Walker describes the Southern Oscillation"],["1969","Jacob Bjerknes links ocean warming and the Southern Oscillation"],["1997","One of the strongest El Niño events of the century begins"],["2015","A very strong El Niño develops"],["2023","An El Niño begins, contributing to record global heat in 2023 and 2024"]],
s2:[["The name","Fishermen in Peru noticed that warm currents sometimes appeared off their coast around Christmas, reducing fish catches. They called it El Niño, Spanish for the boy child, referring to the infant Jesus. Scientists later used La Niña, meaning the girl, for the opposite cool phase."],["El Niño in the Philippines","Strong El Niño events have caused serious droughts in the Philippines, such as in 1997–1998, 2015–2016 and 2023–2024. Rice and corn fields dried up, dams and reservoirs fell to low levels, causing water shortages in Metro Manila, and some provinces declared states of calamity. The government responds with cloud seeding, irrigation, drought-tolerant crops and aid for farmers."],["La Niña in the Philippines","La Niña years often bring above-normal rainfall, more frequent floods and landslides, and sometimes more typhoons making landfall in the Philippines. The heavy rains can replenish dams but also damage crops and homes. Disaster agencies prepare evacuation plans and warn communities in flood-prone areas."],["Global effects","El Niño tends to raise global average temperatures temporarily, while La Niña tends to lower them. Many of the hottest years on record occurred during or after El Niño events. ENSO also affects Atlantic hurricanes, Indian monsoons, African rainfall and wildfires in Australia and Indonesia."],["Walker’s discovery","In the early 20th century, the British scientist Gilbert Walker, working in India to predict monsoon failures, discovered a seesaw in air pressure between the eastern and western Pacific, which he called the Southern Oscillation. In 1969 the Norwegian-American meteorologist Jacob Bjerknes showed how it was linked to ocean temperatures, explaining El Niño."],["ENSO and climate change","Scientists are studying how global warming may change ENSO. Some studies suggest that extreme El Niño and La Niña events could become more frequent, while others find the evidence uncertain. Even without changes to ENSO itself, warmer temperatures can make the droughts and floods it brings more severe."]],
sp:["Dry fields in Mindanao","In early 2024, during a strong El Niño, farmers in parts of Mindanao and Luzon watched their rice fields crack under the sun. The rains that usually came had failed for months. Irrigation canals ran dry, and some farmers lost entire harvests. In towns and cities, water utilities reduced pressure and scheduled interruptions. Provincial governments declared states of calamity to release emergency funds. Agricultural officials distributed seeds of drought-tolerant crops and encouraged planting vegetables that need less water. When the rains finally returned and El Niño faded later in the year, weather experts were already watching the Pacific for signs of La Niña."],
g:[["El Niño","A warm phase of Pacific Ocean temperatures"],["La Niña","A cool phase of Pacific Ocean temperatures"],["Trade winds","Steady east-to-west tropical winds"],["Upwelling","Cold deep water rising to the surface"]],
f:["El Niño often brings drought to the Philippines.","La Niña often brings heavy rain.","El Niño means the boy child in Spanish."],
fun:"El Niño was named by Peruvian fishermen who noticed the warm current around Christmas."});

;
/* data/earth9.js */
// Planet Earth: thunderstorms and lightning, rainbows, rivers and the Dead Sea.
ADDART({id:"thunderstorms",t:"Thunderstorms and Lightning",c:"Weather",i:"⛈️",w:"Thunderstorm",r:["staticelec","climate","storms","monsoon","firstaid"],
p:[["Towering clouds","Thunderstorms grow from tall cumulonimbus clouds."],["Lightning and thunder","Lightning heats the air so fast that it explodes into thunder."],["Stay safe","When thunder roars, go indoors."]],
l:"A thunderstorm is a storm with lightning and thunder, produced by a tall, towering cloud called a cumulonimbus. Thunderstorms form when warm, moist air rises rapidly, cools and condenses into clouds, releasing heat that drives the air even higher. Inside, collisions between ice crystals and hailstones separate electric charges until a giant spark, lightning, jumps between cloud and ground or within clouds. Lightning heats the air to about 30,000 degrees Celsius, causing it to expand explosively and create the sound of thunder. Thunderstorms can bring heavy rain, strong winds, hail and sometimes tornadoes. At any moment, around 2,000 thunderstorms are active around the world. In the Philippines, afternoon thunderstorms are common, especially during the rainy season.",
s:[["How they form","A thunderstorm needs moisture, unstable air that rises easily and a trigger, such as heating by the Sun, mountains forcing air upward or a cold front. In the tropics, the strong afternoon sun often heats the ground, causing air to rise and storm clouds to build by late afternoon."],["Life of a storm","A typical thunderstorm goes through three stages: the growing stage, with rising air building the cloud; the mature stage, with heavy rain, lightning and strong downdrafts; and the dissipating stage, when downdrafts cut off the rising warm air and the storm weakens. Many storms last less than an hour, though some organized storms last much longer."],["Lightning","Lightning is a massive electrical discharge. Most lightning stays within clouds, but cloud-to-ground strikes are the most dangerous. A single bolt can carry tens of thousands of amperes and is several kilometers long. Worldwide, lightning flashes dozens of times every second."],["Thunder","Thunder is the sound of air expanding rapidly after being heated by lightning. Because light travels much faster than sound, we see lightning before we hear thunder. Counting the seconds between a flash and the thunder and dividing by three gives the approximate distance in kilometers."]],
k:[["Storm cloud","Cumulonimbus"],["Lightning temperature","About 30,000 °C"],["Storms active worldwide at once","About 2,000"],["Distance rule","3 seconds ≈ 1 km"],["Safety rule","When thunder roars, go indoors"]],
d:[["1752","Benjamin Franklin’s kite experiment links lightning to electricity"],["1880s","Lightning rods become common on buildings"],["1989","Scientists first photograph sprites above thunderstorms"],["1990s","Satellites begin mapping lightning worldwide"],["2016","A weather satellite with a lightning mapper is launched"],["2022","The longest lightning flash on record, about 768 km, is certified"]],
s2:[["Thunderstorms in the Philippines","During the southwest monsoon and the rainy season, the Philippines experiences frequent thunderstorms, often in the afternoon and evening. PAGASA issues thunderstorm advisories and warnings for heavy rain, lightning and strong winds that may cause flash floods. Lightning strikes have killed farmers in fields, fishermen at sea and people sheltering under trees."],["Lightning safety","Experts advise that no place outdoors is safe during a thunderstorm. People should go inside a sturdy building or a hard-topped vehicle, stay away from open fields, hilltops, tall isolated trees, water and metal fences, and wait 30 minutes after the last thunder before going out. Indoors, avoid using corded phones, plumbing and electrical appliances during storms."],["Hail","In strong thunderstorms, rising air carries raindrops high into freezing parts of the cloud, where they turn into ice and grow layer by layer as they collide with supercooled water. When they become too heavy, they fall as hail. Hail is uncommon in the Philippines’ lowlands but occasionally falls during strong storms, especially in higher areas such as Baguio."],["Sprites and elves","Above large thunderstorms, brief flashes of red and blue light called sprites, jets and elves shoot up toward space. Pilots reported seeing them for decades, but scientists were skeptical until 1989, when researchers accidentally captured a sprite on camera. Astronauts on the International Space Station now photograph them regularly."],["Record lightning","Some places have extremely frequent lightning. Lake Maracaibo in Venezuela experiences storms on many nights of the year, producing almost continuous lightning called Catatumbo lightning. In 2022 the World Meteorological Organization certified a single lightning flash stretching about 768 kilometers across the southern United States as the longest ever recorded."],["Struck and survived","Most people struck by lightning survive, though many suffer lasting injuries such as burns, nerve damage and memory problems. An American park ranger, Roy Sullivan, was struck by lightning seven times between 1942 and 1977 and survived each time. Doctors advise giving first aid immediately to lightning victims; they do not carry an electric charge and are safe to touch."]],
sp:["Counting the seconds","A family is having a picnic in a park in Davao on a hot afternoon when dark clouds begin to tower over the mountains. A flash of light streaks across the sky. The children start counting: one, two, three, four, five, six. Then comes a deep rumble of thunder. Their father explains that dividing six by three means the lightning was about two kilometers away, close enough to be dangerous. Quickly, they pack up and run to their car, closing the doors just as heavy rain begins to pour. Inside, they watch the storm safely, counting the seconds between each flash and boom as the storm moves past."],
g:[["Cumulonimbus","A tall thunderstorm cloud"],["Lightning","A giant electric spark in the atmosphere"],["Thunder","The sound caused by lightning heating the air"],["Hail","Balls of ice that fall from storm clouds"]],
f:["Lightning is hotter than the Sun’s surface.","Light travels faster than sound.","No place outdoors is safe in a thunderstorm."],
fun:"One lightning flash stretched about 768 kilometers, longer than the road trip from Manila to Legazpi."});

ADDART({id:"rainbows",t:"Rainbows",c:"Weather",i:"🌈",w:"Rainbow",r:["light","climate","newton","illusions","wcycle"],
p:[["Sunlight and rain","Rainbows form when sunlight shines through raindrops."],["A circle of color","A rainbow is really a circle, usually cut off by the ground."],["A second bow","Sometimes a second, fainter rainbow appears with colors reversed."]],
l:"A rainbow is an arc of colored light that appears in the sky when sunlight shines on raindrops or mist while the Sun is behind the observer. Each raindrop acts like a tiny prism, bending, reflecting and splitting white sunlight into its colors, from red on the outside of the arc to violet on the inside. Rainbows always appear opposite the Sun, at an angle of about 42 degrees from the point directly opposite it, so they are most often seen in the morning or late afternoon when the Sun is low. A rainbow is actually a full circle, but the ground usually hides the lower half. Rainbows have inspired myths, religious symbols and scientific discoveries in cultures around the world. In Filipino, a rainbow is called bahaghari.",
s:[["How rainbows form","When sunlight enters a raindrop, it slows down and bends, or refracts. Different colors bend by slightly different amounts, spreading out. The light reflects off the back of the drop and bends again as it leaves. Each color exits at a slightly different angle, so we see red from drops higher in the sky and violet from drops lower down."],["Colors","Rainbow colors are often listed as red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet, but in reality the colors blend smoothly into each other. Isaac Newton chose seven colors partly to match the seven notes of the musical scale. Different cultures divide the rainbow into different numbers of colors."],["Double rainbows","Sometimes light reflects twice inside raindrops, producing a second, fainter rainbow outside the first, with its colors reversed. The sky between the two bows looks darker, an effect called Alexander’s band, named after the Greek philosopher Alexander of Aphrodisias, who described it around 200 AD."],["Your own rainbow","Because a rainbow depends on the angle between the Sun, the raindrops and the observer’s eyes, each person sees a slightly different rainbow formed by different drops. This is why you can never reach the end of a rainbow; it moves as you move."]],
k:[["Filipino name","Bahaghari"],["Angle","About 42°"],["Outer color","Red"],["Inner color","Violet"],["Second bow","Colors reversed"]],
d:[["c. 200 AD","Alexander of Aphrodisias describes the dark band between double rainbows"],["c. 1300s","Theodoric of Freiberg studies rainbows using glass spheres of water"],["1637","René Descartes explains the rainbow’s angle"],["1666","Isaac Newton begins experiments splitting light with prisms"],["1704","Newton publishes Opticks"],["1978","A rainbow flag is designed as a symbol of LGBTQ+ pride"]],
s2:[["The science of Descartes and Newton","In 1637 the French philosopher René Descartes traced the paths of light rays through water drops and explained why rainbows appear at about 42 degrees. Isaac Newton later showed with prisms that white light is made of many colors, explaining why rainbows are colored. Their work was a landmark in the science of optics."],["Rainbows in myth and religion","In the Bible, God places a rainbow in the sky after the great flood as a sign of his promise never again to destroy the earth by water. In Norse mythology, the rainbow bridge Bifröst connects the world of humans with the realm of the gods. Many indigenous cultures, including in Australia and the Philippines, tell stories of rainbow serpents or spirits."],["Bahaghari","The Filipino word bahaghari is often explained as coming from words meaning a king’s loincloth, imagining the rainbow as a colorful garment of a great ruler. In some Philippine folk beliefs, pointing at a rainbow was thought to bring bad luck. Rainbows are also used as symbols of hope and diversity in Filipino art and media."],["Moonbows and fogbows","Rainbows can also form from moonlight, creating faint moonbows that usually look white to the eye because our vision sees little color in dim light. Fogbows form in fog and mist and are nearly white because the tiny droplets blur the colors. Waterfalls often produce rainbows in their spray on sunny days."],["Full-circle rainbows","From an airplane, a mountaintop or a tall building, people can sometimes see a full circular rainbow, because the ground does not block the lower half. Pilots occasionally see a small circular rainbow around the shadow of their plane on clouds, called a glory."],["A symbol of hope and diversity","Rainbows have become symbols of peace, hope and diversity. The rainbow flag designed by artist Gilbert Baker in 1978 became a widely recognized symbol of LGBTQ+ pride. Rainbow flags have also been used by peace movements, and during the COVID-19 pandemic, children in many countries displayed rainbow drawings in windows as signs of hope."]],
sp:["Newton’s dark room","In 1666, while Cambridge University was closed because of the plague, young Isaac Newton conducted experiments at his family home. He darkened a room, made a small hole in the window shutter to let in a beam of sunlight and placed a glass prism in its path. On the opposite wall appeared a long band of colors. Many people then believed that prisms added color to light. Newton used a second prism to recombine the colors into white light, showing that white light itself contains all the colors. His discoveries explained the rainbow and became part of his book Opticks, published in 1704."],
g:[["Refraction","The bending of light as it passes into a new material"],["Prism","A piece of glass that splits light into colors"],["Spectrum","The range of colors in light"],["Bahaghari","The Filipino word for rainbow"]],
f:["Rainbows appear opposite the Sun.","A rainbow is really a full circle.","In a double rainbow, the second bow’s colors are reversed."],
fun:"No two people ever see exactly the same rainbow."});

ADDART({id:"rivers",t:"How Rivers Work",c:"Geography",i:"🏞️",w:"River",r:["wcycle","deltas","lakes","waterfalls","amazonriver"],
p:[["From source to sea","Rivers carry water from highlands to lakes and seas."],["Shaping the land","They carve valleys and build deltas."],["Lifelines","Civilizations grew along rivers."]],
l:"A river is a large natural stream of fresh water flowing in a channel toward an ocean, sea, lake or another river. Rivers begin at a source, such as a spring, melting glacier, lake or rainfall in mountains, and are joined by smaller streams called tributaries as they flow downhill. The whole area that drains into a river is its basin or watershed. Rivers are powerful shapers of the land, eroding valleys and canyons, carrying sediment and depositing it in floodplains and deltas. They provide water for drinking, farming and industry, as well as fish, transport and energy. Many of the world’s first civilizations grew along rivers. The Cagayan River is the longest in the Philippines, and the Pasig River flows through Metro Manila.",
s:[["Parts of a river","The upper course of a river is usually steep and fast, cutting narrow V-shaped valleys and creating rapids and waterfalls. In its middle course, the river widens and begins to wind. In its lower course, it flows slowly across flat land toward its mouth, where it may form a delta or an estuary, where fresh and salt water mix."],["Meanders and oxbows","As a river crosses flat land, it often curves in loops called meanders. Water flows faster on the outside of a bend, eroding the bank, and slower on the inside, depositing sediment. Over time, meanders grow, and sometimes the river cuts across a loop during a flood, leaving behind a curved lake called an oxbow lake."],["Floods and floodplains","When heavy rain or melting snow overfills a river, it spills over its banks onto the surrounding floodplain, depositing fertile silt. Floods can enrich farmland but also destroy homes and lives. People build levees, dams and floodways to manage floods, though these can have side effects downstream."],["Watersheds","A watershed is all the land that drains into a river. Forests in watersheds slow rainwater, reduce erosion and help keep rivers flowing in dry seasons. Cutting forests, building on floodplains and dumping waste can increase floods and pollution downstream."]],
k:[["Smaller streams","Tributaries"],["Drainage area","Basin or watershed"],["Curving loops","Meanders"],["Longest Philippine river","Cagayan River"],["Metro Manila river","Pasig River"]],
d:[["c. 3500 BC","Cities rise along the Tigris and Euphrates rivers"],["c. 3000 BC","Egyptian civilization flourishes along the Nile"],["1999","A commission is created to rehabilitate the Pasig River"],["2009","Typhoon Ondoy causes severe flooding along the Marikina River"],["2018","The Pasig River wins an international river restoration prize"],["2024","A riverside walkway project begins along the Pasig River"]],
s2:[["Rivers of the Philippines","The Cagayan River in northern Luzon is the country’s longest river and has its largest basin, flowing north to the Babuyan Channel. The Rio Grande de Mindanao and the Agusan River are major rivers of Mindanao. The Pampanga River supports rice farming in Central Luzon. Many smaller rivers flow from mountains to the sea, and some flood quickly during typhoons."],["The Pasig River","The Pasig River connects Laguna de Bay to Manila Bay and was once a busy waterway lined with houses, churches and markets. By the late 20th century, it was heavily polluted by sewage, factories and garbage. Government agencies, businesses and communities have worked to clean it, relocating informal settlers, building parks and restoring tributaries. In 2018 the Pasig River won the first Asia RiverPrize for its rehabilitation efforts, though it remains polluted."],["Floods and the Marikina River","In September 2009, Tropical Storm Ondoy dumped a month’s worth of rain on Metro Manila in about a day. The Marikina River rose rapidly, flooding large parts of Marikina, Pasig and nearby areas and killing hundreds. Since then, the city has improved warning systems, river dredging and evacuation planning."],["Rivers and civilization","Many early civilizations grew along rivers: Mesopotamia along the Tigris and Euphrates, Egypt along the Nile, the Indus Valley civilization, and early Chinese civilization along the Yellow River. Rivers provided water for crops, fertile soil from floods, fish and transport routes for trade."],["Which river is longest?","The Nile and the Amazon are the two longest rivers in the world, each about 6,400 to 7,000 kilometers long. Which one is longer depends on where their sources and mouths are measured, and experts still debate it. The Amazon carries far more water than any other river."],["Dams and rivers","Dams provide electricity, water for cities and irrigation, and flood control. But they also block fish migration, trap sediment, flood land and can displace communities. In the Philippines, dams such as Angat supply most of Metro Manila’s water. Plans for new dams, such as the Kaliwa Dam, have been debated, with supporters citing water needs and opponents raising concerns about indigenous lands and the environment."]],
sp:["The river that rose overnight","On September 26, 2009, residents of Marikina City woke to heavy rain that did not stop. Tropical Storm Ondoy, known internationally as Ketsana, was dropping record rainfall on Metro Manila. By afternoon, the normally calm Marikina River had risen several meters, pouring into streets and homes. Families climbed onto roofs, waiting for rescue boats. Cars floated through flooded avenues. When the water finally receded, it left thick mud everywhere. The disaster led Marikina to build one of the country’s most advanced flood warning systems, with river gauges, sirens and color-coded alerts that tell residents when to prepare and when to evacuate."],
g:[["Tributary","A stream that flows into a larger river"],["Watershed","The land that drains into a river"],["Meander","A loop in a river"],["Estuary","Where a river meets the sea"]],
f:["The Cagayan River is the longest in the Philippines.","Meanders can form oxbow lakes.","The Amazon carries the most water of any river."],
fun:"The Amazon River carries more water than the next several largest rivers combined."});

ADDART({id:"deadsea",t:"The Dead Sea",c:"Geography",i:"🧂",w:"Dead Sea",r:["lakes","rift","desert","judaism","christ"],
p:[["Lowest place on land","The Dead Sea shore is the lowest land on Earth."],["Float easily","Its water is so salty that people float easily."],["Shrinking","The Dead Sea is getting smaller every year."]],
l:"The Dead Sea is a salt lake bordered by Jordan to the east and by Israel and the West Bank to the west, lying in the Jordan Rift Valley. Its shores are about 430 meters below sea level, the lowest land on Earth. The Dead Sea is one of the saltiest bodies of water in the world, nearly ten times saltier than the ocean, so salty that fish and most other life cannot survive in it, giving it its name, though some microbes do. The high salt content makes the water very dense, so people float effortlessly. The Dead Sea has been visited for thousands of years for its mineral-rich mud and water, believed to benefit health. Since the mid-20th century, it has been shrinking rapidly as water from the Jordan River is diverted.",
s:[["Why it is so salty","The Dead Sea has no outlet. Water flows in mainly from the Jordan River and springs, but it can only leave by evaporation in the hot, dry climate. As water evaporates, salts and minerals are left behind and become concentrated. Its water contains salts such as magnesium chloride, sodium chloride and potassium chloride."],["Floating","Because salt makes the water much denser than the human body, swimmers float high on the surface, able to lie back and read a newspaper. However, the water stings eyes and cuts badly, and swallowing it can be dangerous, so visitors are advised to be careful and rinse off afterward."],["The rift valley","The Dead Sea lies in a deep valley formed by the Dead Sea Transform, a boundary where the Arabian Plate and the African Plate slide past each other. This movement has caused the land to sink, creating the basin. The region has a history of earthquakes."],["Life in the Dead Sea","Although fish cannot live in the Dead Sea, scientists have found microbes that tolerate extreme salt. Divers exploring springs on the lake floor have found mats of bacteria living around freshwater vents. After rare floods dilute the surface, red blooms of salt-loving algae and microbes sometimes appear."]],
k:[["Bordering areas","Jordan, Israel, the West Bank"],["Shore elevation","About 430 m below sea level"],["Saltiness","Nearly 10 times saltier than the ocean"],["Main water source","Jordan River"],["Famous discovery nearby","Dead Sea Scrolls"]],
d:[["c. 1st century BC","Ancient writers describe the Dead Sea and its asphalt"],["1947","Bedouin shepherds discover the first Dead Sea Scrolls"],["1960s","Diversion of Jordan River water increases"],["1980s","Sinkholes begin appearing along the shores"],["2013","Jordan, Israel and the Palestinian Authority sign a water agreement"],["2021","A planned Red Sea to Dead Sea water project is shelved"]],
s2:[["The shrinking sea","Since the 1960s, the Dead Sea’s surface has dropped by more than a meter a year on average, and its area has shrunk dramatically. Most of the Jordan River’s water is now used for farming and drinking before it reaches the sea, and mineral extraction industries evaporate seawater in large ponds. As the water retreats, resorts once on the shore have been left far from the water."],["Sinkholes","As the Dead Sea shrinks, fresh groundwater dissolves underground salt layers, creating thousands of sinkholes along the shores. Some have swallowed roads, date palm groves and buildings, making parts of the coast dangerous. Scientists monitor them with satellites and ground surveys."],["The Dead Sea Scrolls","In 1947 Bedouin shepherds searching for a lost goat found clay jars in caves near Qumran, on the northwestern shore of the Dead Sea. Inside were ancient scrolls, later found to include the oldest known copies of books of the Hebrew Bible, written over 2,000 years ago. Further discoveries in nearby caves produced thousands of fragments, which are studied by scholars of Judaism and early Christianity."],["Saving the Dead Sea","Several plans have been proposed to bring water from the Red Sea or Mediterranean to refill the Dead Sea, combined with desalination to provide drinking water. A 2013 agreement between Jordan, Israel and the Palestinian Authority aimed to start such a project, but it was shelved in 2021. Environmental groups worry that mixing seawater could change the Dead Sea’s unique chemistry, while others argue that action is needed to stop its decline."],["Health and tourism","Visitors come to float in the water, cover themselves in black mineral mud and enjoy the sunny, dry climate. Some people with skin conditions such as psoriasis report improvement after visits, and the lower ultraviolet radiation at such low altitude allows longer sun exposure. Dead Sea salts and mud are sold worldwide in cosmetics."],["Ancient uses","In ancient times, lumps of natural asphalt, or bitumen, floated up from the Dead Sea and were collected for waterproofing boats and possibly for embalming in Egypt. Biblical stories, such as the destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah, are associated with the region, and King Herod built a fortress at Masada overlooking the sea."]],
sp:["The goat and the scrolls","In the winter of 1946 to 1947, a young Bedouin shepherd named Muhammad edh-Dhib was searching for a lost goat among the cliffs near Qumran, close to the Dead Sea. He threw a stone into a cave opening and heard something shatter. Climbing in, he and his companions found tall clay jars, some containing old leather scrolls wrapped in linen. They did not know what they had found and sold some scrolls to an antiquities dealer. Scholars soon realized they were extraordinarily ancient, among the oldest surviving biblical manuscripts. Over the following years, archaeologists and Bedouin searchers found many more scrolls in nearby caves, one of the most important archaeological discoveries of the 20th century."],
g:[["Salt lake","A lake with very salty water"],["Evaporation","Water turning into vapor"],["Sinkhole","A hole formed when the ground collapses"],["Rift valley","A valley formed where Earth’s crust pulls apart or slides"]],
f:["The Dead Sea shore is the lowest land on Earth.","Its water is nearly ten times saltier than the ocean.","The Dead Sea is shrinking."],
fun:"The Dead Sea is so salty that people can float on it while reading a newspaper."});

;
/* data/tech11.js */
// Technology: microwave ovens, typewriters, sewing machines and calculators.
ADDART({id:"microwave",t:"The Microwave Oven",c:"Invention",i:"📡",w:"Microwave oven",r:["refrigeration","radio","waterchem","foodsafety","elec"],
p:[["A melted chocolate bar","The microwave oven was invented after a scientist’s chocolate melted near radar equipment."],["Heating water","Microwaves heat food by making water molecules move."],["In most kitchens","Microwave ovens are now common in homes around the world."]],
l:"A microwave oven is a kitchen appliance that heats food using microwaves, a type of electromagnetic radiation with wavelengths longer than visible light but shorter than radio waves. Inside the oven, a device called a magnetron produces microwaves that bounce around the metal box and are absorbed by water, fat and sugar molecules in food, making them vibrate and heat up. Microwave ovens cook and reheat food quickly and use less energy than conventional ovens for small amounts. The technology grew out of radar research during World War II, when American engineer Percy Spencer noticed that microwaves from a radar device had melted a chocolate bar in his pocket. Early microwave ovens were huge and expensive, but by the 1970s and 1980s, smaller countertop models became common in homes, including in the Philippines.",
s:[["How it works","The magnetron converts electricity into microwaves at a frequency of about 2.45 gigahertz. A metal waveguide directs them into the cooking chamber, where they reflect off the metal walls. Water molecules in the food have a positive and negative end, so they rotate back and forth rapidly in the changing field, producing heat by friction with neighboring molecules."],["Why a turntable","Microwaves form patterns of hot and cold spots inside the oven. A rotating turntable moves the food through these spots so that it heats more evenly. Some ovens use a rotating metal stirrer instead. Stirring food and letting it stand after cooking also helps spread heat."],["Metal and sparks","Smooth metal containers reflect microwaves and prevent food from heating, and sharp metal edges, such as forks or foil with crinkles, can concentrate electric fields and cause sparks. That is why most microwave instructions advise against putting metal inside, though some specially designed metal parts are safe."],["Is it safe?","Microwave ovens are designed with metal walls and a mesh screen in the door that keep microwaves inside. The radiation is non-ionizing, meaning it does not make food radioactive or damage DNA like X-rays. Studies show microwaving does not destroy nutrients more than other cooking methods, and may preserve some vitamins better because of shorter cooking times."]],
k:[["Inventor","Percy Spencer"],["Year discovered","1945"],["Heat source","Magnetron"],["Frequency","About 2.45 GHz"],["First commercial model","Radarange, 1947"]],
d:[["1940","British scientists develop the cavity magnetron for radar"],["1945","Percy Spencer discovers microwave cooking"],["1947","The first commercial microwave oven, the Radarange, is sold"],["1967","A popular countertop microwave oven is introduced for homes"],["1970s","Microwave ovens spread rapidly in American homes"],["2000s","Microwave ovens become common in Philippine households"]],
s2:[["The melted chocolate","In 1945 Percy Spencer, an engineer at the American company Raytheon, was working near a magnetron, the device that produces microwaves for radar. He noticed that a chocolate bar in his pocket had melted. Curious, he placed popcorn kernels near the magnetron, and they popped. Then he tried an egg, which exploded. Raytheon patented microwave cooking, and in 1947 released the Radarange, a refrigerator-sized machine for restaurants and ships."],["From radar to kitchens","The cavity magnetron, developed in 1940 by British physicists John Randall and Harry Boot, made powerful compact radar possible and was crucial to the Allies in World War II. After the war, engineers looked for peaceful uses. Early microwave ovens cost as much as a car. Over the following decades, smaller, cheaper models made them affordable for households."],["Microwaves in Filipino homes","In the Philippines, microwave ovens became common in urban households in the 1990s and 2000s, used mainly for reheating leftovers such as rice and ulam, defrosting meat and quick cooking. Convenience stores also use them to heat ready-to-eat meals. Many families still prefer stoves and rice cookers for most cooking."],["Microwave myths","Some people believe microwave cooking makes food radioactive or less nutritious. Scientists say these beliefs are not supported by evidence. However, heating food in some plastic containers can release chemicals into food, so experts recommend using microwave-safe glass or ceramic dishes. Liquids can also superheat and suddenly boil over, so care is needed."],["Uneven heating","Microwaves heat unevenly, leaving some parts of food cold, which can be a food safety concern when reheating meat or leftovers. Health authorities advise stirring, rotating and checking that food is steaming hot throughout before eating."],["Beyond cooking","Microwave technology is used in many other areas, including mobile phones, Wi-Fi, satellite communications, radar and medical treatments. The cosmic microwave background, the faint afterglow of the Big Bang, is also made of microwaves, though at far lower energy."]],
sp:["Popcorn in the laboratory","Percy Spencer had grown up poor and never finished elementary school, but he became one of America’s most respected engineers, holding hundreds of patents. One day in 1945, after noticing his candy bar had melted near a working magnetron, he sent for a bag of popcorn kernels. He held them near the device, and they began to pop and scatter around the lab. The next morning, he tried an egg in a kettle near the magnetron; it exploded, splattering a curious colleague. Spencer realized that microwaves could cook food quickly. His accidental discovery changed kitchens around the world, and microwave popcorn became one of the most popular uses of the invention."],
g:[["Microwave","A type of electromagnetic wave"],["Magnetron","A device that produces microwaves"],["Non-ionizing radiation","Radiation that does not damage DNA"],["Turntable","A rotating plate in a microwave oven"]],
f:["The microwave oven was discovered by accident.","Microwaves heat food by moving water molecules.","Microwaving does not make food radioactive."],
fun:"The first commercial microwave oven was about as tall as a refrigerator."});

ADDART({id:"typewriter",t:"The Typewriter",c:"Invention",i:"⌨️",w:"Typewriter",r:["print","comp","paper","novel","journalism"],
p:[["Writing machine","Typewriters printed letters by striking inked ribbons onto paper."],["QWERTY","The keyboard layout we use today came from typewriters."],["Changing work","Typewriters opened new jobs for women in offices."]],
l:"A typewriter is a mechanical or electric machine for writing characters on paper. Pressing a key swings a metal type bar that strikes an inked ribbon against the paper, printing a letter. Typewriters became widely used in the late 1800s, after inventors Christopher Latham Sholes, Carlos Glidden and Samuel Soule patented a practical design in 1868 that was manufactured by Remington from 1873. Typewriters transformed offices, journalism and literature, making writing faster and more legible than handwriting, and created new jobs, especially for women as typists and secretaries. The QWERTY keyboard layout designed for typewriters is still used on computers and phones today. Typewriters were largely replaced by computers in the 1980s and 1990s, but some people still use and collect them, and sidewalk typists still serve customers in some Philippine cities.",
s:[["How it works","On a manual typewriter, each key is connected by levers to a type bar with raised letters. When a key is pressed, the bar swings up and strikes the ribbon, leaving an inked letter on the paper. The carriage holding the paper then moves one space to the left. At the end of a line, a bell rings, and the typist pushes a lever to return the carriage and advance the paper."],["The QWERTY layout","The QWERTY layout, named after the first six letters on the top row, was developed by Sholes in the 1870s. A popular explanation says it was designed to separate common letter pairs to reduce jamming of type bars, though historians debate this. Other layouts, such as Dvorak, have been proposed as faster, but QWERTY remained the standard."],["Electric typewriters","Electric typewriters, powered by motors, made typing easier and more even. In 1961 IBM introduced the Selectric, which used a spinning golf-ball-shaped element instead of type bars, allowing typists to change fonts and avoid jams. Later electronic typewriters had small memories and displays."],["Carbon copies","Before photocopiers, typists made copies by placing carbon paper between sheets, so that each keystroke printed on several pages at once. The abbreviation cc, still used in email, comes from carbon copy."]],
k:[["Practical design patented","1868"],["First maker","Remington, from 1873"],["Keyboard layout","QWERTY"],["Famous electric model","IBM Selectric, 1961"],["Replaced by","Computers"]],
d:[["1714","Henry Mill receives a British patent for a writing machine"],["1868","Sholes, Glidden and Soule patent a practical typewriter"],["1874","The Sholes and Glidden typewriter goes on sale"],["1961","IBM introduces the Selectric typewriter"],["1980s","Word processors and personal computers replace typewriters"],["2020s","Typewriters are collected and used by hobbyists and some writers"]],
s2:[["Women in the office","Typewriters created a new profession: the typist. By the early 1900s, many offices employed women as typists and stenographers, giving them new opportunities for paid work outside the home. In many countries, typing schools trained young women for office jobs, though they were often paid less than men."],["Writers and typewriters","Mark Twain is often said to have submitted one of the first typewritten book manuscripts, Life on the Mississippi, in the 1880s. Many 20th-century writers, journalists and poets composed on typewriters, and some still prefer them because they help them focus without the distractions of a computer."],["Typists of the Philippines","For decades, typewriters were essential in Philippine government offices, schools and businesses, used for letters, forms and legal documents. Outside city halls, courts and government offices in places like Manila, sidewalk typists set up small tables with typewriters to fill out forms and affidavits for customers, a service that continues in some places today."],["The end of an era","With the rise of personal computers and word processing software in the 1980s and 1990s, typewriter sales collapsed. Many manufacturers closed or switched to making computers and printers. Typewriters remained in use in some offices and countries for years, and some government agencies keep them for filling out pre-printed forms."],["Typing skills","Typewriters made touch typing, typing without looking at the keys, an important skill. Schools taught typing classes, and typists were tested on words per minute. Today, keyboarding remains an essential skill for using computers and phones."],["Typewriters and security","Typewriters leave no digital traces, so in 2013 reports said some government agencies, such as in Russia, had bought typewriters to protect secret documents from computer hacking. Each typewriter also has small quirks in its letters, which forensic experts can use to identify which machine typed a document."]],
sp:["The ring of the bell","In a government office in Manila in the 1970s, the room is filled with a constant clatter: dozens of typists pounding the keys of heavy manual typewriters, each keystroke a sharp clack. Every few seconds, a small bell rings as a typist reaches the end of a line, followed by the zip of the carriage being pushed back. Carbon paper is sandwiched between sheets to make copies. Mistakes must be corrected with erasers or white correction fluid, or the whole page retyped. By the 1990s, the clatter fades as computers arrive, but on the sidewalks outside some city halls, a few typists still type forms for customers, keeping the sound alive."],
g:[["Typewriter","A machine for printing letters on paper"],["QWERTY","The standard keyboard layout"],["Carbon copy","A copy made using carbon paper"],["Touch typing","Typing without looking at the keys"]],
f:["The QWERTY layout came from typewriters.","Typewriters created many office jobs for women.","Email’s cc stands for carbon copy."],
fun:"The cc in email stands for carbon copy, from the days of typewriters."});

ADDART({id:"sewingmachine",t:"The Sewing Machine",c:"Invention",i:"🧵",w:"Sewing machine",r:["weaving","fastfashion","clothing","steam","indrev"],
p:[["Faster sewing","Sewing machines stitch fabric much faster than hand sewing."],["Changing clothing","They made mass production of clothes possible."],["At home and in factories","Sewing machines are used in homes, tailor shops and garment factories."]],
l:"A sewing machine is a machine that stitches fabric and other materials together with thread. Most use a needle with an eye near its point and a second thread from a bobbin below to form a lockstitch, a strong stitch that interlocks the two threads. Sewing machines were developed in the first half of the 19th century by inventors in France, Britain and the United States, including Barthélemy Thimonnier, Elias Howe and Isaac Singer, whose company became famous worldwide. Sewing machines greatly increased the speed of making clothes, shoes and other goods, transforming the garment industry and home sewing. They helped create the ready-made clothing industry and are still central to garment factories. In the Philippines, dressmakers, tailors and garment workers rely on sewing machines.",
s:[["The lockstitch","Hand sewing uses a single thread passed through fabric. Most sewing machines use two threads. The needle pushes the upper thread through the fabric, forming a loop below, where a rotating hook catches it and wraps it around the bobbin thread. The threads lock together in the middle of the fabric, creating a secure stitch."],["Treadle and electric machines","Early home sewing machines were powered by hand cranks or by foot treadles, pedals that turned a wheel. Electric motors became common in the 20th century. Modern computerized machines can sew many decorative stitches and embroidery patterns automatically."],["Industrial machines","Factories use specialized machines that sew faster and perform particular tasks, such as overlocking edges to prevent fraying, sewing buttonholes or attaching buttons. Workers often operate one type of machine all day in assembly-line production."],["Changing everyday life","Before sewing machines, making a man’s shirt by hand could take many hours. Machines cut this time dramatically, making clothing cheaper and more available. They also allowed many women to earn income by sewing at home or in workshops."]],
k:[["Main stitch","Lockstitch"],["Early inventors","Thimonnier, Howe, Singer"],["Howe’s patent","1846"],["Power sources","Hand, foot treadle, electric"],["Philippine word for dressmaker","Mananahi"]],
d:[["1830","Barthélemy Thimonnier patents a sewing machine in France"],["1846","Elias Howe patents a lockstitch sewing machine"],["1851","Isaac Singer patents an improved machine"],["1856","Sewing machine makers form one of the first patent pools"],["1889","Singer introduces an electric sewing machine"],["1970s","Garment factories grow in the Philippines and Asia"]],
s2:[["Thimonnier and the angry tailors","In 1830 the French tailor Barthélemy Thimonnier patented a sewing machine and later opened a factory to sew uniforms for the French army. Tailors who feared losing their jobs attacked the factory and destroyed the machines. Thimonnier tried again but died poor, while others profited from later designs."],["The sewing machine war","Elias Howe patented his lockstitch machine in 1846, but Isaac Singer and other makers used similar ideas. Howe sued and won royalties. To end the costly patent battles, the major manufacturers agreed in 1856 to share their patents in a combination, considered one of the first patent pools in history."],["Singer and installment buying","Isaac Singer’s company made sewing machines affordable to families by allowing them to pay in installments over time, an early example of consumer credit. Singer machines spread worldwide, including to the Philippines, where many households and dressmakers used treadle Singer machines for generations."],["Sewing in the Philippines","Filipino dressmakers, called mananahi or modista, and tailors, called sastre, sew clothes, uniforms and formal wear such as the barong Tagalog and terno. Neighborhood tailor shops repair and alter clothes. The Philippines also has a garment export industry, though it has faced competition from other Asian countries."],["Garment workers","Garment factories around the world employ millions of workers, many of them women, who often work long hours for low wages. Disasters such as the 2013 collapse of the Rana Plaza building in Bangladesh, which killed over 1,100 garment workers, drew attention to working conditions and led to calls for safer factories and fairer pay."],["Sewing as a skill","Sewing remains a useful skill for repairing clothes, creating costumes and starting small businesses. Sewing classes are taught in some Philippine schools as part of technology and livelihood education. Mending clothes instead of throwing them away is also promoted as a way to reduce textile waste."]],
sp:["The dream of the needle","A popular story about Elias Howe says that while struggling to make his sewing machine work, he had a nightmare in which he was captured by warriors holding spears. As they raised their spears, he noticed that each had a hole near the tip. When he woke, he realized that his machine needed a needle with an eye near the point, not at the top like a hand-sewing needle. Historians are not sure the story is true; it was told many years later. But Howe’s machine did use such a needle, and the eye-pointed needle became a key part of almost every sewing machine that followed."],
g:[["Lockstitch","A stitch made by interlocking two threads"],["Bobbin","A small spool holding the lower thread"],["Treadle","A foot pedal that powers a machine"],["Mananahi","The Filipino word for a person who sews"]],
f:["Most sewing machines use two threads.","Elias Howe patented the lockstitch in 1846.","Singer let families pay in installments."],
fun:"Tailors in France once destroyed an early sewing machine factory because they feared losing their jobs."});

ADDART({id:"calculators",t:"Calculators",c:"Invention",i:"🧮",w:"Calculator",r:["comp","binary","chip","zero","alg"],
p:[["Counting machines","Calculators do arithmetic quickly and accurately."],["From abacus to pocket","Calculating tools range from counting boards to electronic calculators."],["A chip revolution","Microchips made pocket calculators possible in the 1970s."]],
l:"A calculator is a device that performs arithmetic and other mathematical calculations. People have used calculating tools for thousands of years, from counting stones and the abacus to the slide rule and mechanical adding machines. In the 17th century, inventors such as Blaise Pascal and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz built mechanical calculators with gears. Electronic calculators appeared in the 1960s, and in the early 1970s new microchips made small, affordable pocket calculators possible. Scientific and graphing calculators can handle complex functions, and calculators are now built into phones and computers. Calculators are used in schools, offices, shops and science. Their use in classrooms has been debated, with some educators worried about students losing basic skills.",
s:[["The abacus","The abacus, a frame of beads on rods, has been used for centuries in China, Japan, Russia and other countries. Skilled users can calculate very quickly, and some can imagine an abacus in their minds to do mental arithmetic. Abacus training remains popular in parts of Asia, including the Philippines."],["Mechanical calculators","In 1642 the French teenager Blaise Pascal built the Pascaline, a mechanical calculator with gears, to help his father, a tax official. In the 1670s Leibniz designed a machine that could multiply and divide. In the 19th century, mass-produced machines such as the arithmometer were used in offices and banks."],["The slide rule","The slide rule, invented in the 1620s using logarithms, allowed engineers and scientists to multiply, divide and calculate roots by sliding scales against each other. For over three centuries, it was the engineer’s essential tool, used in designing bridges, airplanes and even the Apollo spacecraft."],["Electronic calculators","Early electronic calculators in the 1960s were desktop machines. In 1971 the Intel 4004 microprocessor, designed for a Japanese calculator company, packed a computer’s central processor onto one chip. Pocket calculators soon followed, falling rapidly in price, and in 1972 the first handheld scientific calculator appeared."]],
k:[["Ancient tool","Abacus"],["Pascaline","1642"],["Slide rule invented","1620s"],["First microprocessor","Intel 4004, 1971"],["First handheld scientific calculator","1972"]],
d:[["c. 2nd century BC","Counting rods and early abacus-like tools are used in China"],["1620s","The slide rule is invented"],["1642","Blaise Pascal builds the Pascaline"],["1820","The arithmometer is invented in France"],["1971","The first microprocessor is designed for a calculator"],["1972","The first handheld scientific calculator goes on sale"]],
s2:[["Calculators and microchips","Calculators played a key role in the history of computers. The Japanese company Busicom asked Intel to design chips for a calculator, and Intel engineers created the 4004, the first commercial microprocessor, in 1971. This general-purpose chip led to personal computers. Calculator makers also pioneered small liquid crystal displays and solar cells."],["Calculators in school","When affordable calculators appeared, teachers and parents debated whether students should use them. Supporters argued that calculators let students focus on problem-solving and real-world applications. Critics worried that students would not learn basic arithmetic. Many schools now teach mental math and written methods first and allow calculators for more advanced work. In the Philippines, scientific calculators are commonly used in high school and college math and science."],["The end of the slide rule","Slide rules were used by engineers and students for centuries, but within a few years of the arrival of cheap electronic calculators in the early 1970s, they almost completely disappeared. Factories that made slide rules closed, and engineers who had trained for years on them switched to calculators."],["Human computers","Before electronic calculators, people called computers performed calculations by hand for astronomy, navigation, engineering and war. Many were women. At NASA in the 1950s and 1960s, mathematicians such as Katherine Johnson calculated spacecraft trajectories, work that was celebrated in the 2016 film Hidden Figures."],["Fun with calculators","Students have long played games with calculators, such as typing numbers that spell words when the calculator is turned upside down. Some calculators can be programmed, and graphing calculators can even run simple games."],["Calculators everywhere","Today, calculator functions are built into phones, computers and watches. Specialized calculators are used for finance, construction and medicine. Yet many people still keep a basic calculator in drawers and cash registers, and accountants often prefer them for quick sums."]],
sp:["A gift for a tax collector","In 1640s France, Étienne Pascal worked as a tax official in Rouen, spending long nights adding up columns of numbers. His teenage son, Blaise, wanted to help. Around 1642, at about 19 years old, Blaise designed a box with numbered wheels connected by gears. When one wheel turned past nine, it pushed the next wheel forward by one, carrying the digit automatically. He built dozens of versions over several years. His Pascaline could add and subtract, though it was expensive and fragile, and few were sold. Still, it was one of the first mechanical calculators, and Pascal went on to become a famous mathematician and philosopher. A computer programming language, Pascal, was later named after him."],
g:[["Calculator","A device that performs calculations"],["Abacus","A frame of beads used for counting"],["Slide rule","A tool using sliding scales to calculate"],["Microprocessor","A computer processor on a single chip"]],
f:["Pascal built a mechanical calculator in 1642.","The first microprocessor was made for a calculator.","Slide rules were used for centuries before calculators."],
fun:"The first microprocessor was designed for a Japanese calculator."});

;
/* data/tech12.js */
// Technology: LEDs, touchscreens, roads and highways, and airports.
ADDART({id:"leds",t:"LEDs",c:"Invention",i:"💡",w:"Light-emitting diode",r:["lightbulb","chip","elec","energytransition","smartphone"],
p:[["Light from chips","LEDs make light from tiny semiconductor chips."],["Saving energy","They use far less electricity than old light bulbs."],["A Nobel blue","The invention of blue LEDs won the 2014 Nobel Prize."]],
l:"A light-emitting diode, or LED, is a small semiconductor device that produces light when electric current flows through it. LEDs are extremely efficient, using a fraction of the electricity of traditional incandescent bulbs to produce the same amount of light, and they last much longer. The first practical visible LEDs, which glowed red, were developed in the early 1960s and were used for decades in indicator lights and calculator displays. In the early 1990s, Japanese scientists Isamu Akasaki, Hiroshi Amano and Shuji Nakamura created bright blue LEDs, which made white LED lighting possible and earned them the 2014 Nobel Prize in Physics. Today LEDs light homes, streets, screens, cars and traffic signals worldwide, including in the Philippines, helping save energy and reduce emissions.",
s:[["How LEDs work","An LED is made of two layers of semiconductor material, one with extra electrons and one with spaces, called holes, where electrons are missing. When current flows, electrons cross the junction and fall into holes, releasing energy as particles of light called photons. The color depends on the materials used."],["Making white light","LEDs naturally produce one color. White LEDs usually combine a blue LED with a coating of yellow phosphor, which glows when hit by blue light; together the colors look white. Other white LEDs mix red, green and blue LEDs."],["Efficiency and lifespan","An incandescent bulb turns most of its electricity into heat rather than light. LEDs convert a much larger share into light and can last tens of thousands of hours, many times longer than incandescent bulbs. Although LED bulbs once cost more, prices have fallen sharply."],["Screens","LEDs light up most modern screens. Many televisions and laptops use LEDs as backlights behind liquid crystal displays. Other screens, called OLED, use organic materials that glow themselves, allowing very thin, flexible displays. Giant outdoor video screens are made of thousands of LEDs."]],
k:[["Full name","Light-emitting diode"],["First visible LED","1962, red"],["Blue LED breakthrough","Early 1990s"],["Nobel Prize","Physics, 2014"],["Main advantage","Energy efficiency"]],
d:[["1907","Henry Round observes light from a crystal carrying current"],["1962","Nick Holonyak Jr. creates the first practical visible-light LED"],["1970s","Red LEDs are used in calculators and watches"],["1993","Shuji Nakamura demonstrates a bright blue LED"],["2014","Akasaki, Amano and Nakamura win the Nobel Prize in Physics"],["2020s","LEDs become the dominant form of lighting worldwide"]],
s2:[["The race for blue","Red and green LEDs were developed in the 1960s and 1970s, but blue proved extremely difficult. Many companies gave up. In Japan, Isamu Akasaki and Hiroshi Amano at Nagoya University, and Shuji Nakamura, working at a small chemical company, Nichia, persisted with a material called gallium nitride. In the early 1990s they succeeded in making bright, efficient blue LEDs, which made white LED lighting and blue lasers possible."],["The Nakamura dispute","After his invention brought huge profits to his employer, Shuji Nakamura sued the company for a larger share, arguing that inventors deserve fair rewards. A Japanese court initially awarded him a large sum, and the case was later settled. It sparked debate in Japan about the rights of employee inventors."],["LEDs in the Philippines","The Philippine government has encouraged switching to LED lighting to save energy and money, including in public buildings, streetlights and homes. LED street lights have been installed in many cities and towns. Solar-powered LED lamps provide light to off-grid homes and remote islands without electricity."],["Light pollution","Because LEDs are cheap and bright, outdoor lighting has increased in many places. Bright, bluish-white LED streetlights can contribute to light pollution, which affects sleep, wildlife such as sea turtles and insects, and stargazing. Some cities now use warmer-colored LEDs and shielded lamps that direct light downward."],["Growing plants","LEDs can be tuned to emit the colors of light plants use most for photosynthesis. Indoor and vertical farms use LED lights to grow vegetables year-round, and scientists use them in research on plant growth."],["Beyond lighting","LEDs are used in traffic lights, car headlights, remote controls, which use invisible infrared LEDs, fiber-optic communication, medical devices and even to disinfect water and surfaces using ultraviolet light."]],
sp:["The persistent engineer","In the 1980s Shuji Nakamura was a researcher at Nichia, a small chemical company on the Japanese island of Shikoku. He was frustrated that his earlier projects had not sold well. Determined to achieve something big, he chose the hardest goal in LED research: a bright blue LED. Most experts had abandoned gallium nitride as too difficult. Nakamura built and modified his own equipment, sometimes working with the machines late into the night, and studied in the United States to learn new techniques. In 1993 Nichia announced a bright blue LED based on his work, astonishing the industry. Two decades later, he shared the Nobel Prize with Akasaki and Amano, whose earlier breakthroughs had opened the path."],
g:[["LED","Light-emitting diode"],["Semiconductor","A material that conducts electricity partly"],["Photon","A particle of light"],["Phosphor","A substance that glows when struck by light"]],
f:["LEDs use far less energy than incandescent bulbs.","Blue LEDs made white LED lighting possible.","The blue LED inventors won the 2014 Nobel Prize."],
fun:"Remote controls use invisible infrared LEDs, which you can sometimes see glow through a phone camera."});

ADDART({id:"touchscreens",t:"Touchscreens",c:"Invention",i:"📱",w:"Touchscreen",r:["smartphone","comp","videogames","chip","digitaldivide"],
p:[["Touch to control","Touchscreens let people control devices by touching the display."],["Fingers and electricity","Most phone screens sense the electricity in our fingers."],["Everywhere","Touchscreens are on phones, tablets, ATMs and kiosks."]],
l:"A touchscreen is a display that detects the touch of a finger or stylus, letting users interact directly with what they see rather than using a keyboard or mouse. Touchscreens are found in smartphones, tablets, ATMs, ticket machines, car dashboards, airport check-in kiosks, game consoles and many other devices. The first touchscreens were developed in the 1960s and 1970s in Britain, Switzerland and the United States. Early ones could sense only one touch at a time, but modern capacitive touchscreens detect multiple fingers, allowing gestures such as pinching to zoom. The launch of popular smartphones in the late 2000s made multitouch screens part of everyday life for billions of people, including many Filipinos who use phones as their main way to access the internet.",
s:[["Capacitive touchscreens","Most smartphones use capacitive touchscreens, covered with a grid of transparent electrodes. Because the human body conducts electricity, a finger touching the glass changes the electric field at that point. The device detects where the change happens and responds. This is why ordinary gloves usually do not work on phone screens."],["Resistive touchscreens","Resistive touchscreens have two flexible layers separated by a tiny gap. Pressing the screen pushes the layers together, completing a circuit at that spot. They work with any object, including gloved fingers or pens, and were common in older devices, ATMs and point-of-sale machines."],["Other kinds","Some large screens use infrared beams across the surface, detecting where a finger interrupts them. Others use cameras or sound waves. Stylus pens for drawing tablets can detect pressure and tilt, letting artists draw naturally."],["Gestures","Multitouch screens recognize gestures such as tapping, swiping, pinching and rotating. These gestures made devices easier to use for people of all ages, including young children, who often learn to swipe before they can read."]],
k:[["First capacitive touchscreen","1965, Britain"],["Most common type in phones","Capacitive"],["Works with gloves","Resistive"],["Popular gesture","Pinch to zoom"],["Key moment","Smartphones of the late 2000s"]],
d:[["1965","E. A. Johnson describes a capacitive touchscreen in Britain"],["1971","Samuel Hurst invents an early resistive touch sensor"],["1973","CERN engineers build a capacitive touchscreen for control rooms"],["1983","A personal computer with a touchscreen goes on sale"],["1994","An early smartphone with a touchscreen is released"],["2007","Multitouch smartphones become widely popular"]],
s2:[["Touchscreens at CERN","In 1973 engineers Frank Beck and Bent Stumpe at CERN, the European particle physics laboratory near Geneva, built capacitive touchscreens to control the laboratory’s accelerator. Operators could press buttons drawn on screens, which could change depending on the task, instead of using thousands of physical switches."],["Phones in the Philippines","The Philippines has very high smartphone use, with many people relying on phones rather than computers to go online, chat, use social media, pay bills and shop. Touchscreen phones became affordable in the 2010s, helping many Filipinos access the internet for the first time. However, those without phones or data remain left behind, part of the digital divide."],["Accessibility","Touchscreens can be difficult for people with visual impairments, since there are no physical buttons to feel. Screen readers, which speak what is on the screen, and gesture controls help blind and low-vision users. Large icons and voice commands also help older users and people with motor difficulties."],["Hygiene","Touchscreens in public places, such as ATMs and kiosks, are touched by many people and can carry germs. During the COVID-19 pandemic, many businesses disinfected screens frequently, and some introduced contactless options such as QR codes and voice control."],["Children and screens","Because touchscreens are so easy to use, young children use them from an early age. Pediatric experts recommend limiting screen time for young children, encouraging active play, reading and conversation, and choosing high-quality content. Debates continue about how screen use affects development."],["Future screens","Engineers are developing foldable and rollable touchscreens, screens that respond to touch with vibrations or textures, called haptic feedback, and touch-sensitive surfaces on tables, walls and clothing. Some devices can detect gestures in the air without touching."]],
sp:["Buttons that changed","In the early 1970s, engineers at CERN faced a problem. The control room for their new particle accelerator would need thousands of buttons and switches, too many to fit on panels. Bent Stumpe, a Danish engineer, remembered an idea he had worked on years earlier and proposed a solution: a screen with transparent touch-sensitive points that could display different buttons as needed. Working with his colleague Frank Beck, he built a capacitive touchscreen using thin copper lines on glass. In 1973 the system was installed, letting operators control complex machinery by touching a screen. Decades later, similar technology would be in the pockets of billions of people."],
g:[["Touchscreen","A display that responds to touch"],["Capacitive","Sensing changes in electric charge"],["Multitouch","Detecting several touches at once"],["Haptic feedback","Vibrations that simulate touch"]],
f:["Most phone screens sense the electricity in fingers.","CERN built an early touchscreen in 1973.","Resistive screens work with gloves."],
fun:"Ordinary gloves usually do not work on phone screens because they block the electricity in your fingers."});

ADDART({id:"roads",t:"Roads and Highways",c:"Engineering",i:"🛣️",w:"Road",r:["cars","bridges","tunnels","jeepney","rome"],
p:[["Paths for travel","Roads connect towns, cities and countries."],["Roman roads","The Romans built a vast network of paved roads."],["Philippine highways","The Maharlika Highway runs through much of the Philippines."]],
l:"Roads are routes built for travel by people, animals and vehicles. They range from dirt paths and village lanes to multi-lane expressways crossing continents. Early roads followed animal tracks and trade routes, and ancient empires such as Rome, Persia, China and the Inca built extensive road networks to move armies, officials and goods. Modern roads are built in layers of crushed stone topped with asphalt or concrete, designed to carry heavy traffic and drain rainwater. Roads are vital for trade, jobs, schools and health care, but they also bring traffic congestion, pollution and accidents. In the Philippines, the Pan-Philippine Highway, also known as the Maharlika Highway, links Luzon, the Visayas and Mindanao using roads, bridges and ferries.",
s:[["Ancient roads","The Romans built tens of thousands of kilometers of paved roads across their empire, beginning with the Appian Way in 312 BC. They were straight, with layers of stones and drainage ditches, and some sections are still used today. The Persian Royal Road and the Inca road system, which stretched along the Andes, were also remarkable achievements."],["How modern roads are built","A modern road has several layers: compacted soil, a base of crushed rock and a surface of asphalt or concrete. In the 1800s Scottish engineer John McAdam developed roads made of small broken stones compacted in layers, called macadam. Adding tar to bind the stones led to tarmac and modern asphalt roads."],["Highways and expressways","Expressways have controlled entrances and exits, multiple lanes and no traffic lights, allowing faster travel. The German autobahns of the 1930s and the US Interstate Highway System, begun in 1956, were major networks. In the Philippines, expressways such as the North Luzon Expressway, South Luzon Expressway and others connect Metro Manila with nearby provinces."],["Road safety","Road crashes kill about 1.2 million people worldwide each year, according to the World Health Organization, and are a leading cause of death among young people. Speed limits, seat belts, helmets, safer road designs, sidewalks and enforcement of drunk-driving laws help prevent deaths. Pedestrians, cyclists and motorcyclists are especially vulnerable."]],
k:[["First major Roman road","Appian Way, 312 BC"],["Road surface pioneer","John McAdam"],["Philippine backbone","Pan-Philippine Highway"],["US Interstate begun","1956"],["Yearly road deaths worldwide","About 1.2 million"]],
d:[["312 BC","Construction of the Appian Way begins in Rome"],["1820s","John McAdam’s road-building methods spread"],["1905","Kennon Road to Baguio opens"],["1940","Construction of the highway that becomes EDSA begins"],["1956","The US Interstate Highway System is authorized"],["1960s","Work on the Pan-Philippine Highway advances"]],
s2:[["The Maharlika Highway","The Pan-Philippine Highway, also called the Maharlika Highway, stretches about 3,500 kilometers from Laoag in northern Luzon to Zamboanga City in Mindanao, connecting islands with roll-on, roll-off ferries. It is part of the Asian Highway Network. Construction took decades, and parts have been widened and repaired over time."],["Kennon Road","Kennon Road, a winding mountain road from Pangasinan to Baguio, was built by the American colonial government and opened in 1905, using the labor of thousands of Filipino, Japanese and other workers, many of whom died. It made Baguio accessible as a summer capital. Today it is scenic but often closed due to landslides during the rainy season."],["EDSA","Epifanio de los Santos Avenue, known as EDSA, is Metro Manila’s main circumferential road, about 24 kilometers long. It was built beginning in 1940 and renamed in 1959. EDSA was the site of the 1986 People Power Revolution. It is known for heavy traffic, and authorities have added a bus carousel lane and the MRT-3 rail line along it."],["Roads or public transport?","Many cities debate whether to build more roads or invest in public transport. Supporters of new roads say they ease congestion and support economic growth. Critics point out that new roads often fill up with more cars, a phenomenon called induced demand, and argue that trains, buses, walking and cycling move more people using less space. Many planners favor a mix."],["Farm-to-market roads","In rural areas of the Philippines, farm-to-market roads connect farming villages with towns, helping farmers sell crops, children reach schools and patients reach clinics. Building and maintaining these roads is a major government program, though some projects have been criticized for poor quality or corruption."],["The Inca road","The Inca built a road network of around 40,000 kilometers through the Andes mountains, including steps, tunnels and suspension bridges made of grass rope. Runners called chasquis carried messages along these roads in relays. The network, called Qhapaq Ñan, is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]],
sp:["The road to the summer capital","At the start of the 20th century, American officials wanted to build a cool mountain retreat at Baguio, high in the Cordillera. Getting there was difficult; travelers rode horses for days on steep trails. In 1901 work began on a road through the narrow Bued River canyon. Engineers faced landslides, floods and steep cliffs, and many workers, including Filipinos, Japanese, Chinese and others, died from accidents and disease. Colonel Lyman Kennon took charge and completed the road in 1905. It was later named Kennon Road. Its zigzagging route, with views of waterfalls and the Lion’s Head sculpture, became famous, though landslides still close it often."],
g:[["Expressway","A high-speed road with controlled access"],["Asphalt","A black, sticky material used to pave roads"],["Macadam","A road surface of compacted broken stones"],["Induced demand","More traffic created by new road capacity"]],
f:["The Appian Way was begun in 312 BC.","The Maharlika Highway links Luzon to Mindanao.","Road crashes kill about 1.2 million people a year."],
fun:"Some Roman roads built more than 2,000 years ago are still used today."});

ADDART({id:"airports",t:"Airports",c:"Engineering",i:"🛫",w:"Airport",r:["flight","tourism","ninoy","megacities","containers"],
p:[["Gateways to the sky","Airports are places where planes take off and land."],["Small cities","Big airports have shops, hotels and thousands of workers."],["Philippine gateways","NAIA in Manila is the country’s main international airport."]],
l:"An airport is a place where aircraft take off and land, with runways, taxiways, terminals for passengers, cargo areas, control towers and facilities for refueling and maintenance. The first airfields in the early 1900s were simple grass fields. As air travel grew, airports developed paved runways, terminals, air traffic control and security systems. Today the busiest airports handle tens of millions of passengers each year and function like small cities, with shops, restaurants, hotels and thousands of workers. Airports are important for tourism, trade and connecting families, but they also raise issues of noise, pollution and land use. In the Philippines, Ninoy Aquino International Airport in Manila is the main gateway, along with Mactan–Cebu, Clark, Davao and other airports, while a large new airport is being built in Bulacan.",
s:[["Runways and taxiways","Runways are long, paved strips where planes take off and land, numbered according to their compass direction. Large jets need runways several kilometers long. Taxiways connect runways to terminals and hangars, and lights and signs guide pilots, especially at night and in bad weather."],["Air traffic control","Air traffic controllers in control towers and radar centers guide aircraft safely, giving instructions for takeoff, landing and routes to keep planes separated. The first air traffic control tower opened at Croydon Airport near London in 1920. Today controllers use radar, satellites and computers."],["Terminals","Terminals are the buildings where passengers check in, pass through security and immigration, wait at gates and collect baggage. Baggage handling systems with conveyor belts sort thousands of bags an hour. Many modern terminals are designed by famous architects and feature open spaces, art and gardens."],["Security","After hijackings in the 1960s and 1970s and the September 11, 2001 attacks in the United States, airports introduced strict security checks, including screening of passengers and luggage, metal detectors and body scanners. Security procedures continue to evolve as new threats and technologies emerge."]],
k:[["First control tower","Croydon, England, 1920"],["Main Philippine airport","Ninoy Aquino International Airport"],["NAIA renamed","1987"],["New Philippine airport","New Manila International Airport, Bulacan"],["Airport codes","Three letters, such as MNL"]],
d:[["1909","An early airfield opens at College Park, Maryland"],["1920","Croydon Airport opens the first air traffic control tower"],["1948","Manila International Airport begins operating at its present site"],["1981","A new international terminal opens in Manila"],["1987","Manila’s airport is renamed after Ninoy Aquino"],["2024","A private consortium takes over the operation of NAIA"]],
s2:[["The story of NAIA","Manila’s main airport began as Nielson Field in Makati in the 1930s before moving to its present site in Parañaque and Pasay after World War II. On August 21, 1983, opposition leader Benigno Ninoy Aquino Jr. was assassinated at the airport upon returning from exile, an event that helped lead to the 1986 People Power Revolution. In 1987 the airport was renamed Ninoy Aquino International Airport in his honor."],["Privatizing NAIA","NAIA has long faced congestion, delays and criticism of its facilities. In 2024 the government handed its operation and upgrading to a private consortium under a long-term agreement. Supporters said private investment would improve services, while critics and some lawmakers raised concerns about higher fees for passengers and airlines. The arrangement continues to be monitored."],["New airports","To relieve NAIA, the Philippines has expanded other airports, including a new terminal at Mactan–Cebu International Airport in 2018 and at Clark International Airport in 2022. A large new airport, the New Manila International Airport, is under construction in Bulacan. Supporters say it will boost the economy, while environmental groups have raised concerns about its effects on coastal wetlands and fishing communities."],["Airport codes","Every major airport has a three-letter code assigned by the International Air Transport Association, printed on baggage tags and tickets. NAIA is MNL, Mactan–Cebu is CEB and Davao is DVO. Some codes come from older names; Chicago’s O’Hare is ORD from its former name, Orchard Field."],["Noise and neighbors","Airports bring jobs and connections, but nearby residents suffer from aircraft noise and air pollution. Many airports limit night flights, use noise barriers and insulate nearby homes. In the Philippines, communities around NAIA live very close to its runways."],["Airports and climate","Aviation produces a growing share of greenhouse gas emissions. Airports are working to reduce their own emissions with solar panels, electric ground vehicles and efficient buildings, while airlines test sustainable fuels. Critics argue that expanding airports encourages more flying and higher emissions."]],
sp:["Homecoming on the tarmac","On August 21, 1983, a China Airlines plane landed at Manila International Airport carrying Benigno Ninoy Aquino Jr., a leading critic of President Ferdinand Marcos, returning after three years in exile in the United States. He knew the risks; before landing, he told journalists on board to be ready. Soldiers escorted him off the plane, and moments later he was shot dead on the stairs leading to the tarmac. The assassination shocked the nation and the world, fueling protests that grew over the next years and culminated in the People Power Revolution of 1986. Four years after his death, the airport where he was killed was renamed in his honor."],
g:[["Runway","A paved strip for takeoffs and landings"],["Terminal","The building where passengers board and leave planes"],["Air traffic control","The system that guides aircraft safely"],["Airport code","A three-letter abbreviation for an airport"]],
f:["The first air traffic control tower opened in 1920.","NAIA was renamed in 1987.","Manila’s airport code is MNL."],
fun:"Chicago O’Hare’s code, ORD, comes from its old name, Orchard Field."});

;
/* data/arts11.js */
// Arts and Culture: Rembrandt, Fernando Amorsolo, the bahay na bato and stained glass.
ADDART({id:"rembrandt",t:"Rembrandt",c:"Art",i:"🎨",w:"Rembrandt",r:["baroque","davinci","vangogh","impress","photography"],
p:[["Master of light","Rembrandt was famous for dramatic contrasts of light and shadow."],["A famous group portrait","His most famous painting shows a group of city guards."],["Honest self-portraits","He painted dozens of portraits of himself across his life."]],
l:"Rembrandt Harmenszoon van Rijn, known simply as Rembrandt, was a Dutch painter, printmaker and draftsman who lived from 1606 to 1669 and is considered one of the greatest artists in history. Working mostly in Amsterdam during the Dutch Golden Age, he painted portraits, scenes from the Bible and history, and landscapes, and created hundreds of etchings and drawings. He was a master of chiaroscuro, the use of strong contrasts between light and dark, which gives his works drama and emotional depth. His most famous painting, The Night Watch, completed in 1642, shows a company of city militia bursting into action. Rembrandt also painted around 80 self-portraits over four decades, recording his own aging with remarkable honesty. Despite early success, he faced financial ruin later in life.",
s:[["Light and shadow","Rembrandt often lit his subjects with a strong, warm light from one side, leaving the rest in deep shadow. This technique focuses attention on faces and hands and creates a mood of mystery and intimacy. He also built up thick layers of paint, called impasto, to catch light on jewelry, fabric and skin."],["The Night Watch","The Night Watch, officially titled Militia Company of District II under the Command of Captain Frans Banninck Cocq, is a huge group portrait. Instead of lining the guards up stiffly, Rembrandt showed them moving, with a drummer, a girl in golden light and flags. Its dark varnish later made it look like a night scene, giving it its popular name."],["Self-portraits","Rembrandt painted, etched and drew himself many times, from a confident young man to a weathered old man. Some show him in costumes and expressions, possibly as studies of emotion. Together, they form one of the most extraordinary records of a human face in art."],["Etchings","Rembrandt was also a brilliant printmaker. He made etchings by scratching designs into wax on copper plates, then using acid to bite the lines into the metal. Prints could be made many times, spreading his fame across Europe. Works such as the Hundred Guilder Print show his skill with line and shadow."]],
k:[["Full name","Rembrandt Harmenszoon van Rijn"],["Lived","1606–1669"],["Country","Dutch Republic"],["Most famous work","The Night Watch, 1642"],["Signature technique","Chiaroscuro"]],
d:[["1606","Rembrandt is born in Leiden"],["1631","Rembrandt moves to Amsterdam"],["1642","He completes The Night Watch; his wife Saskia dies"],["1656","Rembrandt declares bankruptcy"],["1669","Rembrandt dies in Amsterdam"],["2019","A major public restoration of The Night Watch begins"]],
s2:[["Life and losses","Rembrandt was born in Leiden, the son of a miller, and moved to Amsterdam, where he became a sought-after portrait painter and married Saskia van Uylenburgh. Three of their four children died as infants, and Saskia died in 1642, the year he finished The Night Watch. Only his son Titus survived to adulthood, and Titus died a year before Rembrandt."],["Money troubles","Rembrandt bought an expensive house and collected art, armor, shells and curiosities. As his style became less fashionable and debts grew, he declared bankruptcy in 1656. His house and collection were sold. He continued to paint in a smaller home, producing some of his most powerful late works."],["The Dutch Golden Age","In the 1600s the Dutch Republic became one of the richest nations in Europe through trade, including with Asia through the Dutch East India Company. Wealthy merchants commissioned portraits, landscapes and scenes of daily life, creating a lively art market. Rembrandt, Johannes Vermeer and Frans Hals were among its leading artists."],["Operation Night Watch","In 2019 the Rijksmuseum in Amsterdam began a major research and restoration project on The Night Watch, carried out in a glass room so visitors could watch. Scientists used high-resolution scans, X-rays and artificial intelligence to study the painting and reconstruct parts that had been cut off in 1715 to fit a wall."],["Real or not?","Many paintings once attributed to Rembrandt were later found to be by his students or followers. Since 1968 the Rembrandt Research Project has studied works using scientific methods and expert judgment, sometimes removing paintings from his list and sometimes restoring them. The debates show how difficult it can be to identify an artist’s hand."],["Influence","Rembrandt’s use of light and his deep sympathy for human emotion influenced generations of painters, from Goya to Van Gogh. Film directors and photographers also study his lighting; a common portrait lighting style with a triangle of light on the shadowed cheek is called Rembrandt lighting."]],
sp:["The painting that was cut","For more than 70 years after Rembrandt finished it, The Night Watch hung in a hall of the Amsterdam militia. In 1715 it was moved to the town hall, but it was too large for the wall between two doors. So officials trimmed strips from all four sides, losing two figures on the left and parts of others. For centuries, people knew of the missing parts only from a small copy made by another artist. In 2021 researchers at the Rijksmuseum trained artificial intelligence on Rembrandt’s style and the copy to recreate the missing strips, printed them and hung them beside the painting, letting visitors glimpse the full composition for the first time in about 300 years."],
g:[["Chiaroscuro","Strong contrast between light and dark in art"],["Impasto","Thickly applied paint"],["Etching","A print made from a design bitten into metal by acid"],["Self-portrait","A portrait an artist makes of themselves"]],
f:["Rembrandt painted The Night Watch in 1642.","He made around 80 self-portraits.","He declared bankruptcy in 1656."],
fun:"The Night Watch is not actually a night scene; old dark varnish made it look like one."});

ADDART({id:"amorsolo",t:"Fernando Amorsolo",c:"Art",i:"🖼️",w:"Fernando Amorsolo",r:["phlit","rice","impress","jluna","modernart"],
p:[["First National Artist","Amorsolo was the first person named National Artist of the Philippines."],["Painter of light","He was famous for glowing, backlit scenes of rural life."],["Planting rice","His paintings of farmers and dalagas became iconic images."]],
l:"Fernando Cueto Amorsolo, who lived from 1892 to 1972, was one of the most popular and influential Filipino painters. He became the first person proclaimed a National Artist of the Philippines in 1972, shortly after his death. Amorsolo is best known for sunlit paintings of the Philippine countryside: farmers planting and harvesting rice, women in baro’t saya by streams, and village fiestas, often lit from behind so that figures glow against the light, a technique called backlighting. He also painted portraits, historical scenes and images of the destruction of Manila during World War II. He taught for many years at the University of the Philippines School of Fine Arts. His idealized images of rural life shaped how many Filipinos and foreigners pictured the Philippines.",
s:[["Early life","Amorsolo was born in Paco, Manila, and grew up partly in Daet, Camarines Norte. As a boy, he helped support his family by painting watercolor postcards. He studied at the Liceo de Manila and the University of the Philippines School of Fine Arts, graduating in 1914, and later traveled to Madrid to study the works of masters such as Diego Velázquez."],["Backlighting","Amorsolo’s trademark technique was painting figures against bright sunlight, so that light glowed around their hair and edges, creating a warm, golden atmosphere. He was fascinated by the tropical light of the Philippine morning and late afternoon, and his use of light has been compared to Impressionism."],["Rural scenes","Many of his best-known works show farmers planting rice in flooded fields, women washing clothes in rivers, and families under mango trees. His idealized Filipina, a graceful dalaga with a round face and gentle smile, became an iconic image. These paintings were extremely popular with buyers, including American visitors."],["War paintings","During World War II and the Japanese occupation, Amorsolo stayed in Manila and painted scenes of war, including burning buildings, refugees and the destruction of the city in 1945. These works, darker than his rural scenes, are important records of the war’s impact on Filipinos."]],
k:[["Lived","1892–1972"],["Title","First National Artist of the Philippines"],["Signature technique","Backlighting"],["Famous subjects","Rice farmers, rural women, fiestas"],["Taught at","UP School of Fine Arts"]],
d:[["1892","Fernando Amorsolo is born in Paco, Manila"],["1914","He graduates from the UP School of Fine Arts"],["1919","He travels to Spain to study art"],["1938","He becomes director of the UP School of Fine Arts"],["1945","He paints scenes of the destruction of Manila"],["1972","He dies and is proclaimed the first National Artist"]],
s2:[["The Grand Old Man of Philippine Art","Amorsolo was enormously productive, creating thousands of paintings over his career. He led the UP School of Fine Arts from 1938 to 1952 and influenced generations of Filipino painters. His studio was always busy, and his works appeared in homes, offices, calendars and advertisements across the country."],["Conservatives and modernists","In the 1930s to 1950s, younger artists led by Victorio Edades argued that Philippine art should move beyond Amorsolo’s academic, idealized style toward modern art that showed the harsher realities of life. Supporters of Amorsolo valued his skill, beauty and celebration of Filipino life. The debate between the conservatives and the modernists shaped Philippine art in the 20th century, and both traditions are now respected."],["An idealized countryside","Some critics argue that Amorsolo’s paintings present an idealized, romantic view of rural life that ignores poverty and hardship. Others say his works express pride in Filipino identity, landscape and labor, especially during the American colonial period, and preserve a memory of traditional ways of life. Both views continue to be discussed by art historians."],["National Artist","The Order of National Artists was established in 1972 to honor Filipinos who have made outstanding contributions to the arts. Amorsolo, who died in April of that year, was the first to receive the honor, posthumously. Since then, writers, musicians, dancers, filmmakers and other artists have been named National Artists."],["Amorsolo today","Amorsolo’s paintings are among the most sought-after Philippine artworks at auctions. They are displayed at the National Museum of Fine Arts and other collections. Forgeries of his work have also appeared, so experts examine signatures, techniques and records carefully."],["Family of artists","Amorsolo’s brother Pablo Amorsolo was also a painter, and several of his children and relatives became artists. His uncle, the painter Fabián de la Rosa, was an important early mentor who encouraged his talent."]],
sp:["The light of the morning","Fernando Amorsolo often rose early to watch the sunlight over the fields outside Manila. He noticed how the low morning sun turned the edges of leaves, straw hats and people’s hair into rims of gold, while their faces stayed in soft shadow. Back in his studio, he would recreate this effect, painting a background of bright, hazy light and placing figures in front of it, their outlines glowing. Visitors who saw his paintings said they could almost feel the warmth of the tropical sun. That glowing light became his signature, so recognizable that people still speak of an Amorsolo light when they see the golden morning glow in the Philippine countryside."],
g:[["Backlighting","Lighting a subject from behind"],["National Artist","The highest honor for Filipino artists"],["Dalaga","A young unmarried woman"],["Modernism","An art movement breaking from traditional styles"]],
f:["Amorsolo was the first National Artist of the Philippines.","He was famous for backlighting.","He painted scenes of Manila during World War II."],
fun:"As a boy, Amorsolo helped support his family by painting watercolor postcards."});

ADDART({id:"bahaynabato",t:"The Bahay na Bato",c:"Architecture",i:"🏠",w:"Bahay na bato",r:["architecture","phfolk","quake","spanph","phtyph"],
p:[["House of stone","The bahay na bato is a traditional Filipino house of stone and wood."],["Built for the tropics","Its design suits heat, rain, typhoons and earthquakes."],["Heritage towns","Vigan has one of the best-preserved collections of these houses."]],
l:"The bahay na bato, meaning house of stone, is a style of house that developed in the Philippines during the Spanish colonial period, especially from the 18th to the 19th centuries. It combines the traditional Filipino bahay kubo, a raised house of wood and bamboo, with Spanish and Chinese building influences. A typical bahay na bato has a ground floor with thick stone or brick walls, used for storage and carriages, and an upper floor of wood where the family lived, with large windows of sliding panels set with thin translucent capiz shells. Its design suited the tropical climate, allowing breezes to flow through, and its wooden upper floor helped it withstand earthquakes. Many survive in heritage towns such as Vigan, Taal and Silay.",
s:[["Design","The stone lower floor, called the silong or zaguan, kept out floodwater and supported the house, while the upper floor, with wooden posts and walls, was lighter and more flexible in earthquakes. A wide staircase led up to a large living room called the sala, with bedrooms, a dining room and a kitchen. An azotea, an open terrace, was often used for washing and drying."],["Windows","Large sliding windows ran along the upper walls, with panels made of capiz shells set in wooden grids, which let in soft light while blocking glare. Below them, small windows called ventanillas with wooden or iron grilles allowed air to circulate even when the main windows were closed during rain."],["Roofs","Early roofs were made of thatch, which were later replaced with clay tiles and then galvanized iron. Steep roofs with wide overhangs protected walls from heavy rain and shaded the windows from the sun."],["From the bahay kubo","The bahay na bato kept key features of the traditional bahay kubo: being raised above the ground, having many windows for ventilation, and using local materials. Builders adapted these ideas to wealthier families’ needs and to colonial building rules, especially after fires and earthquakes destroyed many wooden houses."]],
k:[["Meaning","House of stone"],["Period","Spanish colonial era"],["Lower floor","Stone or brick"],["Upper floor","Wood with capiz windows"],["Famous town","Vigan, UNESCO World Heritage Site"]],
d:[["1600s","Stone houses begin to be built in Manila"],["1700s–1800s","The bahay na bato style spreads across the islands"],["1863","A major earthquake damages stone buildings in Manila"],["1945","The Battle of Manila destroys much of Intramuros"],["1999","Vigan is inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2009","The National Cultural Heritage Act is passed"]],
s2:[["Vigan","The city of Vigan in Ilocos Sur has one of the best-preserved Spanish colonial towns in Asia, with streets of bahay na bato houses along Calle Crisologo. Vigan was a trading port where Chinese, Filipino and Spanish influences mixed, visible in its architecture. It was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1999 and named one of the New7Wonders Cities in 2014."],["Other heritage towns","Taal in Batangas, Silay in Negros Occidental, Malolos in Bulacan, San Miguel in Bulacan and Carcar in Cebu also have notable ancestral houses. Many are still lived in by descendants of the original families, while others have become museums, restaurants or inns."],["Living with earthquakes","After strong earthquakes in Manila in the 1600s and 1800s destroyed many stone buildings, builders learned to make the upper floors of wood, which could sway, while keeping stone only on the lower floor. Some historians call this style earthquake baroque when applied to churches, which have thick walls and buttresses."],["Threats to old houses","Many ancestral houses have been demolished for new buildings, damaged by neglect, fires, typhoons and termites, or sold piece by piece. Some were moved to a private heritage resort in Bataan, a project that supporters say saved houses from destruction, while critics argue that removing houses from their original towns harms local heritage."],["Protecting heritage","The National Cultural Heritage Act of 2009 provides for the protection of important cultural properties, including historic houses. Local governments, heritage groups and owners work to restore houses using traditional materials and techniques. Tourism can provide income for conservation, but it also needs careful management."],["Casa Manila","Casa Manila in Intramuros is a reconstruction of a 19th-century bahay na bato, built in the 1980s on the site of houses destroyed during World War II. Its rooms are furnished with period furniture, showing visitors how wealthy families lived during the Spanish colonial era."]],
sp:["Evening in an old Vigan house","At dusk in Vigan, the stone streets cool after a hot day. In an old bahay na bato on Calle Crisologo, a family opens the capiz windows of the upstairs sala to let the evening breeze flow through the house. Light from inside glows softly through the shell panels. Downstairs, the thick stone walls of the old storeroom keep the air cool, and a kalesa, a horse-drawn carriage, clatters past outside. The great-grandparents of the family built the house more than a century ago, and the wide wooden floorboards, polished by generations of feet, creak gently. Tourists passing by pause to take photos of the house, a living piece of Philippine history."],
g:[["Bahay na bato","A Filipino colonial house of stone and wood"],["Capiz","A thin translucent shell used in windows"],["Sala","The main living room"],["Ventanilla","A small window below the main windows"]],
f:["The bahay na bato combines Filipino, Spanish and Chinese influences.","Its windows use capiz shells.","Vigan is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],
fun:"Capiz windows let in soft light like frosted glass, but are made from seashells."});

ADDART({id:"stainedglass",t:"Stained Glass",c:"Art",i:"⛪",w:"Stained glass",r:["gothic","baroque","architecture","christ","islamicart"],
p:[["Windows of color","Stained glass windows are made of colored glass pieces."],["Medieval cathedrals","Gothic cathedrals filled their walls with glowing windows."],["Telling stories","Windows often showed stories from the Bible and lives of saints."]],
l:"Stained glass is colored glass cut into shapes and arranged to form pictures or patterns, usually held together by strips of lead and set into windows. When light shines through, the colors glow brilliantly. The art reached its height in the Gothic cathedrals of medieval Europe, such as Chartres and the Sainte-Chapelle in Paris, where vast windows filled walls with scenes from the Bible and the lives of saints, teaching stories to people who could not read. Stained glass is also found in mosques, palaces, homes and modern buildings, and artists such as Louis Comfort Tiffany and Marc Chagall created famous works. In the Philippines, churches such as San Sebastian Basilica and Manila Cathedral have notable stained glass windows.",
s:[["Making colored glass","Glassmakers color glass by adding metal compounds to molten glass: cobalt for blue, copper for green or red, gold for ruby red, and manganese for purple. Painters added details such as faces and folds of clothing with dark enamel paint fired onto the glass, and a silver stain introduced in the 1300s gave yellow and gold tones."],["Building a window","An artist first draws a full-size design called a cartoon. Glass is cut to match each shape and joined with grooved strips of lead called cames, soldered at the joints. The finished panels are waterproofed and set into iron frames in the window. Large windows can contain thousands of pieces."],["Gothic cathedrals","Gothic architecture, developed in France from the 1100s, used pointed arches, ribbed vaults and flying buttresses to support high walls with less stone, leaving room for huge windows. Round rose windows, like those at Notre-Dame de Paris and Chartres, became masterpieces of stained glass."],["Light and meaning","For medieval Christians, light coming through colored windows symbolized divine light. Windows told stories in pictures, showing Bible scenes, saints, kings and guild workers who paid for them. The colors changed throughout the day as the sun moved."]],
k:[["Material","Colored glass"],["Joined with","Lead cames"],["Golden age","Gothic period, 1100s–1400s"],["Famous examples","Chartres, Sainte-Chapelle"],["Philippine example","San Sebastian Basilica, Manila"]],
d:[["c. 600s","Colored glass windows are used in churches in Europe"],["1100s","Gothic architecture begins in France"],["1200s","The windows of Chartres Cathedral are completed"],["1248","The Sainte-Chapelle in Paris is consecrated"],["1890s","San Sebastian Church in Manila is completed with German stained glass"],["2019","Fire damages Notre-Dame de Paris, though most of its rose windows survive"]],
s2:[["Sainte-Chapelle","The Sainte-Chapelle in Paris, completed in 1248 for King Louis IX, has walls made almost entirely of stained glass, with 15 tall windows showing over a thousand scenes from the Bible. On a sunny day, the upper chapel glows with deep blues and reds. Much of the glass is original, though parts were restored in the 1800s."],["San Sebastian Basilica","San Sebastian Basilica in Manila, completed in 1891, is a Gothic Revival church made of prefabricated steel parts shipped from Belgium. Its stained glass windows were made by the Heinrich Oidtmann company in Germany. It is one of the few all-steel churches in the world and a National Historical Landmark. Restoration work has been needed to repair rust and damage."],["Tiffany glass","In the late 1800s and early 1900s, the American artist Louis Comfort Tiffany developed new kinds of opalescent glass with swirls and textures, creating windows and lamps with natural scenes of flowers, landscapes and birds. Tiffany lamps became famous and are widely imitated."],["Modern stained glass","In the 20th century, artists such as Marc Chagall, Henri Matisse and Gerhard Richter designed stained glass windows for churches, synagogues and public buildings, using bold colors and abstract designs. Modern techniques include fused glass and glass set in concrete."],["Beyond churches","Stained glass decorates mosques, where colored windows filled with geometric patterns cast patterns of light, as in the Nasir al-Mulk Mosque in Shiraz, Iran, known as the Pink Mosque. It is also used in homes, libraries, railway stations and government buildings."],["Saving old windows","Old stained glass windows can be damaged by pollution, weather, war and fire. During World War II, many European cathedrals removed their windows and hid them for safekeeping. In 2019 a fire at Notre-Dame de Paris destroyed its roof and spire, but its three famous rose windows survived. Specialists carefully clean and restore windows using traditional methods."]],
sp:["Light in the Pink Mosque","Early on a sunny morning in Shiraz, Iran, visitors gather inside the Nasir al-Mulk Mosque, built in the 1880s. As the sun rises, it shines through a wall of tall stained glass windows in red, yellow, blue and green. Beams of colored light spill across the Persian carpets and the patterned walls and columns, creating a kaleidoscope of color that shifts as the sun climbs. Photographers wait for the perfect moment, and worshippers and tourists sit quietly to watch the light. The mosque, popularly known as the Pink Mosque for the pink tiles of its decoration, shows how stained glass has created spaces of beauty and contemplation in many faiths and cultures."],
g:[["Stained glass","Colored glass arranged to make pictures"],["Came","A lead strip that holds glass pieces"],["Rose window","A large round window with radiating patterns"],["Cartoon","A full-size design drawing for a window"]],
f:["Stained glass flourished in Gothic cathedrals.","Lead strips hold the glass pieces together.","San Sebastian Basilica in Manila has German stained glass."],
fun:"Some medieval red glass got its color from tiny particles of gold."});

